      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>LADIES' LETTER.</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>MY DEAREST NELLIE, At the moment quiet colours and a rather subdued tone prevail in the world of fashion, and although the winter festivities are in full swing before Lent, white, black, and what are known as half-mourning tints are the rule for evening; this is presumably in deference to Court mourning. To my mind, there is a great deal of affection about this donning of "Court" mourwing by people who never had the remotest connection with the Court in any way. I quite approve of the custom of wearing slight mourning on ordinary occasions. At a time like the present it is an outward and visible sign of respect and sympathy to the Queen and her family ; but to talk of " complimentary " mourning in connection with ball dresses is snobbish and heartless on the face of it. People who mourned, even in quite a " com- plimentary" degree, wowld not go to balls at all if their conscience would not permit them to abandon the trappings and suits of woe. Hats for the coining season will be of straw of the brightest coloup. For early spring, a brilliant green will be a favourite hue, and the trimmings will be garlands and groups of flowers, with tulle and wing feathers. There will be a further increase of size in the round-brimmed hats, and primulas of the largest and most oddly-coloured kinds will be favourite flowers.</p>
        <p>A sm, ~, ant. rather novel shaped bonnet for a married lady is one with a turned-up brim forming three points—one in the centre of the front and one at each side. It is lined under with velvet, show- ing an edging of straw all round the top. A twist of ribbon under the brim is caught on the left side with a paste ornament, and finished in a loop and end. The outside trimmings consist of full bows of narrow satin ribbon with an osprey in front, and ostrich tips at the back, one at each ear. The strings are of velvet ribbon.</p>
        <p>Everybody who condescends to talk about fashion at all takes the small sleeve for her text, but of this I have told you now some weeks, so that its advent into popularity should not surprise you at all. lam wondering, though, whether the small sleeve will really take a firm hold on our affections at once. It seems so impossible that the individual taste of womanhood—and I suppose it is only fair to presume that they have some individual taste—should be swayed immediately by a mere word of the authorities; that to•day the balloon- like arm has irresistible charms, and to-morrow— hey! presto !—the conjuror has spoken, and, as if by magic, the voluminous folds have disappeared; nature is allowed to have her say again, and the contour of the arm feminine is permitted to express itself.</p>
        <p>A smart little cog,t which will avoid the severity of a simply planned tailor-made gown, is close-fitting, yet open with a vest of white kerseymere braided in gold and adorned by mess buttons to reveal a blouse or it might have been a vest only in pale blue silk. The coat collar was faced back by dark violet velvet, and handsome buttons held down the tabs on these cape-like epaulettes of the cloth. For a black satin skirt for ordinary home even- ing wear, two excellent suggestions may be given. The one is a trimming of that black lisse with a white lace applique; the other is to make the silk into a simple bodice, provided it with a fichu also of the silk frilled with a kilted chiffon, passing through a belt at the waist made of black satin buttoned with diamonds, the ends of the fichu to fall loosely on either side. The sleeves of this are to be slightly fully at the top, where they should be lined, but guileless of lining down to the wrist, whither they should wrinkle their way tightly.</p>
        <p>A neautiful tea-gown is made of cdpon (in any colour you may buy the model, black or cream or pink or blue), and the yoke and epaulettes are of white silk, striped with lace insertion, and edged with lace frills. It is an ideal gown in which to come down to breakfast, while for an afternoon tea you could desire nothing more attractive. Another gown is made of an English satin, with a white ground, upon which pink flowers daintily disport themselves. This is lined throughout with nuns' veiling, the most comfortable lining in the world let me mention in parenthesis, and trimmed with superior lace frills; ' green ribbons are tied round the waist, and the back sets in a Watteau pleat. your loving JENNIE.</p>
