      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>EXAMINEE, SATURDAY FEBRUARY 29,</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINEE, SATURDAY FEBRUARY 29,</p>
        <p>able except in a circus ring. But how wonderfully well most of the women look ! A badly built, baggy skirt, ballooning out at either side is now so rare as to occasion stares and comments which, of ceurse, speaks volumes for the splendid energy of satorial pioneers. The Panama sailor-shaped hat with a high crown is the very newest headgear for cycling. It is simply trimmed with a plain band of ribbon and is severe enough to suit the most severely athletic. By the way, all cycling hats should be provided with elastic. No pins, be they ever so long or so firm, are proof against wind and rapid movement, and it is not an edifying spectacle to see an unconscious rider careering along with her hat on an angle of forty-five degrees. Miss Minnie Cochrane, one of the Maids of Honour to the Queen, is an accomplished cyclist. When in the Isle ot Wight her only companion in these expeditions is a ginger-coloured Pomeranian,one of Her Majesty's breed. When not in waiting, Miss Cochrane resides with her mother, Lady Cochrane, at Quarr Abbey, Binstead, about half-way between Ryole and Osborne. _ _</p>
        <p>Last year,the lion of the season was the Shahzada,this year it seems we are to receive a visit from the Srltan of Morocco. He is only sixteen years of age, but like all other Orientals be looks considerably older than he really I is. But we are to have another distinguished visitor shortly, something much more uncommon than an I Oriental Prince, viz, a real live clergywoman. She re- joices in the name of the Rev. Augusta Chapin, Dl)., I and in the distinction of being the only woman Doctor of Divinity. The distinguished lady has just resigned the pastorate of her church in Omaha,Nebraska. 'I he reason is not stated in the paragraph which heralds her advent amongst us. A number of our comedians have lately- entered the lists as authors ; amongst them being Mr. Toole, Mr. Arthur Roberts, and Mr. R. G. Knowles, who have des- cribed their droll adventures in small volumes of anec- dotal reminiscence. Mr. Albert Chevalier, once more, alas, deserting us for America, has also written a small book entitled " The Uninitiated," in wbich he chats upon life, and people, and his experiences of them in humorous style. Mr. Chevalier's causerie is to be issued very shortly with special illustrations. Two long promised books seem really now within prospect of pub- lication. Renan's Correspondence and Memoirs will be out during the second week in March, and Miss Mary Anderson's Autobiography is timed for about the same date. The new instalment of Herbert Spencer's colossal work "Social Institutions "cannot be anticipated much ' before mid-summer. The veteran novelist, Mrs. Lynn Linton, who will shortly claim the honour due to a septuagenarian, has, at the present time, a new novel in the press. Dogs form quite an absorbing topic just now, and the somewhat ludicrous sight of a slal wart policeman lead- ing those animals whose owners have failed to provide them with the necessary muzzle, off to the police station, is familiar to most of us. Apropos, the other day I witnessed the arrest of a small dog who was apparently destitute of any owner and unable to give its name and address, owing to the carelessness of its proprietor in not providing it with a collar, bearing an inscription giving these details. The animal had on a silk coat trimmed with fur, which was fastened with a diamond brooch, and in a small pocket of the coat there was a miniature pocket handkerchief. The very latest idea with regard to the toilet of the up-to-date dog is, to provide him with shoes! Not the unsightly leather arrangements which absorbed most of the animal's attention when out walking and which caused him much discomfort, but a foot covering of thin and flexible rubber that fits closely to the paw, and is supposed to cause him no annoyance after bis first sur- ' prise has been overcome. Now, many of us know from painful experience, 'what unsightly tracks a dog with muddy feet will make when he bounds over a light colored carpet on his return from a promenade in the damp streets, and doubtless this novel arrangement does away with this particular disaster, but .vhat with shoes and the infliction of the muzzle,one cannot help thinking the poor animals would prefer to dispense with the daily constitutional altogether. .</p>
        <p>Lockets are to be worn again, and the newest being faf,hinned like unto coins have the merit of not arousing curiou.ity, always a drawback to the ordinary locket. This little coin dangles innocently from the bangle or neck chain, and although the wearer knows that there is a spring, and inside there is a miniature, she knows also that it is so well protected that no questions, which per- chance might not be welcome, are not likely to be asked.</p>
        <p>_ No well-dressed head looks complete without some crnament. For evening the little high-standing aigrettes of flowers are becoming, and infinitely more so than the "flue brush "so many fashionable women are just now indulging in. A bow of narrow velvet ribbon with the loops and ends wired to stand upright looks smart.spe- cially if the velvet be edged with the narrowest Valen- ciennes lace and studded with bright spangles. The vel- vet should either match the gown in colour, or be of black studded with silver sequins. Waist buckles, for afternoon wear are in high favour. Some resemble serpents with jewelled heads,while others have the monogram, each letter being in a different shade of gold Old-fashioned buckles of the beginning of I the century are greatly sought for and are imitated in gold set with turquoises or aqua-marines. Veils which hear the hall-mark of fashion are in black Russian net with white chenille spots. A few women are indulging in white net with black chenille spots,but the effect of this fabric on the complexion is anything but fascinating. The real lace veils which made their appearance again last winter have now disappeared al- together. This is an item of news we cannot be too grateful for, as it is difficult to imagine anything more disfiguring than these particular veils. Dr. Sarah Anderson Brown, when lecturing recently on behalf of Lady Henry Somerset's " Inebriate Home" 1 is reported to have said that " England is a nation of women drunkards,"—Now I do not think that Dr. S. Anderson Brown has any excuse for so unjustifiable a libel as thin. If she had said we are a nation of tea drunkards, no one would have attempted to dispute her statement, aa she would doubtless have been nearer this</p>
        <p>less offensive: truth.—As it is, the mildest view we can take of her remarks is, that all persons engaged in these so-called " Temperance Crusades" are apt to take an exaggerated view of the evils they are fighting.—lt is true that a very large proportion of the women of the lowest classes are frighful drunkards, and as will be admitted by anyone who pays. sav,a visit to the casualty room of any of ourlave hospitals at midnight on Satur- days and Sundays, a considerable number of women be- longing to the coster class drink very heavily, but star- vation, had health, and insanitary lives are in a large measure responsible for this. Most of us can happily boast a social circle in which the female inebriate is entirely unknown and there cannot be any doubt, des- pite the peculiar remarks of Dr. Sarah Anderson Brown, that the great body of upper and middle class women, if servants, and respectable working men's wives, are habitually sober. _ _</p>
        <p>Many are the prepsrationa put before us with which to clean marble. I have tried many of tbem, and found none equal to Common Salt. This should be slattered on the soiled surface which should then be scrubbed, when the marble will then appear beautifully clean. POTATO PUFFS are delicious.—Take about three quar- ters of a pound of mashed potatoes, stir in two tables- spoonful of milk and two ounces of butter and two well beaten eggs. Beat the whole mixture till very light, season it with pepper and salt, and a little mace. Pour into a buttered pie dish and hake in a quick oven. GERMAN SPONGE CAKE.—Mix a quarter of a pound of castor sugar with the yolks of five eggs for twenty minutes, stirring always the same way. Beat the whites of the eggs to a stiff froth and stir them in lightly with the rest, then add a quarter of a pound of flour with teaspoonful of baking powder. Bake for abcut an hour in a moderate oven.—" Madge."</p>
        <p>The product of the wax tree of the Andes cannot be distinguished from beeswax. In ancient times bones were collected from the batt fields, ground to powd,r, and used to fertilise the land. A prize Of thirty thousand francs has been offered by a florist in Mayenne tc anyone who can produce a plant on which blue roses will bloom. Caged lions and tigers, pumas and jaguars, take no notice of the men and women passing them, but if a dog be brought anywhere near the cage they show their savage nature at once. The finest shaving brushes are manufactured from badgers' hair. and " camels' hair" brushes are turned out in reat quantities from squirrels' tales and from the hair that crows inside the ears of oxen. Lord Brassey has scandalised Melbourne snciey. The first time he occupied the vice-regal box at the theatre, he noticed, sitting in the gallery, several sailors from his yacht, and, terrible to relate," nodded familiarly to them.</p>
        <p>Princess Christian has been so shollvad at the bad manners displayed by the vives of foreign ministerq, nf newly created peers and baronets, and of civic digni- taries attending a drawing-room or "commanded "to Windsor for the Brat time, that it is said she hag under- taken to write a book on etiquette "for ladies who attend royal and imperial courts."</p>
        <p>not very attractive at first sight to many minds. It con- sists in driving the blood vessels into firm contraction, after which secondary dilation follows. The feet should be dipped in cold water for a brief period ; and then, immediately before getting into bed, they should be rubbed till they glow, with a p ir of hair flesh gloves or a rough Turkish towel. After this a hot water bottle will be successful enough in maintaining the temp.ra- ture of the feet, though, without this preliminary, it is impotent to use one.</p>
        <p>Coffee and How to Make It.</p>
        <p>Chemically speaking, coffee consists of a volatile aromatic oil, to which its flavour is owing; the stimulant caffeite ; and the astringent acid, tannin, to which any injurious effect is due. Good coffee depends on the manner of roasting, grinding, and making into a beverage. It should be as freshly roasted as practicable, since the aroma gradually disappears from the roasted product. It should be ground as fine as possible, as, in this way,the hot water has a more penetrating effect and le!,s coffee is required. The correct method of making coffee as a beverage is by quick infusion ; prepared by the ordinary method it is little more than a solution of tannin, the arcmatic oil having been dissipated by the continued heat. Dr. Willis Barnessuggests the following method of preparation :—Make into a thick paste with cold water ; then, five minutes before using, pour on a cup of boiling water for each person, put the pot on the five and let tho infusion come to a boil, settliw.,, with eggshells or a dash of cold water. Such a preparation is perfectly healthful, but should not be imbibed to the extent of more than one capful at each meal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>Cure for Cold Feet.</head>
        <p>Cure for Cold Feet.</p>
        <p>The association between cold feet and sleenlessness is much closer than is commonly imagined. Persons with cold feet rarely sleep well. especially women. Yet the number of persona so troubled is very considerable. We now know that, if the blood supply of the brain be kept up. sleep is impossible. An old theologian. when weary and sleepy with much writing, found that he could keep his brain active by immersing his feet in cold water; the cold drove the blood from the feet to the head. Now, what this gentlrman accomplished by design, is secured for many per,ons much against. their will. Cold feet are the bane of many people. Tight boots keep up a bloodless condition of the feet in the day, and there Is no sutsequent dilation of the blood vessels when the boots are taken off. Placing the feet to the fire and a hot bottle in bed, is all of no um The effective .plan is</p>
