      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>WHAT A. WEAK BOY DID.</head>
        <p>WHAT A. WEAK BOY DID.</p>
        <p>Consumption is too often traced to weakness in child- nood. When from any cause the child is unable to aTiimilate nourishment necessary to make a healthy growth, there is almost at once impoverishment of the blood. This condition is dangerous, not only to growth, but to life itself. No child can hope to have solid bones, fat rosy cheeks, and vital strength. if its food supply is cut off from the blood.</p>
        <p>The son of W. D. Ray,Heacl Master of Plaxtol National Schools, Sevenoaks, Kent, at four years old showed a weakness of the chest. What his condition was, and what his physician recommended is told by Mr. Ray,the Head Master, in the following letter,dated January 15th, 1896 :</p>
        <p>" I take pleasure in acquainting you of the great benefit Scott's Eatulaion has given to my little boy. He is four years old, and although a good big boy, has at times shown a weakness of chest, which we have been afraid would develop into serious lung trouble. For the last two winters he bas had severe attacks of congestion of the lungs, and has been,' up to the last two months, peculiarly succeptibla to catching cold. In October, 1895, be had another grave illness—conges- tion and bronchitis—and he was reduced in strength and appearance to a very low ebb. Our doctior ordered him to take Sc.tt's Emulsion. He has done so for the last two months, and the benefit to him is remarkable. His appetite, which before was capricious, is now good and steady, and he has increased in weight to a considerablg extent. By the aid of Scott's Emulsion, solely and entirely, he appears to be rapidly getting into a sound, healthy state. I have every faith that by its continued use he will soon outgrow that weakness of the lungs which had shown itself in him. I have begun to take it myself as a tonic, and have good reason to hope that it will prove ot equal service to me.</p>
        <p>" You are at liberty to publish this, and I shall be pleased to answer any question respecting the case, either from you or from anyone to whom you might quote me as a reference."</p>
        <p>Scott's Emulsion certainly has a wide range of useful- ness. Food is designed to supply the body with every- thing needed by Nature; but Scott's Emulsion has the advantage over food of acting directly upon tfie throat and lungs, healing sore places and re:ieving all local irritation, and at the same time feeding the blood with the most essential nourishing properties of food. It is easily assimilated, and this enables the patient to gain flesh and strength, even though weakness may have im- paired the digestive organs. For all who are pale, and thin, and weak, Scott's Emulsion is almost a necessity. Its value is attested by the high esteem in which it is held by the medical pro- session, which recommends it in nearly all cases where there is decreased vitality. It is peculiar to itself, and cannot be imitated. The genuine Scott's Emulsion is easily distinguished by the trade-mark of a man carry- ing a big fish on his back, on the wrapper.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>" APOSTOLIC BLOWS AND KNOCKS."</head>
        <p>" APOSTOLIC BLOWS AND KNOCKS."</p>
        <p>The pastor of a peculiar sect, established by a woman some years since in a Western State of America, had re- cently the idea of demanding that everyone of his adult parishioners, 200 in number, should fill in replies to a set of printed questions as to their exact age and the number of times they have been married. This was about three months ago. and (says the " Daily News ") all but twenty ot his flock have meekly obeyed their spiritual guide and filled in answers. Those who re- fused to do so were forbidden to enter the church. One of the recalcitrants, a lady, on attempting to go into the building, was dealt a violent blow on the nose by the Dastor'a wife, a sort of argument not unknown in the annals of religion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>JAPANESE COMPETITION WITH GREAT BRITAIN:</head>
        <p>JAPANESE COMPETITION WITH GREAT BRITAIN:</p>
        <p>Great efforts are to be made by Japan to wrest from Great Britain a share of the carrying trade of the world. The line to Bombay is (says a " Daily News "correspon- dent) already arranged, and the Peninsular and Ori,ntal Company must expect severe competition. The line to Australia is not yet opened ,but in March the Tosa-Maru a fine steamer of 5.000 tons,leaves for Europe. Captain DEl`Millan has left for England to place orders for Si vessels of similar capacity to the Tosa.-Maru, and when finished these vessels will be placed on the European line. They will be twin-screw steamers, and be fi,ted with three masts. Their passenger accommodation will be limited to a dozen berths, but they will be great cargo carriers, and are to have a speed of 15 knots.</p>
        <p>HOOD'S pILLS are EASY TO TAKE For Nlliongness, Sick Headache and all liver troubles. Price 1. ltd.. at chemists or by post of C. I. Hood &amp; Co., 34, Snow Hill, London, H C. (No. 2).</p>
      </div>
