      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>PRINCE BISMA RCK AND "MIGHT BEFORE RIGHT."</head>
        <p>PRINCE BISMA RCK AND "MIGHT BEFORE RIGHT."</p>
        <p>--- Prince Bismarck, in the course of his recent interviel, with the Halle deputation, incidentally referred to tb, saying, erroneously attributed to him, "Might bef°" right." He assured his hearers that he had never er pressed such an opinion in so uncompromising a forrii; but had merely remarked that in politics men havill: power must use it in accordance with the prover'; " 134ati po3sidentes." He went on (says the" Standard e correspondent) to criticise the Generale who led rPtl armies in 1866 and 1870, and warmly extolled the merlhe of General von Goeben, of whom he said that, if army had lost Moltke, he would have liked to see hira the head, "for the soldiers went through fire for hill!, Speaking of the war with France, he said," War is sy'll When a soldier is cold, he has to warm himself, and ha finds no wood for fuel, and uses mahogany pianev instead; it is all the same. •Noboxly can expect a PrOi sian Grenadier to let himself Ire( ze to death and call Of ta the French with his dying breath, 'Here is Ylq mahogany furniture untouched!' And as to the 0100 —well, in many quarters there were none, and in °Woe three or four. It is the duty of the soldier always to ed at his post in time, and it may perhaps have haPPerher once that a clock was carried from one place to ano',";,4 —but taken away! I think the Prussian foot solulci,' was in no harry to carry about with him in his knaPsa a bronze clock several pounds in weight, and worth hundred marks at most."</p>
      </div>
