      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>TII tt; 1-',X AM I N EU., SATURDAY, FEBRUARY 29, 1896.</head>
        <p>TII tt; 1-',X AM I N EU., SATURDAY, FEBRUARY 29, 1896.</p>
        <p>WA RRINGT( )N Fl RMARY AND</p>
        <p>MANCHESTER SHIP CANAL COMPANY.</p>
        <p>HALF-YEARLY REPORT. The half-yearly report of this company, which in view of the financial position of the company has been antici- pated with an exceptional amount of interest, states The expenditure on capital, account during the half- year, after deducting proceeds of sales of plant, was £152,894, making a total expenditure out of capital of £15.101,446, and leaving a balance on that acccount of £305,553. Receipts on Ship Canal revenue account Expenditure, including £12,004 for maintenance 15,117 25,057</p>
        <p>Leaving a balance of — , Profits of the Bridgewater Undertaking were</p>
        <p>Total balance of</p>
        <p>40.174</p>
        <p>THE FOLLOWING ARE THE DETAILS OF THE NET REVENUE ACCOUNT (NO. 10). At the commencement of the half-year the credit balance Carried forward amonnted to Profits for the half-year 40,174 Balance of general interest account burplus on realisation of cashinvested 14,400 - 81,428</p>
        <p>Total After charging interest due on Mortgage Deben- tures for the half-year, viz 160,640</p>
        <p>There remains a debit balance of This debit balance of £79,212 on net revenue account is the balance after debiting to the account £168,750,the amount of the interest accrued due on December 31, but not paid to the Manchester Corporation. The gross receipts from the Ship Canal undertaking amounted to £74,437, an increase, as compared with the correspond- ing six months of the previous year, of £20,844. The net revenue was £15,117, after charging £12,004 for maintenance, against £10,998 for the same period of the year 1894,when no maintenance was charged to revenue. The weight of merchandise paying tolls which passed over the Ship Canal during the two periods was as follows : Six months ending Dec., 1895. Dec., 1894. Tons. Tons. Sea-going 606,659 425 052 Barge 152,116 130,643</p>
        <p>Total 758,775 555,692 The efforts to secure traffic are being prosecuted vigorously in every quarter, and will bring about a large increase when the traders of th district realise not only that considerable savings can be effected now by mak- ing full use of the Ship Canal, but that as a matter of policy they would do well to support the canal even when the older rival routes are for the time being offering equal advantages. A great impetus will also be given when the railways have to put the port of Manchester on the same footing as other ports in regard to the mileage rates of carriage, which the railway companies so far refuse to do. The net revenue from the Brideewater Undertaking was £25,057. as compared with £25,871 for the same period of 1894. The traffic returns up to this date of the current year are satisfactory. The directors have deposited a Bill in Parliament in tbe present session in order to obtain powers to fill up the tidal openings in the embankments of the Ship Canal inconsequence of the inconvenience and expense caus2d by the influx of silt and ice, and the navigation being rendered difficult by the currents through the openings-; to construct a small piece of railway for the purpose of connecting docks Nos. 4 and 5 and for various other purposes. The following directors retire by rotation, and will be proposed for re-election :—Sir Joseph Leigh. Mr. Alexan- der Henderson,Mr. S. R. Platt,and Mr. H. C. Pingstone. Mr. Edwin Guthrie, one of the auditors, also retires by rotation, and will be proposed for re-election. The following is the engineer's report as to works : " Dredging operations have been continued without in- termission during the half-year. The balance of the de- posits of silt which had accumulated in the tidal portion of the canal during construction has been removed. Large quantities of silt have passed into the canal over the sills of the tidal openings, the removal of which was kept, and is keeping, the dredgers in that section con- stantly employed. The work for the permanent electric lighting at the docks are well advanced towards com- pletion, and are now in partial operation. The new hydraulic installation at Mode Wheel is also in a forward state, the engines being in course of erection. The warehouses which are being erected by the Manchester Ship Canal Warehousing Company, Limited, are so far advanced that some of the floors of five of the ware- houses are being used for traffic purposes, although the buildings are not completed. The sidings for the accom- modation of traffic at the North-Western junction at Weaste have been connected witb the new line of the London and North-Western Railway, and the junction is being used for the conveyance of traffic to and from trio docks. The works at Butchersfield and Woolston for the more ready passage of the waters of the river Mersey from the canal through Warrington have been completed and are in operation. The new deep-water jetties and coal tip at Runcorn are nearly complete,and will shortly be in use. Various small works required for the equip- ment of the canal and docks Kaye been completed, and others are in progress. Considerable additions have been made to the protective work of the canal elopes, and the slopes generally are in good condition. The works throughout the canal have been efficiently maintained and are in good order."</p>
        <p>The ordinary half-yearly meeting of the shareholders of the Ship Canal Company was held on Wednesdav,Mr. J. K. Bythell in the chair. In the course of an explana- tory address the Chairman said the prospects of the undertaking for the present half-year were very much more cheerful than was the case twelve months ago, because the navigation was closed for a long period in 1895. They were pretty sure now that there would be no such catastrophe this year. He gave statistics in detail with regard to the financial position of the Com- pany, and said that on the 31st December the interest due to the Manchester Corporation was nine months in arrear. The Directors saw their way to make various heavy payments which he described, including interest on the first and second debentures; but in the autumn of this year, he said, the situation will have to be care- fully reviewed, so that there may be ample time to con- sider whether any further powers should be obtained in the 1897 session of Parliameiat, in order to provide the further equipment which, after this year, will undoubt- edly be trom time to time required, as the trade of the port continues to increase, if the traffic is to be dealt with to the satisfaction of traders and shipowners. Turning from finance to traffic questions, the Chairman saidlthe Company had only got a tithe of what they would have if all the obstacles could be removed. He regretted to have to say that the action of the railway companies was a most serious hindrance to the port of Manchester. All the companies were acting together on the question of rates, and the Canal Company were con- pelled now to go to the Railway Commissioners. At the conclusion of the Chairman's speech several shareholders offered some observations, and the motion for the adop- tion of the report was passed unanimously. The meet- ing also sanctioned the Company's new bill, the main obj‘•ct of which is to close the tidal openings into the canal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>DISPENSARY.</head>
        <p>DISPENSARY.</p>
        <p>The present Duchess of Sutherland owes her poF.;i- tion to the somewhat stupid superstition that, ANNUAL MEETING OF THE WORKINGMEN'S thirteen is an unluekly number. At a dinner given • COMMITTEE. by her mother, Lady Itosslyn, it was discovered, The annual meeting of the repr;sentatives of the vari- with no little alarm,that there were thirteen guests ous works in the town and neighbourhood was held at the table. To exorcise the fatality of the dreaded on Saturday afternoon in St. Paul's :Schoolroom, grEc:rprtensaenntuastlilvaels,,naaaniodiy. the number, Lady Millicent, a school-girl of sixteen and Kendr;cll-st., the Mayor in the chair. Thera was not yet " presented," was sent for to join the party. g s attendanceia s teh The now Duke of Sutherland was among those P rocTeln °lCOn hour and a half. Mr. E. M. Crosfield (hon. secretary) present (he being then the Marquis of Stafford), find was unable to be present, and Mr. Joseph Taylor, assist- as soon almost as the young laoy entered the room ant secretary, officiated in his stead. Letters of apology he fell in love with her, seen proposed, and was am- for non-attendance were read from Major Pickmere and mediately accepted. Mr. E. M. Crosfield. . THE PAST YEAR'S INCOME. It vi,is stated that the contributions from the employes for 1895 amounted £530 9s. oil., compared with £449 12-;. 9.1. in 1894. The workingmen's infirmary concert at Parr aall, on October 26,1895, realized £7l 6s. 3d., and the half proceeds from the Infirmary benefit night at Mr. Ohmy's circus £l5 IN.—Mr. JOHN ROBERTS said he was pleased to find an increase in the vsorkingmen's subscriptions this year. Ever since the Wsrkinginen's Committee was formed in the year I.EBO they had been endeavouring to make the annual subscriptions into £50.0, and last year they succeeded in doing so. At first tile subscriptions only amounted to £5O A. £6O, so that it would be seen that the interest of the workingmen in the institution had greatly increased. (dear, hear.) . They suggested for the consideration of the Management ,•Committee that all cases of accidents which should cause the sufferer loss of employment should be attended to without reference to the question of income, and that to meet such cases a special form of accident recommenda- tion be issued.--.-The printed statement as to the contri- butions of the employes was then taken as read, on the motion of Mr. GEORGE DEOMPSON, seconded by Mr. 'GROSSKILL.</p>
        <p>Mr. John Morley will he warmly welcomed or re- turning to the House of Commons, and not merelyl by friends on the Opposition side. His victory was., of course, never in doubt. The fact that he has vastly increased the Liberal majority is another tribute to his strength as a platform controversia- list, which was made so brilliantly conspicuous in his contest with Mr. Ralli at Newcastle after he had accepted office as Chief Secretary in 1892. As to Southamption, it is frankly admitted by Sir Francis Evans and the local Liberals that much of the success achieved must be attributed to Mr. William; Allan,: the member far 'Gateshead, Mr. Allan directed his attention mainly to the malcon- tent labour vote, which was withheld from the Liberals at the last election, 4nd made a personal oauvass of the "dockers " ola the day before the poll.</p>
        <p>IC the hcveless carriage does not make headway in popular favour this year it will not be from want of publicity. Already two exhioitions have been arranged in London, and there will probably be more. One is to be held at the Imperial Institute from May to August, and will he of "a comprehen- sive character. "The very latest developments of motor carriages propelled by the agencies of oil., steam, electricity or compressed air, will he shown and worked, as well as the practical application ef motors to tramway cars, locomotives, fire engines, vans, omnibuses, phaetons, cabs,,quadricycles, tri- cycks,and bicycles, carriages of every kind,marine, stationary, and portable motors of every descrip- tion, as well as electric and other installations." This is under the auspices of the Motor Car Ciub, the other is to be held at the Crystal Palace.</p>
        <p>EXECUTIVE COMMITTEE</p>
        <p>_ The following gentlemen were elected the Executive .committee for the ensuing year -:—Messrs. John Roberts (chairman), David Fanshaw, George Legge, Thomas Burton, John Griffiths, Charles Bunch, Thomas Wads- worth, Gorge Thomps _in, C. 11. Hibbert, J. P. 1 aylor, William Crosskill, George Walker, H. Emerson, William Higginson, John Waring, William Cosgrove, Joseph Cor- bett, Thomas Ice, William Gleave, and Thomas Ather- ton.</p>
        <p>'Looking forward., perhaps, to the coming time when the Manchester, Sheffield, and Lincolnshire Railway will run its own trains into London, that company has turned out from its works at Gorton a very handsome set of new coaches, for use on the London and Manchester expr-sses. For tile pre- sent they will form the two p m.down from King's- cross one day,and the like up train (two p.m.) from London-road, Manchester, the day following. The third-class compartments have the seats set very low, with quite a comfortable width between the opposite ones, so that passengers can get in and out without at all incommoding those sitting down ; the padding of the backs runs up as high as could be wished. The first-class carriages are diffe- rent from each other as to fittings, but all are very handsomely upholstered, and have lavatory accom- modation. The most distinctive feature of the new stock is theroupiends to the first-class coaches, which have a great t xpanse of window.</p>
        <p>The Earl of Mansfield, who attained his ninetieth birthday the other day, is, accord- ing to a French writer, "The most pic- turesque figure in England's aristocracy." His lord- ship, who well deserves such a description, wears the bottle-green coat and high roll collar of the last generation, and, in spite of the pretests of his family, resolutely refuses to change to a more modern style. Earl Mansfield is said to be the "Earl" whom Mrs. Hodgson Burnett aimned in the old and eccentric nobleman of "Little Lord Fauntleroy."</p>
        <p>The Jameson troopers were rather fully in evi- dence in various parts of London on Monday, and in many instances in the clothes in which they landed. Very incongruous too did some of them look, especially when several inches of stocking were disclosed between the end of the riding breeches and the tops of the ammunition boots some of them wore. A great deal of needless trouble has evidently been taken in bringing many of them back, as a large number are said to have booked passages for .next Saturday to the Cape, in- tending to go at once to Rhodesia ,for further ser- vice. They may not be accepted as troopers there again, but many at them have claims in the Char- tered Company's lands.</p>
        <p>Although no further news of the reported dis- covery by Dr. Nansen of the North Pole has been received either from the explorer himself or from the trader who is supposed to have obtained news of him off the cast ot Siberia, I learn from Norway that Dr. Nansen's relatives are now more inclined to credit the statements than they at first were. It cannot, however, be long before the real value of the news forwarded by the Siberian merchant is known, as the Russian Government is instituting the strictest inquiry into the matter through the Governor General of Eastern Siberia, who is now at St. Petersburg. Until the result of the enquiry is know, geographers here say that there is rut hing more to be said, now that every Arctic expert of any note has bad his say on the etzbject.</p>
        <p>I learn that the Council of the English Arboricul- tural Society is considering the desirableness of establishing an examination, with certificates, for the benefit of practical foresters. Although it is not intended to require candidates to furnish evi- dence of having attended any systematic course of instruction, the existence of the examination will no doubt have the effect of creating a demand for teaching in an important and at present somewhat neglected department of technical education. The examination will be of both a theoretical and prac- tical character.</p>
        <p>The retiring clerk of the London County Council will receive as pension £SIG a year, that being at the rate of one-sixtieth of his salary for each year of service. The Council would have liked to signify its high appreciation of Mr. De La Hooke' s long and valuable services, extending over 31 yeare,by grant- him a higher retiring allowance, but they have not the legal power to do so.</p>
        <p>It has before been stated that the Rivers' Com- mittee of the London County Council contemplate the establishment of a municipal service of steam- boats on the Thames. rho question of the river traffic was discussed at Tuesday's meeting of the Council, but no reference was made to this parti- tular scheme. I have, however, seen a report in which all the details are worked out. It is proposed to ozpend a capital of £1,000,000 on the undertak- ing, with an annual outlay of £400,000. The steamers are to run from five o'clock in the morn- ing until midnight, and it is estimated that an an- nual profit of £6,000 can be made.</p>
        <p>A private letter from Sydney, New South liVales, dated January 19, says :—" We have been having a very intimate acquaintance with real summer. It has been so hot that all the observatory records have been exceeded, and they go back for sixty or seventy years. Last Monday we had 108ieg. in the shade,and during the week it has been over 100 deg. several times. Yesterday morning it was 99deg. for a little while, but a southerly 'buster' came up and cooled things a bit. In the back country the thermometer has been up to 130 deg. There have been many deaths from sunstroke, the estimated number being 200, several in this city."</p>
        <p>Rechad Effendi, brother of the Sultan and pros- pective heir to the throne of Turkey, is kept a prisoner by Abdul Hamid. He is permitted to read neither books nor newspapers. Rechad is fifty years of age, tall and well-proportioned, and re- sembles the Sultan in face.</p>
        <p>It was like old times on Wednesday afternoon listening to Mr. John Morley discussing Irish affairs albeit be spoke from the side of the table opposite to that on which he has been accustomed to stand. The fate which has sent him to Scotland for a seat is no new thing. The northern kingdom has always had a reputation for steadfastness. Thus, when Mr. Roebuck was sent temporarily into the wilderness by the fickleness of Bath, be was found looking longingly northward. "1 wish very much," he wrote," to represent a Scots constituency. Once in se nftoart iv e apliascesuorne opfrohpisersepatrianscilpolnegs, aasuLtherermeapirnes- true," and he lugubriously adds, 'This is not the case with us."</p>
        <p>To MoTirEns.—Mrs. Winslow's Soothing syrup has been used over fifty years by millions of mothers for theirchildren while teething with perfect success. It will relieve the poon sufferer immediately. It is pleasant to taste ; it natural quiet sleep, by relitqing the child from pain, and ths little cherub awakes "as brighti as a button." Of all chemists. la 11d. per bottle.—ildvt. producer</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>MONDAY. LORD SALISBURY AND PROTECTION.</p>
        <p>Replying to a question put by Mr. G. Whiteley in the lions* of Commons on Monday, Mr. Chaplin' answering Sir J. T. Brunner, said the population of the districts in England where the notification of disease was compul- sory was at the last census over twenty-seven millions, out of a total population of twenty-nine millions. Sir M. WHITE RIDLEY said the question of licensing bicycles with a view to the identification of their owners was worthy of consideration, though it was full of diffi- culties. Any sums received for such licenses would naturally go to the relief of local rates. In reply to Mr. S. Buxton, Mr. CHAMBERLAIN said he proposed to consent to the appointment of Earl Grey as administrator of the Chartered Company's property. The debate on Mr. Balfour' s resolution devoting Fri- days to Supply and limiting discussion under that head to twenty days was resumed.—Mr. Balfour replied upon the debate, and accepted the suggestion that the rule should be tried for a session only, instead of being added permanently to the Standing Orders.</p>
        <p>In the House of Lords Lord RUSSELL of Killowen ob- tained some modification of a clause in the LORD CHAN- CELLOR'S bill for the amendment of the law of evidence. Lord STANLEY of Alderley put a question to the PRIME MINISTER with regard to a return showing the prices of wheat and bread in other countries, and incidentally challenged the statement recently made by the noble Marquis to the effect that France had suffered as severely under Protection as Engls,nd had under Free Trade. The PRIME MINISTER,iII the course of his reply, denied that in the speech referred to he had advocated protection. "He did not believe," he added, " that the protection of articles of the first necessity would ever come during any period it was necessary for them to consider.'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>TUESDAY.</head>
        <p>TUESDAY.</p>
        <p>The QUEEN'S reply to the Address was presented to the house of Commons on Tuesday. Her MAJESTY thanked the members of the House for their sympathy with her and her daughter in the loss they had sustained by the death of Prince Henry of Battenberg. The CHANCEE,LOR of the ExcuEotsEft informed Mr. OSWALD that there was no probability of an alteration of the obverse, or Queen's head, side of the new farthings, in order to prevent mistakes and fraud. The word " farthing" was stamped in clear letters, and tne ab- sence of milling round the edge made the difference be- tween a farthing and a half sovereign more obvious. In answer to Mr. W. A Lturi,lllr. BALFOUR repeated bis former statement,to the effect that the Government had no objection to an inquiry into the tied-house system, provided the various parties interested in the question could come to an agreement as to the terms of the re- ference. He hoped such an agreement would be come to. Before the debate on the resolution to limit the dis- cussion on Supply to twenty days was resumed, Mr. BALFOUR proposed that his motion should have prece- dence of the orders of the day and notices of motion on any day for which they might b 3 appointed. After some discussion the proposal was adopted by 336 votes to 125. The debate on the resolution was then resumed. Sir R. REID said it was his intention to support the resolu• tion throughout. Closure was necessary because of the great amount of business thrust upon the House, and until the system of devolution was adopted he did not see that the closure could be avoided. A number of the amendments to the resolution were disposed of before he House rose.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>WEDNESDAY.</head>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Wednesday Mr. CIANCT moved the second reading of the Evicted Tenants (Ire- land) Bill, which, he said, embodied a purely voluntary scheme, and was based on suggestions thrown out by leading Liberal Unionists. The main provision of the bill was the establishment of a board of conciliation, although no objection would be taken to the introduction of the Land Commission. The bill proposed that the Board should have ample funds, provided from Imperial sources, to be devoted to wiping out arrears and to enabling restored tenants to make a fresh start in life. It was thought that £250,000 would be sufficient for the purpose. Mr. W. REDMOND seconded the motion. Dr. RENTOUL moved the rejection of the bill. Mr. DILLoN having,on behalf of the Nationalist party, supported the bill, Mr. MORLEY pointed out that it carried with it the voluntary principle to an extent which, he was afraid, would considerably deprive it of its efficacy, and said be should be disappointed if the Chief Secretary was not prepared to accede to the second reading of the bill, the principles of which had been emphatically assented to by important members of the present Government. Mr. G. BALFOUR said it might be of advantage that there should be some means whereby a conciliator should come between landlords and tenants, but the proposal to appoint a Commission was not one to wlaich the Govern- ment could under any circumstances accede. Mr. A. LI/axone said that the Government intended to bring forward legislative proposals which, he trusted, would enable the evicted tenants to settle with their landlords. On a division the bill was defeated by 271 votes to 174, and the House rose.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>RICHARD EVANS AND CO„ LIMITED.</head>
        <p>RICHARD EVANS AND CO„ LIMITED.</p>
        <p>REPORT OF THE DIRECTORS.</p>
        <p>The report of the directors of Richard Evans and Co., Limited, to the shareholders, states that the output of the collieries during the year amounted to 1,268,631 tons, which, compared with 1894, shows an increase of 88,779 tons. Prices have continued to decline. At the Princess Pit capital expenditure is nearly completed. The equipment of Nos. 4 and 5 pits, Parr, is progressing satisfactorily. The brick works continue in fair work, but the demand, owing to continuance of bad trade, is not brisk. The business of the company at Acton Grange Shipping Wharf is increasing. The fixed and movable plant has been kept in an efficient condition, and additions have been made thereto. A small addition by purchase has been made to the freehold properties of the company. The additions under the head of lease- hold property and agency plant account are for plant and equipment connected with the extensions of the company's agencies for the sale of coal, &amp;c. The ac- counts for the year ending December 31st, 1895, show, including income from the property of the company, a profit of £45,313 9s. 9d., making with the balance of £BB7 03. Bd. broeght forward at the commencement of the year £46,200 103. 51. From this amount has to be deducted the sum of £18,797 8 bl. written off for de- preciation, leaving a balance of £27,403 2s. From this amount interest on the debenture has been deducted, amounting to £13,291 131. 4d., leaving a disposable balance of £14,111 Bs. 81., out of which an interim dividend for the half-year at the rate of £3 per cent, per annum (less income tax) was paid on July 15, 1895, ab- sorbing £8,132 193. 31., and leaving a balance still available for distribution of £5,978 93. 51. To the balanco, as above, of £5,978 93. 51. your directors pro- pose adding £B,OOO from the reserve fund, making to- gether £13,978 93. sd. available for payment of dividend, and thew recommend a dividend for the half-rear at the rate of 2 per cent, (less tax), making the interim divi- dend a total dividend for the year 1895 of 4 per cent. (less tax). This will absorb £13,596 103., leaving a balance of £3Bl 19s. sd. to be carried forward to the credit of profit and loss account.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>DEATH OF THE REV. G. F. ROBERTS.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF THE REV. G. F. ROBERTS.</p>
        <p>We announce with regret the death of the Rev. George Farrar Roberts.M.A.,vicar of St. Helens Church, Hollin- fare, which took place on Sunday afternoon, at four o'clock, at his residence,Beechfield,Glazebrook. The de- ceased clergyman's life has been a very active one in many directions. He was ordained in the year 1868, took priest's orders in the following year, at about this time he officiated as one of the curates of Warrington Parish Church, and in 1871 he was appointed by the then Rec- tor of Warrington (the Rev. W. Quekett), as Vicar of Hollinfare, Lancashire, which position he retained up to the time of his death. For many yeara he resided at Juniper Cottage, Martinscroft. About ten years ago he built his present house near Glazebrook station, and re- sided in it up to the time of his death. During the local government changes of 1894 he took an active part in enlightening his parishioners, and was ultimately elected district councillor. Since he has been the Vicar the St. Helens Church, which stands a short distance from Liverpool-road, has been greatly altered. The day schools have also been enlarged and improved, and he took a great interest in the formation of the new cemetery,whese frontage faces the church. The roads in the vicinity were improved at the same time, and indeed the immediate neighbourhood of the church altered. He has also been a very prominent and useful member of the Warrington Board of Guardians. For several years he discharged the duties of Chairman of the Board. He had also been Chairman of the Padgate Scnools Visiting Committee, the Workhouse Visiting Committee, the Union School Attendance Committee, and at the time of his death he was Chairman of the Assessment Commit- tee and the Rural District Council ; and it was this last- named honour which entitled him to sit as a county magistrate. He was also a member of several of the more important committees of the Board. He had an extensive and useful knowledge of poor law administra- tion, and his loss to the union generally will be very great. His last appearance at the Board was on the 4th September, 1895. The deceased was in the earlier stages of his illness attcnded by Dr. Sephton, of Culcheth, and latterly by Dr. Steele, of Manchester. The pulpit at St. Helen's Church was occupied on Sunday by the Rev. Canon Willis. rector of Warrington, but no -direct refer- ence was made to Mr. Roberts's death. He leaves a widow and one son.</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CRICKETERS.—Messrs. George G. Bussey and Co.. have issued their " Cricketer's Diary and Com- panion "for 1896. In it are the usual pages for memor- andas. results of county and other important matches for 1895 (Ist class), English and Australian fixtures for 1896, records, batting aad howling averages,higli scores, laws of cricket, and three plans of field positions for various bowling. The Diary is neatly hound with gilt edged paper, and is also well and cleverly printed. For general usefulness it s invaluable to cricketers</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>WYCLIFFE P.S.A. TOTAL ABSTINENCE</head>
        <p>WYCLIFFE P.S.A. TOTAL ABSTINENCE</p>
        <p>SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>On Monday evening a well attended meeting of the Total Abstinence branch of the P.S.A. Society vi as held in the unper schoolroom at Wycliffe. Councillor ROBERTS occupied the chair,and in his open- ing remarks said he did not think that at present teeto- talism was as popular as it might be and as they would like it to be ; he doubted if it was as popular now as it was 12 months ago. As they knew, many of them had an idea that it was the duty of the State with regard to the liquor traffic to allow the people themselves to de- cide how mach or how little they would have, and upon that question the general election was fought, but un- fortunately for them their opinions were not in the as- cendancy, and they had therefore an adverse majority ire Parliament which was largely floated in on the wave of drink. That could not last for ever. (Hear, hear.) There was a public conscience which sooner or later would exert itself in the right direction. He thought every person who took the slightest interest in the affairs of this country ought to read a letter which was sent a few days ago by the Rev. Dawson Burns to the " Times." It was a letter which he wrote every year for the" Times" in connection with the money spent upon intoxicating liquors. When they found that during last year the drink hill went up by £3,876.000. or in other words it increased from £138,000,600, in 1894 to £142,000,000 in 1895, he thought that awful increase was in itself sufficient food for reflection for every man and woman in the country. It meant that the expen- diture per head of the p6pulation,men, women and chil- dren. was £3 12s 9p. and for every family of 5 persons £lB 33. lOid. Those figures he thought deserved a little consideration. It was more that a serious matter,—the wonder was how the money was found to spend. Some- thing must go short and one did not need to go far for the explanation. If it was not for that terrible expendi- ture of money they would not have such cases of poverty and child neglect as those which came up in the Police Court. Here it was found that the sole cause of that terr- ible condition of things was the fact that the money that should go for food; and clothes, was going into the pub- licans' and brewers' pockets. That was rather too bad and needed a man's most eager and earnest interven- tion. Miss B. DUTTON, in the course of a lengthy and en- couraging address on temperance and temperance work, said she was very glad to be there to congratulate them upon having a temperance society in connection with the P.S.A. Right glad she was to cheer its members in any way by just putting in her little word. (Applause) She exhorted those who were not teetotalers to throw in their lot with them if only as an example to the children, and an aid to them in serving the God they wanted them to serve. Songs were given by Miss May Pierpoint and Miss Broadhurst in an excellent manner. Mr.Taylor,of Earles• town, gave an amusing Lancashire reading, and Mr.H. M. Ashton delivered "Bill Adams," with the usual mirth- ful result. Mr. Sandal!, of Bristol,gave an instrumental illustration of the fairy bells. The meeting concluded with the singing of the doxo- logy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>"CAPITAL" CHM.</head>
        <p>"CAPITAL" CHM.</p>
        <p>7'3lE COLLECZIONS.</p>
        <p>It was decided that the collections be made as usual at the la ter end of May and ,November, on the motion oi Mr. FANsriaw, seconded by Mr. LEGGE.</p>
        <p>VOTES OF THANKS.</p>
        <p>On the motion of Mr. BURTON, seconded by Mr. HIGGIN.OI3, a hearty vote of thanks was acc.srded to the President of the Infirmary, Mr. J. 0. Parr, to the resi- dent surgeons, and to the medical men of the town for their services, during the past twelve months.—Mr. FANSHAW moved a vote of thanks to Mr. E. M. Orosfield for his services as hon. secretary. Mr. Crosfield did not come to see them very often. He wished he would do so. Mr. WATKINS seconded the motion which was agreed to. On the motion of Mr.Laoos,se,;onded by Mr.CROSSKILL, a similar compliment was paid to the assistant secretary, Mr. Joseph Taylor. Mr. GEORGE WALKER proposed a vote of thanks to Mr. Roberts for his past services as chairman of the committee. Mr. WATKINS seconded the motion, which was agreed to. Mr. ROBERTS, in reply, said he had had the pleasure of being the chairman of that committee since it was formed, in 1880. He had acted to the best of his ability. Indeed, there was no committee in which he took more interest than the one in question.</p>
        <p>TEE PLACE OF MEETING:</p>
        <p>Mr. GEORGE TH---011-PS-0-N-;-ai-d they had been accustomed for some years to hold their annual meeting in the Mayor's Parlour at theTownHall.Now,they had been com- pelled to find a meeting place elsewhere, He was glad the Mayor was present because, as ratepayers, who did the best the could for the Infirmary, they did not think it was fair that they should be turned out of the Town Hall without some just reason being given.—The MAYOR said he should like to explain that the Town Hall was totally inadequate for the business which had to be transacted in it, and it was felt that if permission was given for the Workingmen s Committee to meet there, that the representatives of the various institutions in the town would expect a similar privilege, and it had,there- fore, been decided to put a stop to all similar meetings being held at the Town Hall. PROPOSED ALTERATION_ OF THE ROLES.</p>
        <p>Mr. WataKna.said he would like an explanation to be given with reference, to the proposed alteration of the rules. It was very desirable that every member of the committee and every working man should know in what relation he stood to the institution. Several cases had recently occurred in which men had been injured, and in accordance with rule 92 they had had to provide a recommendation on their second visit. Most of the workmen who subscribed to the institution through the means provided by the Workingmen's Committee were also subscribers to clubs from which they received bene- fit, but the fact that they received this benefit was suf- ficient to deter them from receiving any benefit from the Infirmary. That was rather a peculiar position to be placed in, .because it was, in fact, to'a certain extent, a tax on thrift. He could not see bow they could rearn- ably expect workingmen to maintain their subscriptions to the Infirmary when they knew they were precluded from possible benefit. It was therefore suggested that all cases of accidents should be treated at the Infirmary as long as that accident caused the loss of employment— until the patient was thoroughly restored and without any reference to his earnings. (Applause.) An expres- sion of opinion on this matter should go from that meet- ing:tm the Board of Management of the Infirmary. (Hear hear, and applause.)—Mr. Trroxesorg said it was impos- sible to thorouchly co into the question that day. They had an Executive Committee which consisted of their own representatives, and they should have confidence in them. The matter in question was to come under the nr;tice of the Board of Management of the Infirmary on Frid my. When that meeting had been held, another meeting of the Workingmen's:Committee should be called —The MAYOR said this was a very vexed question. He should take it very bard indeed if he had to suffer be- cause he was a member of a club and had made prepara- tions f3r a rainy day. He thought that all ought to be treated alike especially as the institution was got up for the benefit of the workingmen of Warrington generally. (Applause.)—Mr. JOHN FAIRCLOVGH said he had never heard of any difficulty being raised about certificates. The Board of Management could not alter the rules. That had to be done by the governors. Through certain disagreements which had risen of late, the Board• of Management had framed new rules. These had been examined, and they were to come on for final discussion on Friday. The Infirmary was a charitable insti- tution, and he did not think it could be regarded in any other light. (Hear, hear).—After some further discus- sion. in which Mr. Thompsen, Mr. Crosskill, and Mr. Fanshaw took part, it was decided to defer the further consideratio9 of the matter until the Infirmary meeting had been held, and then a general meeting of the Work- ingmen's Committee will be called to consider the mat- ter-1 vote of thanks to the Mayor for presiding, and to Mr. John Fairclough for his attendance, having been passed, the meeting terminated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>SHIP CANAL.</head>
        <p>SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>LIST WEEK'S TRAFFIC.</p>
        <p>As compared with the previous seven days the sea- going traffic upon the Ship Canal last week showed a slight fallir:,-; off. The defici.ncy was wholly in the out- ward traffic, the arrivals having been fully up to the average. Coastwise trade deceased by 1,997 tons, although the number of vesselslwas greater by 28, and in the foreign department there was an increase of five ships and 439 tons. Amongst the arrivals were 19 vessels with general cargoes, nine with pig iron, 11 with china clay, &amp;c., seven with stone and slate, four with wood pulp, four with fruit, two with roofing-tiles, and one with cotton. 23 vessels sailed with general cargoes, including one each to Alexandria and Bombay; coat was shipped to the South coast, Ireland, and Laguna ; Ipitch to Cette, salt to Scotland. and bricks to the Isle of Man.</p>
