      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>OLD COUNTRY.</head>
        <p>THE OLD COUNTRY.</p>
        <p>(BY MR. H. 'WRITRim). I wonder does anyone hope to see the day when the plougshare will have taken the place of the sword, and the lion and the lamb will peacefully repose beside each other without the stronger be- ing overcome with a desire to absort the weaker? The events of the past few weeks must have made many a" doubting Thomas" of these nineteenth century old statements. Ten or twelve years ago great efforts were made to establish throughout England branches of the Peace and Arbitration Societies. It was felt at that time that the money that was being spent upon armies and navies was worse than wasted—that it was being spent in criminal efforts to manufacture such murderous weapons of warfare that humanity and our boasted civilization might well stand aghast at. The lapse of years seems to have proved that we humanitar- ians, or, as we were called, " peace at any price men," were altogether wrong and that it was really true that the best way to preserve peace was to be prepared for war. Well, although appearances are against Us, I for one still believe that the European policy of keeping up one constant everlasting struggle to maintain the "Balance of power" by tha help of powder and shot is wrong, and stamps the nation doing it as lost to the least of the Chris- tian or Pagan virtues.</p>
        <p>„ Great Britain flaunts her flying squadron to the world, and then, I.suppose.the earth trembles. Not</p>
        <p>a bit of it. Briton's grandson simply steps out and demands such a sum of money as will enable him to go and do likewise. Germany must have a navy. Another youth over in Russia, not to be behind- hand declares that against England he will have</p>
        <p>ship for ship,alao clear sailing r—oom,and the Bear ab- sorbs the Turkey,to the infinite disgust of the riroud bearers of the message "Peace with Honour." The Royal Families of Europe have married and inter- married, until it would take a Philadelphia lawyer all his time to make it clear as to who is who, and</p>
        <p>like the commonest plebs they fall out and vex the nations of the world with their family jars and jealousies. Where will it all end? Well I think I see that we are now at the beginning of the end. As a result of the Monroe Doctrine squabble, and the almost universal feeling throughout the States that the land hunger no,; only of England, but of other European powers, is likely in the future to grow rather than weaken, the United States Government has entered with the full consent of the people,into the suicidal policy of determining to have also its Flying Squadron, and all other squadrons it can construct on as short order as possible. Of course England must rule the waves. To do so she will have to keep on building murder machines—France, Germany, Russia and America will do likewise and so we prepare the way for those who will follow us, to bring matters to a climax. To me it seems clear that events of the very greatest import- ance are quite within measurable distance, and that, should we be able tb ride over the present miserable jingo jealousies the term of peace will be but of short duration. My train of thought at this point, runs towards the world'B commerce in the future, but _I will leave it for a</p>
        <p>future letter, and try to explain, as 1 may, some o the features of the existing in this country towards the old country. It is quite likely that my education like that of thousands, I may say millions, of otlfer Engl.shmen was neglected when I was a boy. I was never taught that on two occa- sions America had beaten England in wars, and had sent the British lion growling home with his tail between his legs. At the schools I went to I have no remembrance of us having "Flag raising," con- sequently the British flag never hovered before me as a thing to be worshipped, nor yet was it the cus- tom of the scholars to be daily bawling themselves hoarse with "God save the Queen" or "Rule Brittania." This possibly explains why I grew up a Radical, and, if these things are done at some of the schools of proud Britain it explains the growth of the Jingo. The propotype of the British Jingo is of luxuri- ant growth out here in the States, I believe more especially in the Western States, and it is no un- usual thing for the son of a British born to shut in from school and practice his little fist at twisting the lion's tail, while he mutters to his dad, "we whipped you twice and we can whip you again if I we want." While I believe it would be impossible to get up in England any war feeling against America, and that such a thing as hatred towards the States has no existence, it is quite difierent in this country towards England, and this no doubt arises from the fact that the wars of 1779 and 1812 are drilled into the minds of school children as lead- ing events in the country's history. More than once have I talked with the "native son" about this hatred and tried to get at the bottom of it. I al- ways find that it dates from the school days. The children are never taught that no portion of the British people ever think with pleasure at the do- ings of Ring George or of his minister Lord North, nor yet are they taught that England refused to join the Holy Alliance, and by such action making the United States a possibility, nor yet that Eng- land during the latter war, was engaged in destroy- ing the greatest scourge the 19th century has seen, and so had her hands pretty full. The one evil result of this vicious system of edu- cation is the growth of a conceited,self-opinionated class of men, who hearing and reading nothing but the blatant political stuff given to them by the self-seeking American politician, do really believe the American continent to be, in all its richness,the work of their own hands, and they themselves the God ordained saviours of the world. Thanks to ft truer and higher education that is given-tit the uni- versities and is gained by travel, thanks also to a bigger brained and better class of men, hailing chiefly from the New England States,England does, through all this jealous hatred, receive a certain measure of praise, and it almost makes full amends when one reads such aspeech as that recently made by Senator Woolcot in the Senate, wnen the Sena- tor up and told how proud he was he came of English stock. In spite of the oft repeated assertion in all the Western papers it was gratifying to find that even a leading French paper could say,—speaking of this univeral hatred towards England, "Is it necessary to say that nothing is further from the mind and heart of a Frenchman than the idea of such hatred? No. We have on the contrary, a grateful and, we may say, almost a tender admirati, n for the land of Shakespeare, for the nation which has enriched humanity with that incomparable literature, for that, virile habit of liberty. Without England the world would suffer the eclipse of a star of the first magnitude." But when all this is said, it still remains a fact that from the Pacific to the Atlantic an immense majority of the voting power would be given in favour of war with England. Not with Great Bri- tain. No greater mistake can be made than to think that this anti-English sentiment is confined to persons of Irish extraction. It is inconceivable to me how it comes about, but all the speeches made, and all the articles written by the high men are brimful of the venomous hatred ofEngland. Senater Mitchell, of Oregon, in a report recently presented to the Senate, said, "We must legislature to increase the value of our export commodities to enable us to meet, reduce, and if possible, wipe out the debt which to-day makes the people of this country virtually slaves to the money-lenders of Great Britain." Words like those burn themselves into the hearts of the people, and they fret and fume with the thought that what England could not do with the sword she is accomplish- ing with the money bag. The free silver party was jubilant at the downfall of the Rosebery Government. With the advent of a Tory Government they were sure of a double money standard. The papers were full of lengthy tele- grams,and sanguine leaders, all cock-sure that Lord Salisbury would appoint or arrange for a conference of the nations on this subject. He gave the ques- tion the same rough treatment that he gave the English farmers petition for protection. He would none of it. Now the free silver men hate both your</p>
        <p>parties. Nothing would have given the war party greater pleasure than that the British government should have refused to give the American prisoners in the Transvaal its protection. The Irish element must also be considered when trying to find a reason for this hatred. There are two features in this and both are sufficiently strong to make a formidable anti-English patty of the Irish. The first, in my opinion, is the Romish church which seizes every opportunity to make u9e of the most bitter language against the English as a people. Then there is the Irish feeling with whioli we are all familiar. The latest figures give the Catholic population at 10,000,000, and show that the mem- bers of that church exceed in number any other denomination of Christianity. I think I have said enough to show that English- men must not rely upon any sentimental feeling in their kin beyond the sea. Other nationalities are here in their millions and few of them love Eng- land. The few who love the old country may be the brainy ones, but they are a small minority. The great majority say with glee,— " What's the use in bootless kicking? Heaven perchance has willed it so, England's doomed to gets; licking, Every hundred years or so. 2,409 Mission-street, ~‘.lan Francisco.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>LATCHFORD PRIMITIVE METHODr-T BAND or lIOPg. A public meeting and entertainment in connection with the above society was held in the schoolroom, Kuutsford-road, on Monday evening, when in the absence of Mr. Sandford, the chair was occupied by Mr. E. Maim After the meeting had been opened with singing and prayer, Miss Sarah Heesom gave the recitation" Is it true ?'' for which she was well received. The applause which followed .tiLiss Annie Berry's rendering of "Jim and the Jack Ass" was well merited. Miss Elizabeth Breadon sang "Softly and Tenderly "in good style, and Miss Fanny Cross ably recited "Is it caused by beer ?" Miss Alice Hilton was deservedly applauded for her contribu- tion of "They did not think," "Eight little mothers,', and" Oh, swiftly," which were given in excellent style rarely met with in one so young. Miss Elizabetti Berke, although suffering from a severe cold, did very well in her recitation, " The Publican's Story," and a Scotch hymn. A Lancashire' recitation, " Don't go out to-fleet," by Miss Eliza- beth Breadon, was well received; and a reading by Mr. J. Cross caused considerable laughter. The programme was brought to a close by a dialogue, "Money, not beer," which received a good treat- ment at the hands of the Misses White, Lamb,Cross, Brimeiow, Buckley, and Messrs. J. Cross and E. Main. Mr. J. Massey presided at the organ. A successful and pleasant evening was brought to a conclusion by the singing of the Doxology. Tu ELECTION OF BOROUGH AUDITORS.—A CON- TEsT AVOIDED. The Mayor attended in his parlour at the Town Hall, on Monday, to adjudicate on the nomination papers received for the election of borough audi- tors. There were also present Mr. J. Lyon Whittle (town clerk), Mr. Stanley Wilson (assistant town clerk), Mr. John Fairhurst (borough treasurer), Mr. John Clayton (Liberal agent), Mr. William Hol- brook (Conservative agent), and others. The fol- lowing objections were put in to the nomination paper of Mr. A. W. howard, and were supported by several of those who had subscribed o the nominal. tion paper :—That the assent preceded the nomina- tion. That all or several of the assenting hurgesses subscribed the nomination paper before the pra- poser had subcribed the same. That all or several of the assenting burgesses subscribed the nomina- tion paper before the seconder had subscribed the same. These objections were offered by Mr. James Bolton' whilst substantially . the same ones was brought by Mr. John Harrison.—The Mayor al- lowed the objections, so that Mess-s. James Bolton and John Harrison are now practically re-elected, and a contest on the 2nd March has been avoided. FUNERAL OF MRS KAYE. The remains of Mrs. Kaye, of Beweey-road, War- rington—who was so brutally murdered by an un- known man at Arnold, Nottingham, on the 10'.h inst.—were interred at Basford Cemetery on Satur- day afternoon. Mrs. Kaye, by her kindly nature, had inspired a deep regard for her in a large circle of acquaintances, many of whom paid their tribute of respect to her memory by attending the funeral. Wreaths were sent by the sons and daughters; Messrs. G. 11. and F. 3lorrall. Warrington, grand- sons; Mr. and Mrs. S. Povvley, Arnold; Mr. and Mrs. Evans, Mr. and Mrs. J. Wilkinson, Mrs. Dutton,Mr. Fieldhouse and family, Warrington; Mr. and Mrs. Hayes, Netherfield ; Mts. Gilbert and Miss Terry, Nottingham. The chief mourners were :—Mr. and Mrs. Morrell, Mr. and Mrs. Kaye, Mr. and Mrs. Brandwood, Mr. and Mrs. Powley, Mr. and Mrs. Grundy, Mr. and Mrs. Hayes, Mr. W. Kaye, and Mr. A. Kaye. From three to four huudred of the general public also assembled in the cemetery, and although Superintent Billington was present with a number of subordinate officers there appeared to. be no need for this precaution, as the people were, orderly and respectful. The service was conducted by the Rev. A. Burnet, of Arkwright-st. Wesleyani Chapel.</p>
        <p>TOWLE'S CHLORODYNE.</p>
