      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY, February 29th, 1896.</p>
        <p>THE most vital point in the The Ship Canal affairs of the Ship Canal Cora- Company. pany at the present time is the one relating to the growth of traffic. When traffic begins to increase by "leaps and bounds" the ultimate success of the Canal as a commercial</p>
        <p>undertaking may be regarded as assured. On the other hand, if traffic does not increas e or only in-</p>
        <p>creases at a slow and uncertain rate the share- holders who have in one form or another provided the fifteen millions of capital may well be appre- hensive as to the final result of their investment. The information bearing upon this important</p>
        <p>question of traffic which was given by the Chair- man of the Canal Company at the general meeting on Wednesday was, unfortunately, not very de- cisive either way. There was undoubtedly an increase of traffic in 1895 as compared with 1894 —an increase in fact of sea-going traffic of 401,000 tons, equal to fifty-eight per cent., but then, as Mr. BYTHELL himself says, this increase falls short of what the promoters expected. It has only been secured, too, by exceptionally strenuous efforts and by the holding out of special inducements. To a certain extent, it may be regarded as a result of an artificial stimulus which cannot be applied per- manently. On the other hand, the Chairman of the Company proved very .clearly that the Canal has had to contend so far with a relentless opposi- tion from the railway companies which deal with the Manchester traffic. These railway companies are, it appears, acting in combination, and to judge by the examples quoted by Mr. BYTHELL they are discriminating against the Canal with an extraordinary and altogether unfair severity. The Canal directors have endeavoured to come to some workable and mutually agreeable arrangements with the railway magnates but they have utterly failed, and they now seam to have no course open to them but an appeal to the Railway Commissioners. It is easy to understand how the railway charges complained of by the Canal Company will seriously dis- courage the growth of traffic on the canal. Aus- tralian wool, for instance, is carried by the railway companies from Goole to Bradford at a rate of us. 3d., but for conveying to the same destination -the six thousand bales which were taken to Manchester by the steamer Gulf of Siam 13s. 11d, was charged, although Bradford is nearer Manchester than Goole. It is quite clear that the direct trade between Manchester and the Australian colonies,of which there has been a great deal of promise lately, may be very seriously impeded if not altogether destroyed by railway tariffs of this description. The hostility of the railway companies has been even more manifest in connection with the carting of goods at Manchester. All the exemptions and special terms which are accorded large firms are withheld from the Canal Company with the result that traders must often find it more expensive to con- sign their goods by the Canal than by other routes. It is, therefore, not surprising that the traffic on the Canal has not increased so rapidly as was expected ; and, as a matter of fact,it is to be feared that no very rapid increase will take place till 'what is practically a " boycot " by the railway companies has been removed either through the interposition of the Railway Commissioners or by friendly negotiation. With regard to the financial position of the Company, Mr. BYTHELL stated that after a care- ful study of the position the directors saw their way to meeting all the calls that would be made upon them until the autumn. He candidly admitted, however, that in the autumn the situa- tion would again have to.be reviowed,and he hinted that steps might then have to be taken for an application to Parliament in 1897 for powers to raise more capital. Additional equipments will be needed in order to deal with additional traffic, and the funds in hand are not sufficient to meet the demands that will be made upon them in this respect. When this is taken into account, and also the fact that the interest on the Man- chester Corporation loans continue to accumulate, it is clear that, whatever may be said of the en- gineering difficulties that the Canal Company has had to encounter, its financial troubles are by no means at an end. In concluding his speech, Mr. BYTHELL referred to those who predicted that the undertaking might collapse or that the canal might develope into merely a barge canal, and he emphatically declared that they would be griev- ously mistaken. He considers that the canal will remain "a permanent and magnificent memorial Of Manchester enterprise,"and that it wilt be saved with the" loyal assistance of Manchester and Lan- cashire traders."</p>
        <p>Three Elections. Tai three elections which have taken place within the last seven</p>
        <p>or eight days although they have only resulted in one net gain for the Liberal party are without exception full cf encouragement for the Liberal cause. Mr. MORLEY'S return for the Montrose Burghs by the enormous majority of 1,993 is a notable event in more ways than one.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>EXAMINER SATURDAY, FEBRUARY 21 1896</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER SATURDAY, FEBRUARY 21 1896</p>
        <p>In the first place it may be taken to indicate that the weakening of Scotch Liberalism which was noticed at the General Election was of a very temporary character and is now at an end. Mr. MORLEY fought his battle splen- didly. His speeches were powerful and eloquent expositions of Liberal principles and policy and criticisms of Tory political methods, and the result is a victory which exceeded the most sanguine expectations. The effect of this election will be, as Mr. MORLEY himself said after tho , declaration of the poll, "to scatter some Tory hopes in Scotland and to quicken Liberal hopes in England." On the same day that Montrose in the North returned this decisive verdict, South- ampton in the South reversed its decision at the General Election by sending a Liberal to the House of Commons in place of a Tory who had been unseated on petition. To complete the test Lichfield in the Midlands on Wednesday returned a Liberal by a greatly increased majority to replace a Liberal who had been unseated on petition. One significant feature in these elections is that the Liberal successes are due not to Iny decline in the Tory or Unionist vote when compared with the General Election but to a decided increase in the number of votes cast for the Liberals. This, we take it, is full of promise for the Liberal party. ' It indicates that the voters who were indifferent or alienated at the General Election are now rallying to the side of the progressive party. The Tories have -.lot been long in office but they have begun . to disclose the policy they intend to pursue, and ' that policy does not commend itself to the demo- cracy of the country.</p>
        <p>The opening scene in the trial of Dr. J ameson and the other leaaers of the South African raid was •witnessed at Bow-street Police Court, London, on Tuesday. According to one account the court was well filled with "ladies of rank and titla, directors of the Chartered Company, one of its dukes, soldiers of renown and others." Some of the " others" consisted of young men from the aristocratic quar- ters of London who, in their admiration for Jame- son, so far forgot what was due to a court of justice that the magistrate threatened to have them ejected. The prisoners were charged with that they "did unlawfully prepare and fit out a military ex- pedition to proceed against the dominion of a cer- tain friendly state " but were allowed out on bail till the preliminary hearing is re- sumed a fortnight hence. There is a dis- position in some quarters to regard the offence with which Jameson and his officers are charged as of a trivial nature, but Sir John Bridge, the pre- siding magistrate at Bow-street, does not look at it in this light. In his opinion—and most right- thinking people will endorse it—to practically levy war upon a friendly State is a "crime of the high- est possible gravity." It is almost certain to make many a home desolate and it may easily bring two previously friendly countries into a state of war.</p>
        <p>POLISK CHANGE. P.C. Lee who has been stationed at Runcorn for a number of years has been removed to Walton Superior, in the Stockton Heath section. During his stay in Run- corn he fulfilled his duties in an able manner. THE WESLEYAN GUILD PARLOUR. At a meeting of the memberS of this guild on Thursday evening,the Rev. F. Brackenbury gave a homely talk on his holiday in Ireland. The proceedings were presided over by Mr. Herbert Taylor. There was a good attend- ance, and an interesting and instructive time was spent. BRUNSWICK-ST. MUTUAL IMPROVEMENT SOCIETY..</p>
        <p>At the meeting t-thi; -society on Wednesday evening, Mr. T. Leach, jun., read a most interesting paper on "The Church and the World." He described the dangers which beset the Church, and pointed out that one weak- ness of the Church was in trying to come down to the maxims of the world, and popularising the church by such things. A short discussion afterwards ensued, taken part in by Messrs.Millington and J. Bisbrown. The chair was occupied by Mr. T. Simister. There was a good attendance. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>STARTLING SUICIDE AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>STARTLING SUICIDE AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Shortly before ten o'clock this morning Thomas Astbury, farmer and greengrocer, living at 2, Union- street, Runcorn, committed suicide by hanging himself. He was found by his servant, Miss Beb- bington, suspended from the ceiling of his stable with a rope tied round his neck. Assistance was quickly obtained but life was found to be extinct. It appears that the deceased had been in a depressed state of mind for some time, and had been attended to by Dr. Robinson. Deceased was exceedingly well known in the neighbourhood, and the event has created quite a sensation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>PARR HALL POPULAR CONCERTS.</head>
        <p>THE PARR HALL POPULAR CONCERTS.</p>
        <p>On Saturday evening another of the miscellane- ous concerts organised by. the Parr Hall Committee was given before a large audience in the Parr Hall, and,as several of the artistes taking part in it were local vocalists,much interest was shown in regard to many of the songs. Both Miss Eleanor Picton and Miss Annie F. Sutton succeeded in pleasing the audience to the extent of winning emphatic and often enthusiastic encores to which suitable re- sponses were given. Mr. W. H. Ireland also con- tributed two songs with a measure of success which would doubtless, as in the case of the ladies, have earned for him the compliment of a recall, had he not been suffering, as to his voice, from the effects of a cold. Miss Sophia Roberts played with good taste two pianoforte solos and in an encore piece of considerable difficulty displayed digital skill of no mean order. In the humorist portion of the pro- gramme the audience were treated to the novelty of the dual appearance of Mr. Mattie Cheshire and Master Aubrey Cheshire—father and son. The re- cent visits of Mr. Mel. B. Spurr and his great popu- larity at these concerts have made it difficult for any humorist who does not escape an odious com- parison, to follow him. It is all the more to Mr. Mattie Cheshire's credit therefore that he was able on Saturdey evening in his clever sketches and re- citals to hit so successfully the tastes of what must have been a critical audience. Although not gifted with that complete abandon and entire absence of self-consciousness which are indispeusible in the ideal humorist as recent developments in mono- logue entertainments have made him known to us, Mr. Cheshire imports into his Per- formances much genuine humour and evidences the possession of considerable musical and histrionic talent. The clever ventriloquial sketch,: given entirely without the customary and conventional apparatus, gave proof of an addi- tional gift which must count for something con- siderable in the equipment of a popular entertainer. Suffice it, therefore, to say that Mr. Cheshire was always loudly applauded and often encored. In regard to Master Aubrey Cheshire's performances •we must confess to holding rather mixed and per- haps somewhat contradictory opinions. Whilst recognising the precocious ability which must have been necessary to hold the audience's attention, as in "Hanging a picture " (Jerome) and in the song "The overgrown baby," we are not sure that this form of genius in one so young is quite what an audience likes to see or cares to applaud. Never- theless, it must be admitted that the little fellow displayed marvellous self-possession, and that he acquitted himself exceedingly well. Miss Richard- son and Mr. A. Picton shared the honour of accom- panists in the various songs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>INCEN.DIA.RISII AT BERLIN.</head>
        <p>INCEN.DIA.RISII AT BERLIN.</p>
        <p>AN EXTRAORDINARY MANIA.</p>
        <p>Mysterious fires have for some time been happening in a suburb of Berlin, and although they are clearly the work of an incendiary, no clue can be discovered as to the perpetrators. Rags saturated with petroleum are found every where,and the door mats are often discovered to be in the same condition, while here and there placards are pasted with the words" This house will be burned down to-night." Theunknown malefactors are even (says the " Daily News correspondent) extending their rascally operations to other parts of the town. Near the Goltz Strasse a placard has been found in a large Board school announcing the burning down of the building and the whole street. The affair has become so serious that the scientific world is beginning to give it attention. Professor Mendel, the psychologist, i of the opinion that the fires are the work of insane or hysterical persons. He recalls similar outbreaks in former years, speaking particularly of one in France in 1833. In this case the guilt was traced to a hysterical girl of nineteen, who had begun the disastrous work, and, so 'to say, infected a number of girls of the same age and tendency, so that at last there was a regular corps of female incendiaries.</p>
        <p>At Sale on Monday three ladies were fined for riding bicycles on the footpath. Two of them pleaded that they thought the police would not summon ladies. Edith Johnson, the daughter of a Canterbury auc- tioneer, a prepossessing girl of about 20, stabbed herself to death at the residence of a young man named Wood on Monday. At the inquest on Monday night a verdict 9fm suicide was returned. It is presumed that the girl injured herseif to excite the sympathy of her lover, and happened to divide an artery, bleeding to death in less than ten minutes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>RUNCORN TONIC SOL-FA. PRIZE</head>
        <p>RUNCORN TONIC SOL-FA. PRIZE</p>
        <p>CHOIR.</p>
        <p>The above organization, which has achieved a more than local reputation as accomplished choralists, have in prepararion Handel's " Joshua " which they propose to perform on the 11th of March in the Public Hall. Anent the works of Handel and their influence on the music of England, a distinguished musician recently uttered what was considered a disparaging remark, and which it is needless to say, was not allowed to pass un- challenged. From the numerous rejoinders, the following extracts will be of interest and weight en account of the ability and influence of those giving utterance to them. Dr. Spark said "The influence of Handel's music on the minds and artistic aspirations of the English nation can- not be overrated. God bless Handel! I say, and may his divine music never cease to have its rightful influ- ence in this or any other of the English speaking coun- tries." Professor Prout "I am one of the most ardent enthusiasts for Handel now living. I have made a special study of his workz for nearly 40 years, and I love them now even more than I did in my youth." The late Sir Joseph Barnby, Principal of the Guildhall School of Music and conductor of the Royal Choral Society, said. "From the time when Handel was in the flesh to the present moment, there is scarcely a composer —if there be one at all—who can be said to have exercised so beneficial an influence upon music in England." It is certain that in addition to the intrinsic merit of the music, the subjects treated in his sacred works being such as appealed to the sympa- thies of the people have enhanced their popularity. The Messiah," treating as it does of the most prominent fea- ture of belief by Christians is justly regarded as the greatest of all oratorios. " Joshua " was Handel's four- teenth oratorio, and was composed in the year 1747. The subject of the oratorio is the conquest of Canaan by the children of Israel under Joshua. They begin by celebrating their miraculous passage over the river Jor- dan. Then Joshua receives his commission from an angel to attack Jericho. A librettist of that period could not be expected to exclude love, even from a sacred theme, and the interval of preparation for the siege is naturally occupied by a love scene between Othniel, a youthful Hebrew warrior, and Achsah, the daughter of Caleb. The taking of Jericho (the walls falling down at the blast of the trumpets), is then described in a warlike March and grand Chorus. Next follows the celebration of the Passover, interrupted by the news of the defeat of the Israelites before Ai, and the consequent return of Joshua to the:war. He with his army rescues the Gibeen- ites from the Amorites, and the miracle of the sun and moon being stayed in their course is celebrated in a vividly descriptive chorus. There is a pause now in the national struggle: the praises of Joshua are sung, and the portion of land promised by Moses is allotted to Caleb. To make his inheritance complete, the town of Debir must be taken. This is achieved by Othniel, who receives praises from J. shua and the people, and the hand of Achsah from her father Caleb. The principal vocalists at the forthcoming performance will be : Madame Goodall, the renowned Yorkshire prima donna: Miss Minnie Cullis (formerly member of this choir). win- ner of the Sainton Dolby Scholarship and Medallist Royal Academy of Music ; Mr. Tom Child, the famous tenor ; and Mr. A. S. Kinnel, the eminent basso.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>RUNCORN CO OPERATIVE SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN CO OPERATIVE SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>TEA MEETING AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>About 250 persons were present at the annual tea meeting and entertainment in connection with the Wid- nes branches of the Runcorn Co-operative Society, Ltd., which took place in Lacy-street schools on Wednesday evening. The tables were presided over by Mesdames Hartles, Dean, G. Anderson, F. Anderson, Law, Lloyd, Lloyd, Truscott, Williams, Brown, Saunders, Blinston, Holroyd, Pearson, and Francis, Misses Clarke, Rogerson, Powell and Roach. Owing to the una- voidable absence of Mr. Betney through illness, the after proceedings were presided over by Mr. W. E. Dudley, of Runcorn. There were also on the platform : —Messrs. Bowyer, Moores, Riley, Stubbs. Dean, Hartles, and Cozier (members of the committee), and Mr. W. Upton (secretary), and Mr. W. M. Morris (manager). The Chairman, in a few introductory remarks, expressed regret at the absence of Mr. Betney, remark- ing that he was a pioneer of the movement. He thought they could congratulate themselves on that successful gathering which augured well for the future of the Co-operative movement in Widnes. (Hear, hear.) An interesting programme was submitted and much appreciated. The vocalists were Miss Cozier, Mrs. Coupe, Messrs. Thomas Johnson, H. Johnson, J. L. Withington and Cropper. Mr. Anderson's performance on the violin was much enjoyed, and Messrs. Cropper, Tallon, Haynes and VVatkin evoked much laughter by their comic sketch entitled " The demon phonograph." Mr. T. Saunders recited. The accompanist during the evening was Mrs. Morris. During an interval Mr. Upton (secretary) delivered a short address, stating that the sales for the year 1895 amounted to £88,472, which showed that the society had held its own in the way of trade. The share contri- butions during the year were £9.322, and the total amount of share capital was £42,834. He thought it was a noble effort on the part of the members to lay by something in case of sickness or other emergency. There was also a penny bank, in which the children of co-operators had deposited during the year £559 7s. 81. —(applause)—and the total amount to their credit was £1,711 4s. 101 He was glad to say tnat the franchise had been given to Widnes, and that they now stood on an equal footing with Runcorn, as far as representation was concerned. The amount paid in dividend and interest was £14,545 18c 11d., and the society had also advanced to members to buy their own houses £12,027. He was pleased to see that this matter was being taken up in Parliament, and he thought that the returns sent up by the co-opera- tive societies had had something to do with the matter. They had invested in land, buildings, and fixtures £14,280, and a reserve fund of £2,545. All honour was due to those men who had given the best years of their life to the movement, not for self interest, but "Each for all and all for each." (Loud applause.) Mr. Riley proposed a vote of thanks to the waiters and artistes. -Mr. Cozier secondei, and it was agreed so un- animously.—Mr. Paulson responded on behalf of the ladies, and proposed a vote of thanks to the Chairman. —The Chairman responded.-1 pleasant evening con- cluded with the singing of the" National Anthem."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL COUNCIL AND THE</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL COUNCIL AND THE</p>
        <p>NEW BILL.</p>
        <p>A special meeting of the Runcorn Rural Council was held in the Boardroom at the Workhouse, Dutton, on Tuesday. Mr. J. Mclntyre, J.P., occupied the chair,and there were also present—Messrs. Aaron, Pilling, Walling- ton, Weir, Wright, Darbyshire, Woodward. Bratherton, ' Heywood, Williams, Mounfield, Roylance, Lowe,and Mr. Ashton (clerk.) The meeting was for the special pur- pose of adopting a petition for presentation in Parlia- ment opposing the Manchester Ship Canal Bill, the chief object of which is the closing of the tidal openings of the canal. It will be remembered that at the last meeting of the C.)uncil a report was presented by Messrs. Beloe and Priest, of Liverpool, on the effecc the closing of the openings would have upon the district,and they strongly advised the Council to oppose the Bill. The Clerk hav- ing read the petition—the objections being based on the report of the engineers alluded to—it was unanimously approved that the seal of the Board be affixed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>SAD DEATH OF A FORMER WESTON</head>
        <p>SAD DEATH OF A FORMER WESTON</p>
        <p>POINT MAN.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday Mr. John Evans, coroner, held an inquest at Aberystwitt, tAniching the death of John Elutes (38),a native ot Weston Point near Runcorn, who died on Sun- day afternoon from injuries caused by the kick of a horse. Enoch Davies, a farm servant, said that he recognised the body as that of a man whom he found lying under the manger and between the two horses in the stables at Penlona on Saturday morning. He told the man to go out, and went to a barn. About two hours afterwards he was passing the barn, when he saw the deceased doubled up with pain, and the latter said he believed one of the horses had trodden upon him. He then walked away,but was all the time doubled up with pain. Witness did not attempt to help him. T 1 Coroner : Why couldn't you have suggested that he should wait a little while until you went to inquire at the house. Witness : Well there are so many knocking about that it is bard to believe them.. He had a battle of whisky with him. From the other information given it seems the deceased walked in the direction of Abery- stwth for a considerable distance, and all this time he was suffering the most acute pain. He was picked up on the side Of the road where he had gone to lie down and taken in a trap to the infirmary, where he died from Peritonitis, which the doctor said might have been Caused by a kick. A verdict in accordance with the medical evidence was returned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>RUNCORN ANT) WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN ANT) WIDNES.</p>
        <p>THE WORK OF THE DISTRICT NURSE.</p>
        <p>From January 25th to February 25th, Nurse Bucken- ham visited 182 cases, and paid 57 occasional visits, making a total of 239, _ _ _</p>
        <p>THE REV. CANON WOOD'b HEALTH.</p>
        <p>In a letter to his parishioners in the " Parish Magazine," for March, writing from Naples, the Rev. Canon Wood says "I think I am a good deal better, and have lately been feeling stronger. The rest of us are all well."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>VOLUNTARY SCHOOLS.</head>
        <p>VOLUNTARY SCHOOLS.</p>
        <p>In Runcorn the petition, which is to be presented to Parliament in favour of relief to Voluntary Schools, and which has been sent throughout the length and breadth of the country by the National Society, has been exten- sively signed. PRESENTATION TO MISS JAMESON.</p>
        <p>The members of the ladies sewing class connected with St. Michael's Church assembled at the residence of Mr. P. Speakman, J.P., C.C., The Mount,near Runcorn, on Monday, for the purpose of making a presentation to Miss Jameson on her approaching departure from the town. The presentation on behalf of the subscribers in a few appropriate remarks to which Miss Jameson suit- ably responded, heartily thanking the donors. GIRLS' BEEIIIVE SEWING CLASS.</p>
        <p>This class which is held in connection with the Green- 1 way-road Primitive Methodist Sunday School, had tea and entertainment in the schoolroom on Monday evening. The meeting which succeeded the tea was presided over by Mr. T. Reynolds who delivered a short address. Master H. Cotterell very creditably performed violin solos, and received an encore. The vocalists were Misses Wilson, A. Davies, and Hitchen. Messrs. Pilgrim, A. Shingler, and John Stubbs. Bath the younger and older members of the Beehive contributed action songs, and also a cantata entitled "Mr. Nobody." The performance was much enjoyed, and at the close a vote of thanks to those who had taken part was passed. PARISH CHURCH RECREATION CLUB. On Monday the first general meeting of the Runcorn Parish Church Recreation Club, took place. The Rev. H. B. Firth was in the chair. There was a capital attend- ance. Certain rules which had been drawn up at a pre- liminary meeting were discussed and confirmed. The election of officers was then proeeeded with—resulting as follows : treasurer, Mr. John Littler ; secretary, Mr. H. Clare; committee : Messrs. Boyers,Dadlev, and Key, representing the Sunday school teachers ; Messrs. Bur- rows, T. Clare, Hitchmough, Miller, Shaw, and Walsh. Judging from the enthusiasm, which was manifested at the meeting, there is every reason to hope that the club will be the means of providing healthy and rational forms of amusement.</p>
        <p>Vor NAME BROOCHES, soli silverd name brooches 10id. (any name) Government stamped. Presents or Articles for yourself, you may visit DEBERT*S, 10, CHURCH-STREET. LIVERPOOL, where you can get hundreds of articles such as Watches, Clocks, Jewellery. Electro-Plate, Cutlery. Purses, Pocket Knives, etc.. at wholesale prices. See advertisement (Illustrated Chron- gracu Watch, 6s. lid.. etc.), on page 7 f this Paper. -Advt</p>
        <p>TEACHERS' REUNION AT RUNCORN. There were about sixty persons present at this rennion held under the ausptces of the local branch of the National Union Teachers' Association. in Holy Triaity School on Friday evening. The room had been very tastefully decorated. A varied programme was gone through. Dancing was zealously engaged in to music provided by Mr. Ravenscroft, and the intervals were made pleasant by songs contributed by Messrs. Crosby. Nickson and Birkett ; a duet by Messrs. Burton and Done; and a pianoforte duet by the Misses Horsfield. The duties of M.C. were ably performed by Mr. Bmwn. The arrangements were made and carried out by Misses Stubt-s, Tyrie and Clayden, and Mr. Cookson, who was to be congratulated upon the success attending the</p>
        <p>gathering. THE SALARIES OF RUNCORN SCHOOL BOARD TEACLIERS. The question of adopting a scale of salaries for head teachers and assistants employed by the Runcorn School Board was again under consideration at a meeting of the School Management Committee, an Tuesday even- ing. Considering the increased staff of the Board, and the frequent applications for increase of salary, it has been found necessary to adopt some fixed scale,as is the case in other towns. The salaries decided upon by other Boards were considered, and a scale was drawn up. This will be recommended for adoption by the Board at its next meeting on Tuesday. We understand that it is based practically on the Reale in vogue at Widnes. Head masters will commence at a salary of £l4O, and will re- ceive yearly advances of £5 until the maximum of £2OO is reached; It is understood that there will be a per- centage allowed if the excellent merit grant is awarded by Her Majesty's Inspectors. SUDDEN DEATH OF MRS. D. SPEAKMAN. Mr. R. Dobson, deputy-coroner, held an inquest at Runcorn Police Court on Tuesday morning, relative to the death of Annie Elizabeth Speakman, 64 years of age, wife of the late David Speakman. Mrs. Speakman had for a number of years resided in Surrey-st.,and on Sun- day morning she was found dead on the sofa. The first witness called was Samuel Sherlock Walker, brother of the deeeased,and who lived with her. He stated that on Saturday night the deceased had a hearty supper. He went to bed immediately afterwards. About 8 o'clock next morning he heard someone knocking at the door, and as no one answered,he went downstairs. He found the deceased on the sofa. She was then dead, and the body was cold. She had been in the habit of sleeping on the sofa for some time past. He had expected that the deceased would die suddenly on account of the com- plaint from whieh she suffered.—Elizabeth Ellis, wife of William Henry Ellis, living in Surrey-st.. stated that she last saw deceased alive on Saturday night. She came to witness's house about 10 o'clock and said she felt starved. She sat by the fire and had some tea, and re- turned home about 11 o'clock.—Dr. Robinson said the deceased was never a strong woman, and was often ail- ing. He was not surprised at her sudden demise. There was no suspicion of want of nourishment. She died from natural causes, attributable to tatty degeneration of the heart. A verdict in accordance with the medical testimony wet; returned. SEAMEN'S INSTITUTE, RUNCORN. A rather unique entertainment was provided in this institute on Tuesday evening, when the entire pro- gramme was provided by representatives of the family of Mr. Shaw, missionary for the Mersey Mission to Sea- men at Runcorn. Three generations of the familiar name of Shaw took part in the entertainment which proved to be of a most enjoyable character. The proceedings were presided over by the venerable Mr. Shaw, mission- ary, and after he had made a few appropriate remarks, a chorus, entitled "Ash Grove," was given by the mem- bers of the family. The following items were then given: —Song, "The British Lion," Mr. Philip Shaw; sone, " Grace Darling," Mrs. Richard ; musical duet, " Only to see her face again," Mr. and Mrs. William jun. ; re- citation, " Good fruits," Miss Florrie ; whistling solo, "Medley on popular airs," Mr. John ; recitation. "The child and the swallow," Master Willie ; trio," Home of the Brave." Edith. Florrie, and Lily ; duet, " Play- mates," Mr. and Mrs. William jun ; song, " Our sailors on the sea," Mrs. Richard • recitation, " What is the Seamen's Institute," Mr. William jun. ; duet, " Silver threads among the gold," Mr. and Mrs. William jun. ; song, " The Spanish Cavalier," Mr. Philip; song," Roses underneath the snow," Mrs. Richard ; song, "Nug- gets," Mr. John; recitation, "The Stowaway," Mr. William jun. ; song, " When the pilot takes the com- mand," M. Richard; song, "When there's love at home," Mrs. William jun.; song, " Harbour Lights," Mr. John. The hearty applause which greeted each item testified to the appreciation of the audience. Mrs. Richard Shaw and Mr. John Shaw received encores. A pleasant evening's entertainment concluded with the usual vote of thanks.</p>
        <p>THE ALLEGED THEFT OF ROPE. John Hedgecock and James Rowley were charged before Messrs. F. J. Norman and E. Liaison, at Runcorn Police Court on Friday morning, with stealing 90 fathi,ms of six inch manilla hawser, value £5 from the barque M. and E. Cann, at Weston Point, the property of Patrick Fitzgerald, master.—Patrick Fitz zerald repeated his evidence as to seeing Hedge3ock coiling the rope into a barrow, with the intention of taking it away. Hedge- cock on being questioned told him that Rowley had en- gaged him to wheel it away. Witness did not see Rowley. The mate named Ryan was in the galley at the erne, and said he knew nothing about the matter. —William Savage. lock-tenter. also gave evidence.— P.C. Bancroft spoke to apprehending Hedgecock who on being charged said "I am innocent, I know nothing about it, I was engaged to wheel the rope tin to Runcorn by Rowley." On Thursday morning about eleven o'clock. Rowley came to the Police Station. and witness took him to the cells where he charged both prisoners together. Rowley made A statement in which he said he had bought rope and iron from the mate of the vessel in question. The captain had been preseet when he bought rope from the mate before. The mate told him on Wednesday that there would be some ropes for him,and he (Rowley) agreed to give ss. a cwt. for it. When they were getting the rope on shore, the captain called out to the mate, who told him (Rowley) to go on shore, and he would make it all right. When he heard that the police wanted him he came to the Police Station. John Hedgecock was eng.ged by him to wheel the rope to the marine stores and did not know anything f the transaction between himself and the mate.— Rowley made a statement to the bench similar to the one given to the police,and stated that he had been going to the docks twenty years buying ropes. He had previously employed Hedgecock.—The magistrates considered there was some doubt in the case and gave the prisoners the benefit of it, acquitting both of them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>RA.LION PARISH COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>RA.LION PARISH COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>A meeting of this Council was held on Tuesday even- in the girls' schoolroom, Halton. The Rev. G. D. Wray occupied the chair, and there were also present, Messrs. J. Stokes, McCullock. Birch, Hughes, Barrow Jeacock, Looker, Ackerley. Astbury and Woodier (clerk}.</p>
        <p>FIN&amp;NCE. The Clerk reported that a precept of £2O would shortly be paid to the credit of the Council, and he asked that an additional precept of £lO be asked for. They would then be able to pay off all accounts, and have a small balance in hand.</p>
        <p>THE COST OF REPAIRING ROADS. Mr. McCullock asked what was the cost of repairing the roads in the parish ?—The Clerk reported that the work undertaken by the Council, viz., the repair of the Hanging Rock-road, the Pinfold, and the pathway to Runcorn amounted to £lB 45. 3d.—Mr. Looker said he would like to know what had been the cost of the Parish Council.—The Chairman said a statement of accounts would be submitted at the next meeting. The Clerk was not yet able to complete the accounts.—Mr. Looker; We have not overstepped the 3d. rate limit ?—The Chairman said, excluding the Cemetery expenses, the Parish Council had really not expended a 2d. rate.—The Clerk stated that he would present a detailed statement at the next meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>AN UNFENCED PIT.</head>
        <p>AN UNFENCED PIT.</p>
        <p>Mr. John Rowland, clerk to the Parish Council of Clifton, wrote stating that a resolution had been passed that a letter be sent to the Halton Parish Council callitag their attention to the extremely dangerous condition of the footway running through the fields and which led from, Halton Lodge to Weston the particular point being the pit which was unfenced. He asked that the Council should not relax their efforts until the matter was remedied.—Mr. Stokes stated that the roadway was extremely dangerous, especially at night, in consequence of a deep pit near the road being unfenced. He thought it better to have it remedied at once, and not wait until someone had been drowned there.—Mr. McCullock pro- posed that the Clerk again write Mr. Linaker on the matter.—Mr. Ackerley seconded, and it was agreed to unanimously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>TIM REMOVAL OF THE PINFOLD.</head>
        <p>TIM REMOVAL OF THE PINFOLD.</p>
        <p>A letter was received from Messrs. Robert Davies and Co., of Warrington, who wrote to the Clerk stating that he would recollect in the year 1889, having some corres- pondence with them relative to the removal of the pin- fold at Dalton. They had now a complaint from Mr. T. C. Evans, the agent of Sir Richard Brooke, that in the removal of the pinfold the Parish Council destroyed the fence between the Duchy of Lancaster land and Sir Richard Brooke's,and he called upon the Duchy to make good the fence, which of course they were not liable to do if the Parish Council destroyed it.—A long discussion took place with regard to this matter, It was pointed out that the Council had not touched the fence,but in making an improvement in the road they had raised it to the height of the fence which surrounded the Vicar's garden. The Chairman said there was no doubt that the Cot-incil had no right to bury a fence, and by raising the road to the height of the former fence,it had ceased to be one. It was ultimately decided that Mr. Evans should be asked to meet the Council in order that they might ascertain his wants in the matter. THE PARISH MEETING.</p>
        <p>The Chairman announced that the parish meeting had been fixed by the Local Government Board for March 9th. Nomination papers could be received from the Ist March to that date. This concluded the business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>R.S.P.C.A. PROSECUTIONS AT FRODSHAM.</head>
        <p>R.S.P.C.A. PROSECUTIONS AT FRODSHAM.</p>
        <p>A BATCH OF CASES. At Frodsham Petty Sessions on Wednesday before Mr.l Charles Reynolds (chairman), and Mr. T. Rigby, a num- ber of cases were heasd elf charges of cruelty to animals, at the instigation of Inspector Bilson, on behalf of the Royal Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Animals. Mr. A. Browne, solicitor, Warrington, prosecuted on be- half of the society. Great interest was manifested in the proceedings, and the court was crewded. The first case heard was that in which John Warbur- ton, farmer, Kingsley, was charged with causing cruelty to a cow by conveying it in such a nsanner as to cause it unnecessary suffering at Newton on the 23rd January. —Mr. Burton, solicitor. Runcorn, defended in this case, —Mr. Browne said it was not an ordinary ease of cruelty to an animal. The proceedings were taken under the 12th section of the act which stated that if any person should carry or cause to be carried any animal in a vehicle so as to subject it to unnecessary Pain' he should be liable to a penalty not exceeding £3. He was in- structed to state that the present was a had case. It appeared that Mr. Warburton on the day in question took a cow to Chester fair to be sold. Not succeeding in selling it, he sent it back again to Kingsley in a float, in charge of his son and nephew.—Sergeant Clays and P.C. Weatherby, on its passing through Newton, were struck by the appearance of the cart,and they requested those in charge to stop it. They found the cow in a fearful state. It had been tied up so badly that Sergeant Clays threatened to lock up the young men in charge. Its head was tied down, the left hon was fixed between the stanchion and its nose turned to the side between the bare of the cart. The nose was in such a dreadful swollen condition that the officers had great difficulty in getting it from between the bars. There were wounds on the face, and blood and saliva were dropping from the nose and mouth. The defendant ad- mitted that he tied up the cow, stating that it ran wild in Chester but that it went like a calf into the float. The animal was quite exhausted.—Sergeant Clays corrobor- ated Mr. Browne's statement. The cow was so tied up that it was unable to move from the position it was in. —P.C. Weatherby also gave evidence for the prosecu- tion.—lnspector Bilson stated that he visited the defers- ant's farm the following day, and he made a careful ex- amination of the cow in question. He found a contused Wound over the left eye, and there was a lacerated 'wound, two inches long, on the jaw. There were !alse several abrasions on the legs. He spoke to the defendant about it, and pointed out to him the wounds and cuts, but defendant could not say how they were caused. He stated that he had done the best he could for the cow.—P.C. Price gave evidence as to being pre- sent with the inspector at the interview. The defendant stated that he tied the cow up in the float himself.—For the defence, Mr. Burton stated that the defendant had been a farmer in the district a great number of years, without a single complaint being made against him. On the 23rd of January the defendant went to Chester with the cow, but as soon as it got to the fair it became wild. It was a valuable cow, and just on calcine. In order to get it home he placed it in a float, and that it might be properly tied he purchased a halter. A number of wit- nesses would prove that the animal's head was fastened ' to the centre of the cart, and in order that it might move its head freely, six inches of rope were allowed. It was sent home in charge of two experienced men, and when stopped by the police, they showed them the slacknessl of rope on one side. The animal was well covered, and every precaution taken for the safety of the animal. It came out of the float very quietly, and showed no martra upon it that could be defined as cruelty.— Evidence in corroboration of Mr. Burton's statement was given by Henry Warburton, son of the defendant ; William Warburton, Manley ; George Spruce, cattle dealer, Kingsley ; William Holland ; John Wright ; and the Vicar of Kingsley. It was stated that the animal was properly and humanely tied in the cart at Chester, and that it ‘-‘ jammed" its head in the position stated by the officers of its own accord. Its head was pulled out of this position several times on the way but it re- peatedly placed it there.—John Henry Bate, veterinary surgeon, Sutton, spoke to examining the bow. He found no wounds on the animal, but a little hair had beers scratched off the hind leg, and from over the left eye. He was told that the animal fell down in Chester.—The Chairman said they had decided to dismiss the case.—The decision was received with applause and clapping of hands by the people in court. —The Chairman said if there was the slightest demon- stration of that kind again he should order the public out of court. Such demonstrations of that kind were lamentable, and he was considerably surprised and astonished at what had taken place. Their difficulties were quite enough without anything of that sort. George Davies, Church Field Farm, Frodsham, was summoned for cruelty to a horse by causing it to be worked in an unfit condition, on the 10th February.— Browne stated that Inspector Bilson was in Frodsham on the date named, when he saw a boy driving the de- fendant's horse, attached to a milk float. He examined it, and found it in a very low condition. It was suffering from rinebone on both fore legs, and the tendons were badly heated and con- tracted.—lnspector Bilson and John Dixon, veterinary surgeon, Warrington,spoke to the condition of the horse, stating that it was absolute cruelty to work it, and that lit was only fit for the knackers.—A fine of Is. and costs (£1 us. 6d.) was imposed. James Andrews, Newton, near Frcelsham,was charged with causing a horse to be worked on the 29th January, and John Harrison, Main-street, Frodsham, was sum- moned for working the same.—The magistrates said they had a difficulty to decide in this case, as the evidence given was so conflicting. They had decided to see the animal themselves. The animal was accordingly brought and after the magistrates had seen the horse, they dis- missed the case on their return to the Court. Thomas Ainsworth, Marsh Farm, Frodsham, was charged with cruelty to a horse bv causing it to be worked in an unfit condition on the 29th January ; and Frederick Ainsworth was summoned for working the same.—The owner was fined ss. and Ll is. costs, and the driver is. and Bs. 6.1. costs. lie AP.</p>
        <p>BEAUTY AND PURITY go hand in hand. They are the foundation of health and happiness ; health, because of pure blood ; happiness, because of clean skin. Thou- sands of useful lives have been embittered by distressing humours. CUTICURA remedies are the greatest of skin purifierg as well as blood purifiers. Because of their peculiar action on the pores they are successful in pre- venting and curing all forms of skin, scalp, and blood humours.when the best physicians and all other methods fail. Safe, innocent, and effective, they especially appeal to mothers and children. Their use at all zeasons insures a clean skin and pure blood, as well as sound bodily health.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <p>2bbtrtistment not elassifitt.</p>
        <p>WANTED. Girl about 15 ; good home ; 38.-1 FoRDEs-ST., Openshaw, Manchester.</p>
        <p>TRlSHMEN.—Baautiful fresh SHAMROCK for Patrick's Day, 3d. a bunch free ; money with order ; pound bag full 23. free.— Miss Gtviixs, Mitchelstown, County Cork, Ireland.</p>
        <p>BOROUGH. of WARRING lON INCORPORATED PERMANENT BUILDING SOCIISTY.—The ANNUAL MEETING will be held at my office, Legh-street, Warring- ton, on WEDNESDAY evening, 'MARCH 4th, 1896, at 7 o'clock. THOMAS SUTTON, Manager.</p>
        <p>NUINR BEHR and WINli EtOUSH, ingoing £65 ; position ix suburbs.—MuLEr, 25, Lime-street. Liverpool.</p>
        <p>e-,AMESON'S RIDE. _ -</p>
        <p>Authentic records of this Historical Event now reach England, and the Crisis is virtually over. Peace and plenty again rule through the Golden Territory of the Iransvaal, and the act of Dr. Jameson links this modern Hero inalienably to ; one of the Historic Incidents of the Century. No Infringement of our neutrality can be urged against us in laying bare to the Liverpool public a subtect of strong local interest and advantage. A rumour was current previously, but is only con- firmed with Dr. Jim's" arrival, that his brave band of Volunteers had become possessed (prior to the Raid) of the redoubtable clothing manufactured especially for South Africa by Messrs. HENOCHS- BERG ELLIS, UNIVERSITY HOUSE, Islington, Liverpool, and that the Natural Wool Under- wear famous for suzli climates, shipped to the South African Repuplic, as well as to the Char- tered Company's Territory, by Messrs. HENOCHS- BERG &amp; ELLIS, was worn by the Doctor's helps during their trying time. The Government Serges (Passing full teat for shades, dyes, and strength) are kept in stock at University House. Messrs. HENOCHSBERG it ELLIS are prepared to under- take and execute ORDERS for GENTLEMEN'S SUITS for 505., tor YOUTHS' SUITS 355., and for BOYS' SUITS 255., from the exact materials, of which they now hold a kood selection, as the contract for the Natal Government is in course of completion by this well-known firm.</p>
        <p>CHRISTIAN ENDEAVOUR, WARRINGTON DISTRICT. CONVENTION AT WIDNES. On Wednesday, the Warrington and District Associa- tion of Christian Endeavour Societies held its Third Annual Convention in Deacon-road Baptist Church, Widnes. There was a good attendance at the afternoon conference, 14 societies being represented. Rev. E. T. Reed (President of the District) conducted the devotional meeting and was supported on the platform by Rev. J. B. Morgan (chairman of British National C.E. Council;,, Mr.A.Mounfield (hon. secretary), Mr. T. Barton Davies, (secretary. Cheshire Union), Miss Lloyd, Rev. W. J. Kirkland,Rev.W.Holroyd,Rev.J. Austin, Mr. J. R. Free- man, (Dunstaple), and Mr. E. Lloyd Jones (pastor of the church). Mr. J. Wilson (Runcorn) took the chair for the after- noon conference, expressing in doing so his good wishes for the success of the conference. He hoped the meetings would give impetus to the work which he knew was being done, and would tend to spiritual upbuilding.— Mr. T. Barton Davies (secretary of the Chester Union) delivered an address on" C.E. and Evangelistic work." He remarked that it was a matter of deep concern that a large part of the world was uninfluenced by Jesus Christ and that multitudes were existing without realis- ing the true object of life. Christian Endeavour had bees called to change the minority into a majority. It was essentially a soul saving force. That the organiza- tion was adapted for the winning of men there could be no doubt for some‘Boo,ooo through its agency had already been joined to the church. Christian endeavour afforded the fittest opportunities for Evanzelistic work. It brought theta into touch with young life, with young people at the most susceptible period of life. If they would imitate their Divine Lord in the care He showed for individuals they would reap a rich harvest. Each society should lay itself out for soul-winning, and each district should in the strength that is born of union make efforts in this direction. At the great Boston Convention the Endeavourers held outdoor and indoor meetings in every corner of the city. He would urge them to a similar concern for those around them.—Mise Lloyd, of Chester, next delivered an address on " Our junior meetings." She recommended the greatest variety in the children's meetings. Junior C.E. was for the chil- dren, of the children, and by the children, and every- thing should be made to contrinute their highest good. —A free parliament followed in which several junior leaders took part.—The concluding address was deli- vereJ by the Rev. J. B. Morgan, Chairman of the Na- tional C.E. Council, on "The mission of Christian Endeavour." He referred his hearers to the fundamental principles ef the movement, emphasising the tact that they existed to foster and promote earnest Christian life. It had been remarked that Christianity was God's programme for the making of a better world. Their mission was identified with that of their Lord, and they stood for out and out loyalty and consecration to Him. They believed him to be as worthy of their enthusiasm as any science or art or recreation. If it was worth while to endure hardships to reach the North Pole it was surely not too much to give life and service to one who had given up all for them. They would render noble service if they could impress their generation with that fact.—Other brief addresses were given by those pre- sent and the audience adjourned to the schoolroom where an excellent tea was provided.—At 8.30 Miss Lloyd, of Chester, conducted a model prayer meeting in the chapel, Mr. Williams, of Chester, giving an address on "The Pledge." The meeting was much enjoyed by the large audience who attended. EVENING PUBLIC MEETING.' The Evening Meeting commenced at 7.30 and was largely attended. Mr. J. R. Freeman of Dunstaple took the chair and was supported by a number of local and other ministers. Rev. W. E. 'Lead, read the scripture and offered prayer.—The Chairman in his address con- gratulated the Association upon its convention and ex- pressed the pleasure it gave him to come to Widnes. The town was certainly not as black as it had been painted. (Laughter.) As one of the first Endeavourers in the country he rejoiced at the spread of the principles and wished them every success.—Mr. Arthur Mounfield, secretary.next gave the annual report and after referring to the loss the Association had sustained by the removal of the Rev. D. W. Roberts stated that they had 14 societies affiliated with the Associa- tion with a membership of 584. Of these 384- were active members 133 associate and 67 honorary. He then proceeded to give a detailed account of the work which was being carried on by the various societies. Among the many activities of the societies, services to the poor and young were conspicuous. A Christmas tea to 600 poor children was given by one society, and another a free supper to 200, and another a breakfast to a number of poor children. A large number of articles of clothing had been distributed. There had been an increased interest in the work of the junior Christian Endeavour, there being now nine junior and one children's society. At a united meeting which had been held during the year, a presentation had been made to the Rev. D. W. Roberts. He concluded by an- nouncing that the officials for the ensuing year were : President, the Rev. E. T. Reed ; vice president, Miss E. M. Allatt ; secretary, Mr. A. Mounfield ; treasurer, .Mr. John S. Green. A solo," 0 rest in the Lord," was given by Miss Wilson, of Runcorn, after which the Rev. H. J. Taylor of Southport, addressed the meeting on " Christian Endeavour in its relation to the nation." He remarked that two great facts were noticeable in the life of England. The first was the increasing public interest in the affairs of the nation, and the second, the rising up in the church of the army of young people who. were destined to be the force in the shaping of national life. It seemed to him very clear that politicians would never deal with the crying evils around them unless they were forced, and that the real governing power was in the hands cf the men and women. A great responsibility was resting upon the church, and he believed that Christian Endeavour in shaping the young life within the church was doing much to shape the nation of the future. Christian Endea- vour insisted as the foremost,the best of all government, was self-government. He urged upon his hearers the duty of taking a definite stand with regard to gambling, impure literature, and the drink traffic.—After the sing- ing of the Christian Endeavour hymn, the Rev. J. B. Morgan, president of the British National Council, ad- dressed the meeting upon " Past, present, and future of Christian Endeavour." He said that the Christian En- deavour;movernent at its inception gave small promise of reaching its present proportions. Although it was only 15 years old, it had now 2,600,000 adherents connected with 446.000 societies of all denominations. It might interest them to know that a new Christian Endeavour Society, was formed every 68 minutes, and every day between 700 and 800 new members were added to the ranks. In England alone, there were four new societies formed every day, and for the second time, the badge banner which was new given by the internat'onal society to the country or state which could show the largest increase in Christian Endeavour Societies had come to England. This showed that the move- ment was growing more rapidly in England than any other country. Referring to the future of the Endea- vour, he said that as long as they believed in the Holy Ghost, their movement would be a power and a blessing. The badge banner referred to by Mr. I%ll'gan was hung in the chapel and excited much interest. It was made of innumerable badges on eachof.wh!ch is printed the name of a country, state, or principality, and the blending af the colours made it most picturesque. At the close of the public meeting, a consecration meezing was held, conducted, by the Rev. J. B. Morgan at which the following societies comprising the Warring- ten district responded: Newton-le-Willows Congrega- tional, Warrineton Emmanuel Reformed Church of England, ‘Varringtor:i Golborne-st. Baptist, Warrington Friars' Green, Warringtonßrick-st. Mission, Latchford Baptist, Warringtpn Wycliffe Congregational, Runcorn Primitive Methodist, Earlestown Primitive Methodist. Runcorn United Methodist Free Church, Warrington Bank Quay. Mission, Lymm Primitive Methodist, Earles- town Baptist, and Widnes Deacon-road. The proceedings closed with the Mizpah benediction,</p>
      </div>
