      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>MINOR CASES. • WARRINGTON BOROUGH POLICE COURT. FRlDAY.—Before Alderman Harrison (chArmart), Mr. S. Wright,.Dr. Gornall, and Councillor Henry Roberts. USING THREATB.—Richard Moss-st., was charged with using malicious threats to Mary Graham, on the 16th February.—Mrs. Graham, 311, Winwick-road, said that on Sunday night she was in a neighbour's house when defendant came in and made ea accusation against her. He then threatened to "do for her."—Corroborative evidence was lacking and the defendant was dismissed. A BLOOD-CURDLING TALE. —Mary Graham. Winwick-street, was charged with assaulting and beating Mary Ann White- head, 305, Winwick-road, on the 17th February. Mr. Davies appeared for defendant, and pleaded not guilty. Mrs. Whitehead said she was in her own house on the day in question when Mrs. Graham came in. They had some words and Mrs. Graham struck her in the face. She then picked up the lamp and was going to throw it at her, when com- plainant took it from her. Defendant thea took a table knife and threatened to rip her up. Complainant cut her hand in taking it from her. Her (complainant's) husband tried to put defendant out when she turned raund and bit his arm.— Complainant admitted to Mr. Davies that defendant had lent her food and articles to pawn, and, in fact, had been kind to her. Ellen Whtteheai, com- plainant's daughter. having given evidence, the case was dismissed. Mr. Whitehead was charged in a cross-summons with assaulting Mrs. Graham.—Mrs. Graham said defendant hail struck her with his fist and knocke i her on the floor. He knelt on her chest while she was, on the floar.—Joseph Graham, sm of Mrs. Graham, corroborated and as Mrs. Whitehead's daughter had admitted in the other case that her father was kneeling on Mrs Graham. the magistrates considered that the evidence for the defence had proved the case for the prosecution, and fined defendant 10s. and costs and allowed Mr. Davies an advocate's fee o 10s. 6d. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Mortnay.—Before the Mayor (in the chair) Alderman Fran- comb, Mr. F. Monks,and Councillor Pier. ,oint, CARRIED AWAY BY HIS FEELINGS.-1b&amp; Ellis, 9, Napier- It.. a .ell-sp)ken woman. was charged with being drunk and disnrderly in Narier-et. on Eunday night —Aftel P.C. Wood had p oved the case the defendant's hwband came forward and said that defendant had been cmtinually drink- ing. He added that there would not be a woman better off than her if she would keep off that d 4 drink.—The Chief Constable: Come I Come ! You are in court, and be- fore a bench of magistrates."—Defendant " I apologise, your Worships. Mv feelings got the better of me."—De- fendant was fined ss. and costs. _</p>
        <p>BAITING A POLICEMAN—Thomas Jones. Golberne-street, was charged with having on the 16V, February unlawfully Incited a dog to bite P.C. William Pllnington ; he was also charged with bring drink end disorderly In Golborne-street on the same date.—P C. Pennington deposed that at, 2-20 on Sunday morning he found the defendant lying drunk across the ptvemen', in Golborne-st. he had a coiiie dog with him and when witness apnntached him, defendant deliber4tely set the dog upon him. The animal rushed at witness and bit his finver.. failing however to draw blood. Witness then kept it off with his lamp until defendant's brother came up and took ilia dog away. Defen- dant gave his wr-nrr n,me and arldro=; at first.—Winiam Jones, brof her to defendant said thaf he came to vive evidence on his behalf, but as he admitted falvin, had drink himself and that he did mt see the mnstatle.he was not, heard.—De- fendant was fined 20s. ad costs for he first offence and ss. and costs for the second (21 190 in all. DOG WITIfoUT A LicE.I.E —Tames Clou2h, 15 Morris- Court Church-street, was fined ss. for having an unlicensed dog in his nossession on the 18th February- DRASTIC TREATMENT OF A " Romut,"--Thorn as Baguiev. 17, Scotland-road, a men with a bad reeo-d was cisarged with breaking the glass panel in the door of the " Theatre 'Cavern". Scotland-road on Saturday night.—A barman said that prisoner came in the rmblic-honse about 10 o'clock on datur- day night and on being refused liqt,o- he commenced bang- ing the swing door and continued until he b-oke the gl.ss. Witness called to him to desist but he did not do so. They never served prisoner there whether d unk or sob.sr.— P.C. Newsy Droved the arrest of defendant.. and as he was only "let off " from a charge of loitering on promising to leave the town, the magistrates fined him 40s. and costs on that charge, or in def,ult one month's hard labour, and for break- ing the window committ,d him to gaol for two months. .CHlNcvEirs ON FIRE. —Joseph F:sher. King-street, and Wien Stuart. 37 Factory-lane. were each fined is. for allow- ing their chimneys to be on fire on divers dates. TIIESDAY.—Bef,,re Mr. R. Garnett (chairman), Mr. B. J. _ _ _ _ _ _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>Guest. and Dr. T. S. Smith.</head>
        <p>Guest. and Dr. T. S. Smith.</p>
        <p>SCHOOL CASES.—The followlng persons were dealt with for net sending' their children reuularly to school : - D.lOl Briars, William-street. fined 2s. 6d : Riehard Tranter, 2. Warburton- street, 54.; John J, Caldwell. 18, taira-street, boy John sent to the' Olin" ; John Waddirgton. 16, St. James-street. 59.; William Ryder. 20. Brwoo i-street. 2s. 6d.; Peter Cartledge, 22, Turner-street. 23.6 d.; Thomaq Grlffiths, 4, Brierley-street, boy Thomas ordered to be birched. THIIRSDLY.—Before Mr. S. Ckandley (chairman), and Mr. B.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>Wright</head>
        <p>Wright</p>
        <p>TEMPORARY AUTHORITY.—Gfecrqe Harrison was granted temporary authority to sell at 2, School-st-eet, in lieu of George Parr. Ax.vrsty.st TRETt. FROM THE ISAMU:T.—Vary Jane Frost. a midtile-a.,ed and respeetableoooking womin. residing at 175, Winwick-road. was charged with stealing from the Market Place on Wednesday aft ernoem. a quantity of wool, (Iry soap, blackt ad, and brass nails, value Is. the property of Thomas Johnson, stall-holder.—Defendant pleaded guitty.—The Chief Cons'-able said that this was an exceedingly melancholy: case as the woman had hitherto borne an unblemished character. For some time stall-hold, rs in the Market had been put to considerable loss by people going and pilfering their goods while they were otherwise engaged and in con,equence Detective - Sergeant Monaghan and other officers had been told off t , watch the stalls. On Wednesday afternoon betwaen 3 and 4 o'olock the defen- dent was seen by the officers to go to the prosecutor's stall and quietly pick up some articles of those enumerated and put them in her basket. Thinking she was unobserve I she re- turned several times and took something. and once to give colour to her visits she bought a pennyworth of candles. She was finally arrested and accused of stealing, and the at once collapsed, and said she did not know what made her do it; but she had taken some whiskey. Several articles had been found at her house but of such a general description as to make it impossibleto identify them. Defendant's husband had informed him that some three or four years ago her daughter had died and since then she had not been mentally quire the same.—The magistrates warned the defendant not to offend again, and said they were going to put a stop to these paltry thefts.—She was fined 10s. including costs.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN PE rTY SESSTONS.</p>
        <p>MoNnar.—Before Mr. A. Ttrmas (chairman). and Mr. T</p>
        <p>Rigby. FAILING, TO REPORT SWINE FEVER.—John Hulse, general dealer. Regent-st., Runcorn, was summoned for having on the 14th February, neglected to report a case of swine fever that had occurred on his premises.—Defendant pleaded guilty. —P.S. Lee stated that on Saturday the 15th inst.. the defen- dant came to the Police Station, and stated that he wanted witness to come to his place and see some pigs which were 111. Witness went to the pigcoates in Public Hall yard, and found four pigs in an advanced state of swine fever. The defend Int said he had had four other pigs which he had sold to Mrs. Goban, of Canal-st.' Witness reported the matter to the Board of Agriculture. On making inquiries witness as- certained that the de'endant had had nine pigs, and not eight as he had stated. This was afterwards acknowledged by the defendant, who stated that one Was SO ill that he knocked it on the head, and afterwards buried it. The animal had been exhumed and a past-mortem examination held. The animal</p>
