      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, FEBEUARY 29, 1896.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, FEBEUARY 29, 1896.</p>
        <p>had been *Differing from swine fever, and had since been cremated at the gas works. There were other cases of swine fever in the neighbourhood. Mrs. Goban who had bought , four of the pigs, complained of their condition to the de'en- dant on the date he roported the matter to the police, and told him that if he didn't report it to the police, she would do so. The defendant exchanged one of Mrs. Goban's pigs and afterwards took it to another place where a eve of swine fever had since been reported.—Mrs. Goban gave confirma- tory evidence.—Superintendent Okell said it was a very bad case. The buying of the pig might have been the means of spreading the disease.—Sergeant Lee stated that nine pigs had been destroyed in the town affected with swine fever. The defendant's place had been thoroughly disinfected. All the pigs were destroyed at the Gas Works.—Defendant stated that he reported the case as soon as he knew what it was. It was .the first time he bad ev,r dealt in pigs. He had only had them three weeks, and was not aware that he had to report cases of disease.—Sergeant Lee stated that the de- fendant was supplied with a leaflet giving instructions with respect to animal diseases.—A fine of S'.s and costs or in de- fault fourteen days' imprisonment was imposed.—Themoney WAS pad. OBSTRuCTION BY A FISH DEALER.—George Duckett, fish dealer. Church-st., was summoned For obstructing the foot- path in Church-St. by causing a crowd of persons to assemble thereon on February 15th.—P.S. Brewster stated that about 10 p.m. on the date named he saw a crowd around the de- fendant's fish stall. The defendant was standing in the door- way shoutiug fish. There wee a crowd of about 30 on the footpath, and two men of the'defenclanes were shouting as hard as they could trying to attract people to the stall. He had previously reported the defendant, but Superintendent Atherton would not prosecute him, and sent Inspector Hicks to caution him about the matter. Sergeant lee had alsa cautioned him. He watched the defendant for about a quar- ter of an honr, and he then went to him and told him that he should summons him.—P.C. Ellis gave corroborative evi- dence. stating that the defendant's son was outside the shop shouting.—Defendant said he should take a summons out against both officers for perjury—P.C. Gregory also gave evi- i deuce. and in answer to a question by the Superintendent, witness said it was a fast that the defendant had told him that it was no use reporting him as Superintendent Atherton would not take any action.—Sergeant Brewster stated that the police books would show that the matter had been re- ported previously, but Superendent 'Atherton never allowed it to go on.—The defendant said Superintendent Ather- ton had never shown him any favours. tie complained of trade being bad, and if any obstrection had taken place it was due to the police. About nine o'clock on Saturday night he made a practice of seliing the remainder of his fish at half- price,and generally a number of poorer customers assembled. They hag never been i terfered with until Sergeant Brewster came into the town, and he (Mr. Duckett) was the only tradesman in town who was not allowed to unpack a crate In the town. On the night in question. the sergeant did not tell him he should take out a summons, and did not attempt to move the people away from the shop door. There was plenty of ro )m for people to pass on the parapet.—Corrobora- tive evidence was given by Henry Weaver, labourer, Higher Runcorn; Edwin Price, 39, Egerton-street; Thomas Woods, 36. Coepe...-str,et. and John Duckett. —The magistrates said they considered the case proved, and imposed a fine of 10s. and costs.</p>
        <p>FOOTBALL PLATING IN THE STREET.—William Igo (14), John Garry (16), John Thompson (15), William Jones (15), John Delaney (15), all living in the vicinity of Brunswick- street were each fined the costs, 4s. 6d. for playing football in that street on the 12th February, at 7.45 p.m..—Sergeant Lee and Gill proved the charge. DOG OWNERi FlNT,D.—William Williamson, South-road, Weston Point, and John Ostle, 62, Winwick-street, Warrin 4- ton, were fined 'a. ineiuding costs for allowing their dogs to be at large without collar or muzzle, The former case was proved by Sergeant Lee, and the latter by P.C. Wylie, PROFANE LANGUAGIC.—EIIen Moran, who had been pre- viously 6iealt with six times in that court, was eharged with using obscene and profane language in Mersey-street on the sth Decemb: r.—P.C. Wright spoke to the defendant using most disgusting language.—She was fined ICs. and costs, or 14 days ,mprisonment.—She chose the alternative. IRREGULAR ATTENDANCE AT Scoot.—lhe following par- ents were each fined 6d. and 4s. 6d. costs for neglecting to cause their children to attend scbeal regularly. :—William Ellis. Surrey-street. James Claford, Stonehills, Peter Johnson Gilbert-street. Mr. T. Bowyer, school visitor,,prosecuted.</p>
        <p>DARESBURY PETTY SESSIONS. TCrESDAY.—Defore Messrs. H. Neild (in the chair), Sohn White; J. Mclntyre. and (3. if. Broadbent.</p>
        <p>A CASE or SWINE FEVER.—William Hill. if Preston Brook, was charged with having, b?tween the 23rd and 28th January, committed an offence against the Contagious Dis- eases (A.nimals) Act of 1878 by having failed to notify to the police a case of swine fever —P.S. Griffiths stated that on the 25th inst. he visited the premises of the defendant, and there found one pig witich was dead. He could see from what it had been suffering, and on as ing defendant why he did not report the matter he did not receive a satisfactory reply. Previoua to that date another pig belonging to defendant had died. A veterinary surgeon had been consulted with refer- ence to Ihe case, ad he had stated that it was swine fever.— Defend nt pleaded ignorance of the law, and stated that was, only a small pig.—A fine of 2s. 6d. and costs was Imposed.</p>
        <p>A SLEEPY DRIVER.—For having been asleep whilst in charge of a horse and lurry. at Newton-by-Dareshury on the 10th inst., Samuel Jones was fined 2s. 6d. and eCt3tB. DOG AT LARGE —George Fletcher was fined 3s. 6d. ger having allowed his dog to run at large unmutzled, on the 12th inst. at Appleton.</p>
        <p>FRODSHAM PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.—Before Mr. Charles Reynolds (chairman), and</p>
        <p>Mr. T. Rigby</p>
        <p>SUPPLYING DRINK TO A DRUNKEN MAN.—James Hancock. licensee o. the Drovers' Arms, Frodsham, was summoned for having on the 11th January sold drink to a drunken person, and David Aston. labourer, Church-street, was summoned for being drunk on the premises.—Mr. Churton, solicitor, Chester. defended Hancock.—Sergeant Clays stated that at a quarter to four on the date named he visited the Drovers' Arms, and found the defen.lant, Aston, standing in the passage drinking from a pint jug of beer. He was drunk.—P.C. Price gave corroborative evidence. He saw Aston go staggering out of the house. Arthur Basnett, butcher. Frodsham, spoke to seeing Aston going towards Hancock's house, He saw him stumbling about. He made some had remarks to a lady and she had to leave the parapet.—James Clarke. Five Crosses, and John B. Foster also spoke to seeing Aston drunk.—ln defence, Mr. Churton stated that Hancock had conducted the house for about 18 years without a complaint being made. Hancock was fined kl and costs or one month's imprison- ment, and Aston ss. and costs or fourteen days' imprisonment with hard labour. The license was not endorsed. DRUNK ON LICENiED PREMISES.—George Ainswo?th was charged with having on the 12th inst., been drunk and refus- ing to quit the licensed premises of George Itogerson,Railway Inn—Sergeant Clays stated that about twelve o'clock on the date named, be saw a crowd of people at the Railway Inn. Hew-oat there and found the defendant drunk. The landlord refused to serve him with drink, and in consequence the defendant reused to leave the house.—He was fined bs. and costs. Bs. 61., or in del ult seven days' imprisonment. DOGS AT LARGE.—The following persons were each fined 51. including costs for allowing their doge to be at large:—; Thomas Fletcher, Netherton ; William Hough, Frodsham John Albert Bate, Frodsham Lordship; and Henry George Linaker. Friodsham.</p>
        <p>THE " INTOLERABLE STR&amp;lN."—There are "intolerable strains " of a worse kind than that heard of in the con- troversy over the Voluntary Schools. Mr. W. Glew, of Riplingbam, Brough. Yorkshire, told the story of such a strain to the " Hull Daily News." For a long time, he said, he had suffered dreadfully from pains in the body, sn agonising singing in the ears, and tightness of the, chest. After goosl medical treatment, he went to a I specialist, who prescribed for him, but could trace no benefit. His malady grew worse, and he was often unable to stir from home, SO fearful was the agony. His medical attendants thought that he must have strained himself, but Mr. Glew was unable to agree with this opinion. One day, in a newspaper, his eye fell upon a remarkable cure effected by Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People in a case very similar to his own. He pur- chased a box of Dr. Williams' Pink Pills, and after tak- irig contents found a marvellous improvement in his infirmity. Delighted, he persevered with the treatment, and, after taking the contents of seven boxes, he found himself a newly-made man. He is now as nimble as a ycung man. Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People have unrivalled success as a tonic. They cure palpita- tions, and disorders arising from impoverished blood, as amen-lie, pale complexion, weakness, loss of appetite, shortness of breath, female weakness, hysteria,paralysis, locomotor ataxy, rheumatism and sciatica. Genuine only with full name, Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People ; sold by chemists and by Dr. Williams' Medicine Company, 46, Holborn-viaduct, London, E.C., at 2s. 9d. a cox, or six for 133. 9d. They invigorate the system 'after overwork, worry, and indiscretions of living.</p>
        <p>Y.M.C.A. P.S.A.</p>
        <p>Sunday was an "open day," of the above and the meeting was enlivened by the presence of a good many ladies, in aldition to a large number of mem- bers. Mr. Kent, the president, occupied the chair, and gave a good and cheerful address, whilst the speaker for the day, Mr. James Evans, was listened to very attentively. His address was very pithy and was much enjoyed. Mr. Davies contributed two oboe solos in capital style, and Mr. Peters and Mr. Gregson also took part in the meeting.</p>
        <p>QUEEN'S THEATRE OF VARIETIES. Good audiences have attended this now popular place of entertainment, each night this week, and there has been a goad programme submitted for their delectation. The Angelo troupe of acrobats came in for much appreciation, their difficult and well per- formed feats winning them rounds of applause. The three Lesters in performing a humorous sketch "He would have a pantomime" elicited roars of laughter from the audience whi:e the Wards, also three in number gave proficient displays of dancing,boxing, and comic singing. Mr. Dick Geldard and Mr. Walter Gordon contributed several enjoyable items to the programme, including a sketch, provoking continual mirth, entitled "Tally-ho l" and the Sisters Twibell made a favourable impression by their singing and burlesque acting ; Master Dolph Leavitt and " Our Jack ' were also applauded for their exploits, the latter on the tight wire.</p>
        <p>RUPTURES.-REYNOLDS' Patent Imperceptible , Indiarubber Stem Pail TRUSS. Suitable in all eases. perfect Fit Rnsured. Write or call for particulars.EYNOLDS, 10, Slater-tteet f',&gt;Nv doors Bold-street), Liverpool. Open on Saturday until 7 advt</p>
        <p>Butterworth and Myers led to no definite result, and Jacques being penalised for standing off-side at "scrum," Houghton made a futile attempt at goal. Butterworth retired hurt for a brief space, and on his return a pass- ing movement nearly ended in his being across the " border," but not quite, and the gams ended : Run- corn, cne try ; St. Helens, nothing. Considering that Runcorn's forwards had the worst of it three-parts of the game, the result might well have been different,but, although the " Saints " backs passed quickly and accur- ately, they were held in check by the opposing iine, who tackled timely and resolutelyeand allowed little progress to be made. Of course, the home forwards being beaten, the halves did not shine with the refulgende of Cross and M'Lees, but Robinson's affection for hugging the ball was too palpable for excuse, and ho would have done far better to have parted to the capable men behind him. Houghton has played better many a time, and Foulkes was far from equal to his reputation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>CRICKETERS should scquu e the "Pocket Diary and Companion," issued by the well-known Sports Manu- facturers, GEottoE G. BussEr &amp; Co„ Peckham, London. All the information Cricketers require is found therein, as well as the usual Diary, yet it will go in the waistcoat pocket. Leather bound, gilt edges, 6d. ; postage, 1-1. If you are not satisfied, G. G. B. &amp; Co. will return the price.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON Y. LIVERSEDGE.</p>
        <p>Warrington played the first of five successive home matches on Saturday, and won it in a most decisive fashion, and Liversedge have had good cause to remem- ber their first visit to Wilderspool. True they were two men short, but there is very little doubt that had the two Fnen been present a substantial victory would have been scored by Warrington. Last year, Liversedge were the champions of the Yorkshire League competition,and were, and are noted for their open style of play. They have some excellent men in their team,the most notable being Wood, who has played half back for England,and he is the Yorkshire half back of the present. The team have had a most erratic career in the Northern League this season. They lost the first five matches off the reel, and were thus the bottom club in the competition for a time. They then pulled themselves together, and for about two months, they did not taste defeat, with the result that: they got within measurable distance of the top. In fact they have been among the top three. Since then, they have played in and out, and have dropped considerably, and they now rank 13th. They were somewhat lucky to beat Warrington by a goal and a try to a penalty goal at Liversedge, as the goal was only obtained in the last two minutes, and a victory at War- ington was looked forward to as a moral certainty. When it was found that Fearnley, their full beck, and Ernest Parkin, one of their wing threequarters, had "gone amissing," and left no word behind them on Saturday, the match was almost a foregone conclusion. The day was extremely fine, and as a result some 7,000 people assembled to witness the match. The following were the teams representing the two clubs : Warrington : Heesom, full back ; Lawless, Barber, Cross and Carey, threequarters ; Bate and Higginbot- torn, half-backs ; Nevins, Burrows, Fairhurst, Saunders, Swit t, Hill, Taylor, and Andrews, forwards. Liversedge : Ben. Sharpe, full back ; N. Sharpe, Wommersley,S. Priestley and Jenkinson.three-quarters ; Wood and P. Priestley, half-backs ; N. Parkin, E. Ford, Smith, Howarth, Medley and Steele, forwards. Hill kicked off in the face of a gale of wind, and there being no adequate return, the Warrington forwards got possession of the ball, and after a brief check, Taylor, Saunders, and Hill, smartly dribbled the ball into the visiting quarters, and finally it was worked over the line, and Carey rushing up, scored before two minutes' play. Swift failed to kick a goal, owing to the strong wind. After the kick out, which the wind carried far down the field, tWood got possession and passed to B. Sharpe, who made a long kick down the field only to be checkmated by Nevins. Barber eased the pressure which the Tykes were commencing to put upon the homesters, but Wood made a grand kick down the field which Fleesem failed to hold, and it is marvel- lous however Steele failed to get over. It was simply over eagerness which caused him to run past the ball, otherwEe he had a clear run in. The Warrington three- quarters were being well fed by Bate and Higginbattom, and half way was reached by Lawless, but Sharpe again got in a huge kick which Carey made his mark in res- ponse to. He kicked in the centre of the field, and Womevley being handy, he had time to steady himself and drop a really fine goal. After the kick out, War- rington were penalised near half way, but N. Sharpe made but poor use from the kick, and Barber and Cross took the play back to centre. Wood was again to the fore by a dashing run and kick. and Smith was sadly at fault in over running the ball which went in touch and thus missing a score. From the line out which followed, Taylor toads a good run to the quarter flag, and a dribble by Fligginbottem, followed by a run by Barber took play to the opposite end, and B. Sharpe was pena- lised for not playing the ball. Heesom was entrusted with the place kick, and the ball was careering towards the goal posts when a sudden strong gust of wind blew it back again, and it fell on one side of the goal posts instead of over them. Only a barren minor followed this attempt. Ben Sharpe was now conceded a penalty kick, and he quickly sent the Warringtonians right to the opposite end of the field, where Cross again kicked in touch at the centre, and Carey getting possession had a long run which had no tangible result, and shortly afterwards the whistle blew for half time with the SCOr3 Liversedge one dropped goal, Warrington one try. After the restart. Warnngt„on with the wind, quickly asserted their superiority, and it was soon seen that barring the vilest of luck, Warrington were in for a de. cisive victory. There was but a poor return to Lawless's _ _ _ .</p>
        <p>exchange alter the kick off, i-nd the first scrimmage took place in dangerous proximity to the Liversedge line. Bate got possession and passed to Cross, who at- tempted to drop at goal, but could not get the ball to rise. He had similar luck a moment later. He was evi- dently " off " for dropping goals. The Warrington for- wards now came down the field in a b aly,and Swift got over at the corner, but the point was disallowed on the ground that the touch line had been crossed. This was only a delay of a minute in the scoring being re-opened, for Bate passed well to Barber after the line out and scrimmage, and the latter transferred to Lawless, who promptly popped the ball over the cross bar, amid such enthusiasm that the boards:which form the barrier in one part of the field gave way. Fortunately,no one was hurt, and the boards were made to answer during the remainder of the match. The seers of Lawless seemed to completely demoralise the Liversodge team, and for the next ten minutes it almost rained goals and tries. After the kick off,Warrington were speedily at the Li . ersedge line again, and Bate finding the way over barred, took his opponents by surprise I.)y a back kick along the goal hne,and the ball travelling to Lawless,the latter secured another try, thus scoring seven points in a minute. Taylor failed to convert the difficult place. Again from the drop out, the Warrington players swarmed along the Yorkshire line,and Bate getting possession.he threw Wood off his guard completely by shamming a pass,and then had a clear run in, Nevins increasing the try to a goal. The game had scarcely been commenced again, when Higginbottons rushed over and scored another try, which Barber only just missed increasing to a goal. From this drop out, Liversedge, chiefly by means of Wood, and N. Sharpe, got to half-way, where Carey bad a try to drop a goal, but had the very hard luck of see- ing the ball strike the goalposta and rebound into play. Some stubborn scrimmages now ensued, and after the break up of one cf these, the ball came to Nevin's hands, and although he was promptly tackled, he had safely sent the ball over the cross bar before he was quite floored. After the kick-off,Liversedge reached the home quarter flag, for the first time this half, and Wood had he been well supported, might have caused a score to be registered for them. There were,however,not sufficient men.for the visitors had one man hurt immediately after Bate's try, and thus had to finish the game with twelve men. Consequently, the homesters were not long en the defensive, and Barber roused the enthusiasm of the spectators by a grand run along touch but had the mis- fortune to be bowled over, just as he had got within a abort distance of the line. A little desultory play now took place, until Bate passed to Barber and he to Cross, the latter of whom rounded Jenkinson and scored another try, the fifth of the afternoon. Swift raised this point to a goal, and immediately afterwards the whistle blew, Warrington winning by two placed goals, two dropped goals, three tries (27 points), to Liversedge one dropped goal (4 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>RUNCORN v. ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN v. ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>St. Helens met Runcorn at the Cheshire town on Saturday, in the presence of about 1,000 spectators. Runcorn had a strong team. Warder had recovered from his accident in the county match, and resumed his place. In the first few minutes Runcorn displayed splendid passing,which possibly might have had a tangi- ble result, but—oh ! that but—Myers, who was the last' link in the chain in each instance, failed to do his duty, and scrummages in the visitors' quarters were the only ' result. Jacques intercepted a pass from J. Faulkner, and after a good run,p•intad judiciously, and Houghton, making a feeble return into touch, gave the "Saints "a footing. For some time the home team was in danger, and a penalty kick brought no relief, Docherty again putting his men on the attack. Although the" Saints " forwards heeled out repeatedly, and the halves passed quickly to their threequarters, the Runcorn men were keenly alert,and every effort to advance was nipped in the bud. Farmer followed up a judicious punt by Faulkner, and picking up cleverly eluded several men, gave to Walker' aho dashed away and scored in a good position, but the point was not improved. From now until the half-time whistle blew St. Helens seldom made much headway, thanks to the alert- ness of Butterworth and Warder, the latter saving twice in most daripg fashion. Cross re-started against the wind, and after desul- tory play,Runcorn were twice penalised for off-side play with no advantage to their opponents. Neither team appeared able to get beyond the centre-line. Butter- worth and Warder, for Runcorn.and Cross and Jacques, for St. Helens, being most prominent in the movements made, while the tackling was of so severe a character in several instances that the referee interfered with an ad- monition. Brilliant passing between Cross, Sudlow,and IJacques ended in the latter kicking, and a seers seemed imminent when Warder effectually intervened, and the attack was repulsed, Houghton afterwards removing the venue ty a fine kick. A passing display by W. Faulkner.</p>
        <p>TO PRESERVE YOUR HAIR use common sense:- don't rnin it by sousing it with cold water or starve it by neglect: all living things require nnurishment. and pure grease is un- doubtedly the best nourisher of the hair: for more than a century.</p>
        <p>ROWLAND'S MACASSAR OIL has been considered the best preaerver of the hair ever used : it prevents it falling off or turning grey, r.-:mnves ecurf am. promotes a luxuriant growth: for children It is unsurpassed : also in a golden colour for fair hair. Bottles 35 6d., 76., 108 6d. (equal to 4 small). Row:and's Macassar 011.—Nourishes the hair.</p>
        <p>Rowland's Manssar 011.—Prevents baldness. Ask anywhere for Rowland's Macassar Oil, of 20, Hatton Garden, London, and avoid cheap, worthless imitations.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>WIDNES v. HUDDERSFIELD.</head>
        <p>WIDNES v. HUDDERSFIELD.</p>
        <p>This match attracted a crowd of 3,000 to the Widnes ground. The home club placed their full team in the field, and Huddersfield were also strongly represented. Widnes played against a stiff wind in the first half, and the Yorkshiremen soon made things lively for the home defence. Boothroyd was prominent in a spirited rush, whicls Drummond and Brennan just accounted for. The home backs made a couple of attempts to break away, but in vain, the Huddersfield for wards using their feet to great advantage. They made several dangerous in- cursions into the Widnes "25," but thanks to some good defensive play by the home backs their attempts were frustrated. An attack on the Huddersfield citadel was repulsed, but the combination on both sides left much to be desired, and play of the scrambling order hovered about the centre line for a long time. Then the forward dribbles of the visitors' again troubled the Widnes de- fence, who were kept on the alert for a long time. Drummond and Rispan were instrumental in the half- line being reached, and France took the ball from the toes of three or four ef the home forwards. Just near the interval the Huddersfield front rank dashed up the field, and some smart passing ended in Smith evading Brennan and running over. The place kick was a failure, and half-time was immediately called, with the Yorkshiremen three points in front. In the first nsinute of the second half the home lines had a narrow escape, Wimpenny getting as far as the corner flag before Brennan sent him into touch. The Widnes issen now warmed to their work, and attacked strongly. After a couple of rebuffs, the equalising point came, Drummond dashing over from a line-out. Brennan's attempt at goal was a failure. Renewing the attack Rispan put in a long kick, and France dally- ing over the line, Foster dashed up and scored ; but he was injured by falling against the palisade, and the ' game was susps,ncled. Oldfield, of Huddersfield, was also hurt, and he took no further part in the game ; while, after Barber had landed a goal from the place, Smith, who had been injured earlier in the game, found it impossible to continue playing, and also retired. With only thirteen men Huddersfield played up manfully, and Boothroyd had very hard lines after a brilliant run. The home team had now a big advantage forward, and after some capital play by Rispan, Nevin added another try. Brennan's goal kick was a failure, and a few minutes later the whistle sounded, leaving Widnes vic- torious by one goal and two tries (II points) to one try (three points).—There was not that difference between the teams which the score would indicate. Huddersfield did quite as much attacking as the home team, and a drawn game would have represented the merits of the two teams. In the open Huddersfield were speedier than the Widnes team, who did not quite play up to form, but the state of the ground and ball had no doubt a lot to do with this. The dribbles of the Huddersfield for- wards were always dangerous, and it speaks volumes for the defensive powers of the Widnes hacks that they withstood the numerous rashes of the Yorkshiremen in the early part of the game. For the winning team Brennan (who himself deserves most credit for the vic- tory) Rispan, Nevitt, Drummond, and Barber among the backs, and Emery, Forster, and Donnelly forward, played the hest game ; while for Huddersfield, Booth- royd, Smith, Wimpenny, Lodge, and Sutcliffe were the most prominent._</p>
        <p>LEIGH A. V. WARRINGTON A.—The above match was played on the Mather-lane ground, Leigh, on Saturday. Warrington had the wind in their faces the first half. For a short time after the kick off the play was near the centre until Isherwood got away and passed to Foster, who, however, was sur- rounded. Leigh returned the ball and Spruce saved well sending the ball to the other end of the field. War- rington attempted to make use of the chance but Bow- den picked up and ran across the field, passing to the forwards who took play to centre. Several scrimmages took place and the ball was taken gradually to the War- rington line. The visitors, however, exerted themselves to their utmost and Leigh had to go back to the centre. Off-side play by Warrington gave Leigh three free kicks in close succession,from Warrington's quarter line, but the ball was cleveriy stopped each time. Warrington then pressed,and Bowden just saved in time with a long kick, the forwards following up and dribbling close to the Warrinton line. Foster pickektna the ball and ran to the centre, where it remained for the few remaining minutes of the first half. On changing ends the visitors rushed the ball to the home quarters and pressed, play being exciting for some time, but Spillane, Hindle, and Rowson, got hold of the ball and it was soon close to their opponents posts. Several vigorous attacks were made, but Spruce, Foster, and Scott repelled them. A scrimmage took place within a couple of feet of the Warrington line, but O'Brien' § Savage, and several Df the Warrington forwards slowly but surely force 4 the ball away, taking it later on to the home section. Lively play ensued for a few minutes, until Leigh got the advantage and pressed Warrington strongly, and the visitors had plenty of work to do to check the desperate rushes of the homesters. Time was called and the game ended in a pointless draw. The muddy and greasy state of the field spoiled any good running, but some short sharp dashes were occasionally made by both sides. The following men were on the field :—Warrington: Full back, Spruce; threequarters. Hockenhall, Scott, Isherwood, and Forster; half-backs, O'Brien and Clark; forwards, Savage, Hughes, Dakin, Freeth, Berry, Bayley. Wilcox, and Sudlow. Leigh: Full back, Bowden; three-qaarters, Price, Hatton, Spillane, and Mcilarty ; half-backs, Shovelton and Officer; forwards, Roberts, Hindle,Arrowsmith,Phcenix, Hemmings, Rawson, Ridyard. and Maltby. RUNCORN V. ST. HELENS "A " TEAMS.—The return match between these teams took place at St. Helens on Saturday before a meagre attendance. The first en- counter at Runcorn ended in a decisive win for the Cestrians by eight tries to nil. Since then, however, St. Helens have greatly strengthened their 2nd string, with the inclusion of a lot of new blood, whilst the bulk of the Recreation players have thrown their lot in with them. Runcorn were short of Richardson and Urmson, other- wise they had a fairly representative team, whilst the other side was at its strongest, Little, AVM, and Fyldes being included. Runcorn won the toss, and played with a strong wind behind them, and it was soon evident that the game was going to be keenly fought. Runcorn pressed with the aid of the wind, and several bouts of passing almost ended in a score, Nickson on one occasion passing when all opposition was passed. However, a few minutes later some capital exchanges between Percival and Nickson ended in the latter getting over, Lightfoot failing to add the major points. The game after this was a most rough and tumble exhibition, and at times was most brutal, the referee seeming to lose all control over the players, and fully three parts of the game was little better than a general fight. A most miserable and wretched exhibition ended—Runcorn, one try; St. Helens nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>NORTHERN _RUGBY UNION.</head>
        <p>NORTHERN _RUGBY UNION.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Committee of-thie-iiiiiOn was held on Tuesday night :at the Spread Eagle Hotel, :Manchester, Mr. J. E. Warren (Warrington) in the chair. The fol- lowing transfers were granted : —F. W. Hampson, War- rington to Widnes ; B. Booth, Bradford to Stockport ; J. Cotton,Wigan to Widnes. Apolica;ion was made by the Rochdale Hornets to be allowed to play home matches on the Rochdale Athletic ground during the suspension of their own ground. The committee refused the applica- tion. Permits to play were granted :—J. Ward, V. Wildsmith. and D. Murray with Broughton Rangers; R. Hockenhnll and N. Freeth with Warrington; M. Walsh with Widnes ; J. Halliwell and H. Dickinson with Wigan; J. Dyer with Runcorn; F. Arrowsmith with Tyldesley ; and J. Edwards, J. Callon, and R. Isherwood with Leigh. The rules as drawn up by the sub-commit- tee at Huddersfield for next season's competition wore approved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>ALTRINCHAM GRAMMAR SCHOOL V. enon.--Ilayed at Altrincham. During the first half Croft, although play- ing against the wind, did most of the pressing, scoring three goals in quick succession. After the interval the school played a better game,but was unable to penetrate the sound defence of the visitors' backs. Eventually a fast but very pleasant game ended in a victory for Croft. Goals for Croft were scored by E. Hartley (two) and A. Hartley. Final score: Croft, 3 goals ; Altrincham Grammar School, 0.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>LOCAL CASES AT LIVERPOOL SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL CASES AT LIVERPOOL SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>At the Liverpool County Sessions. yesterday, James Donoughue (27), collier, for haying,at New- ton, on the 17th January. feloniously stolen a piece of beef ,the property of Eliza Clarke, was committed for two months' imprisonment with hard labour.— James Walsh (36), labourer, for having, at Martins- croft, on the 17th December, stolen a shawl, the property of Mary Gibbin, was sent to gaol for six mouths, and David Clay (18) labourer, for having at Widnes, on the 23rd .Tanualy, stolen an overcoat, the property of Richard Leather, for three months.</p>
        <p>A MINUTE WITH YOU PLEASE</p>
        <p>Sufferers from Gravel, Lumbago, Piles, Pains in the Back Dropsy, Wind and Water Complaint, Diseases of Kidneys BlPdder Stone, Sciatica, Rheumatism and Gout will find a positive Cure in Holdroyd's Gravel Pills. Try a small box and If not satisfied your money will be returned. Prices is. 14. d. Of all Chemists, or post free for 12 stamps, from HOLDROYD'S Medical Hall, Checkheaten, Yorks. Don't be put off, if you cannot get them write to the Proprietorj andLe box will be seat next post.—Advt</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND</p>
        <p>ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTS XLL.</p>
        <p>Warrington are at the present time having a rare time of it in the Northern Laagu3, and if they continue their present good form, they will occupy quite a res- pectable position both in the combined League, and also the Lancashire section. They have gained eight points in the last four matches, one of which was away, and this is exactly one-third of the total number of points they have gained during the whole of the season. Not only have they gained points, but they have improved their average greatly, and have now scored more than a hundred points. During the past four matches they have scored 41 points to 10.</p>
        <p>The mitch was, of course, practically a foregone conclusion on Saturday when Li versedge only arrived with thirteen men, but there was such a strong gale blowing that there was the off-chance that with the wind behind them, Liversedge might manage to score, and with a bit of luck keep Warrington from doing the same when sides were changed. This, however,did not take place, for Warrington actually scored against the wind,and when the wind was in their favour they piled on an immense score. It is a pity that Warrington could not have scored eight more points, for then they would have passed the record score in the Northern Union. This was made on December 28 by Manningham, who scored 34 points against Widnes. Warrington were only seven bohind this, and they have, therefore, put on the second best score.</p>
        <p>Liversed.9,:e have had a very erratic career in the Northern League. They ara noted scorers, and they have experienced some heavy scoring againt them. They are among the five who have put on over 2100 points this season,;!and yet their score average is anything but a good one,and the points against them are more than the vast majority of the clubs in the League. They are evidently as poor at defence as they are good at attack. Wood, their county and ex-international half back is a host in himself, and he was the most prominent of their players on Saturday.</p>
        <p>Among the Warringtonians, Heesom did not play so well as usull and he did not show the same confidence that he did a short time ago. The three quarters were in fine fettle, and Barber had the vilest of luck in being the only one of them who did not score. He was as near as possible several times, but the fates were against him. Lawless scored seven points, and Cross and Carey each scored three, so that 13 out of the 27 points came from the three quarter line.</p>
        <p>Each half back scored a try, ad that of Bate was really tbe cleverest of the afternoon, for it was quite a treat to see how he dodged over the line by deceiving Wood like he did. Bate's inclusion in the team has strengthened it very much indeed,and it is perhaps more than a coincidence that since he has played with it. War- rington have never lost. In Higginbottom. Bate has a very efficient partner when Foden is hors de combat.</p>
        <p>The eight Warrington forwards had an easy thing; on against the six Liversedge men. Nevins, Taylor, Saunders, and Swift were the more prominent, and Nevins surprised himself and the spectators by both dropping and placing goals. Notwithstanding his long service, he is evidently not done with yet, and there is plenty of football left in him for a long time to come.</p>
        <p>Warrington meet St. Helens to-day, and on form should beat them, for while Warrington have been carry- ing all before them of late, St. Helens have been going backward,and now stand on the same level as Warring- ton, so that should the latter win to-day, they will get ahead of S. Helens, who not long ago were actually among the first five, St. Helens are not very gentle players as witness their game with Runcorn on Saturday when it took the referee all his time to keep the players straight. When Warringqoa visited St. Helens earlier in the season, they had the best of the game all through but lost by a try.</p>
        <p>To represent Warrington at the match to-day, the fol- lowing team has been chosen : Heesom, full back ; Cares', Cross, Barber, and Lawless, three quarters ; Bate and Foden, halves ; Nevins, Burrows, Thornton, Fair- hurst, Saunders, Swift, Hill, and Taylor, forwards.</p>
        <p>The Runcorn team continue their onward career in the Northern League Competition. On Saturday they secured a further two points at the expense of St. Helens. The margin—three points to nil—cannot be considered a formidable one, but St. Helens only won by five points, when the two teams met at an earlier date. The match on Saturday was not a pleasantly contested one, and the splenetic outbursts of brute force were to be regretted. No fewer than four of the visiting players, and one of the home forwards, had to be cautioned for rough play by the referee (Mr. Dillon, of Warrington), who displayed rare firmness throughout the match. Several of the home players—who were much lighter than their opponents—were seriously injured, and if such rough exhibitions as that of Saturday were to be frequent, it is almost certain that the spectators would dwindle in numbers.</p>
        <p>The victory of the home team has enabled them to reach the forsinost place in the Lancashire and Cheshire section of the competition, and their most danterous opponents in preventing them from maintaining this coveted position are Oldham. In the combination,Run- corn continue to hold a high position, shaving the best average of any club, and to-day (Saturday) they will, no doubt, render a good account of themselves when they meet the Manningham team. It is perhaps un- fortunate for the latter that they will have to play with a weakened team owing to the Lancashire and Yorkshire match on the same day.</p>
        <p>Widnes have actually won two matches in succesiion a feat they have previously been quite un,nual to. Huddersfield,who trounced them to the time of 20 points to nil a short time ago, went down on Saturday by eleven points to three. This is quite in accordance with Widnes traditions, and it will not be at all surprising to see them wipe the floor With Manninghaixt who have already beaten them by 34 points to three, when the return match comes off.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>STOCKTON HEA.T.R.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEA.T.R.</p>
        <p>Sr. TrconiLs' Y.M.C.—On Monday evening a pleasant gathering in connection with the above took place in the school, about 75 members being present, when a very handsome tea service was presented to a member of the class. Mr. J. Maddock, v;Tha was married a few days ago in the village church to Mies Sarah Longshaw. Mr. Baldwin,af ter referring to the object of the meeting, expressed a wish that it would be a happy one to all. Mr. Down said it was a pleasure to him to come amongst them, as such good fellowship and brotherly feeling existed. He asked them to keep on in the same good old way, and the class would not only be a blessing to themselves but also a help to their fellows. He remembered Mr. Maddock years ago. He was at that time a member of the class which was then under his care, and he was very glad to and him still a member. He concluded by wishing him and his partner every happiness. (Applause.) Mr. Maddock suitably replied. After a humorous address by Mr. Rose on weddings in general, Mr. Spenseley (secretary) said he hoped the kind and brotherly feeling which pervaded the class would continue, as there was .no doubt that was one great reason why it had met with so much success. The class was growing. almost every Sunday brought fresh faces amongst them, and he was glad to say the old ones were quite content to stay. On the motion of the teacher (Mr. Hallows) a hearty vote of thanks was posed to the managers for their kmndness in lending the use of the school. Mr. S. Spencer proposed a vote of thanks to Mr. Down for his kindness in coming amongst them as one of themselves and for his gener- osity in providing refreshments. This was seconded by Mr. Whitton, and carried. The rest of the evening was spent very happily in various ways. The refreshments were under the care ot Mrs. Clark. At ten o'clock all joined heartily in singing the hymn " Sun of my soul" accompanied by Mr. Down, and the proceedings termin- ated.</p>
        <p>CADBURY'S COCOA</p>
