        <head>LITTLE SANKEY PARISH COUNCIL</head>
        <p>LITTLE SANKEY PARISH COUNCIL</p>
        <p>A meeting of the above ouncil was held in the Wes- leyan schoolroom on Thui, day last week, Mr. R. B. Matthews being in the chair. There were also present 311essrs. B. Grimsh tw, S. Williamson, J. L. Tunstall (clerk), J. Smith (assistant clerk), and G. Thompson (district councillor).</p>
        <p>THE VOTE OF CONLOLENCE WITTE MR. SELBY'S FAMILY. Aletter was read by the Clerk from Mr. J. B. Sc,lbY,of Leigh, in which he said that he was desired by Mrs. 841 by to acknowledge the letter sent from the Council liPon the occasion of Air. Selby's death. He assured the Council of the sincere thanks of Mr. Selby's family for the kind sympathy expressed.</p>
        <p>THE HALTON'S BRIDGE IMPROVEMENT. In reply to the petition of the Council to the County Council asking them to proceed with the improvement of Halton's bridge which lies between the townships of Sankey and Penketh, Mr. F. C. Hulton, clerk to the County Councihreplied saying that the matter would be aid before the Main Roads and Bridges Committee at heir next meeting. A letter was also read from Mr. Jonas Forster, the cier.to tile P:.nketh Parish Council, soliciting the co-operation of the Little Sankey Parish Council with th-m in endeavouring to move the County l&amp;h,,tii cil to improve t ~. road at Halton' id lettereunc th —"llan iWe have alreAdy done so. But I move that e o iibleoeinafektiowle, ed and that the P 6 Benrkegtleja'rribseh —armed that the matter has already had our attention at our last irweting.—Mr. Williams seconded, and the motion was carrikd. THE PROPOSED LIGHTINO OF LITTLE SANKEY. !fisting of Messrs. At the last -- meeting of : ht- Council a committee con. Greenwell, Corradine, and Jackson, was appointed to consider the subject of lighting the bee n held, and the clerk in giving tee has side of Liverpool-road. A meeting of the commitf- Berfoote in the mase the report or Council g slid that the committee was met by * • atzeets£3 ' th° insPector on behalf of the Rural District lighting Llt was ascertained that the annual charge for 110 definite i e lamps—wP. Iver Pool-road would be £2 145., and for back eon 0 th each lan, Was by the there -as more than had been anticipated, As the cost involved in the erec- also infn te decision was arrived it. The committee were Town Clerk of Warrington that , belng obastaeiverY Probability of the borough extensiaosn. sured t tif'd at an early date, and on that being r ,Ivr.e. a deputation upon the question.— The ~,... , he an 'Committee would be prepared to id that that that practically meant that as soon as it was he- Oat, decided that Sankey Bridges Chairman should go into e Eorough ;;;Innimittee , the Town Clerk would advise the Lighting, of the borough at once to take up the light- td question and thoroughly light the place, without taknit,having to make any application to the Rural Ms- vouncil whatever. He therefore moved that a letter thewtit'‘',en. to the Rural District Council withdrawing th.l,l.,PPlication for additional lights. He thought that AN:. “.i.Wn Clerk's suggestion was very satisfactory.— ,keTrrieSa. Williamson seconded the motion, which was</p>
        <p>TtfE-- EXTENSION OF TIIE LONDON AND NORTII-WESTERN</p>
        <p>tk RAILWAY. i The committee which was appointed to inquire into _ Ar a_Nlulta way in VI extensions of the London and North-Western PleY Meadows reported that they attended !..eeting of the Warrington District Highway Board at vir_litichbeeas present Mr.Thompson,thesurveyor,who gave e artetyia.aeleedassaiesdtaanicietthoethe inquiry. Several pla,ns .wer ,zr.,4Pl.de.e:gmittee had to report that the present application footpaths were duly explained. nuie railway committee was practically limited to e th of their main line, and the interests of the "IllhiP would not in any way be injured thereby. The Ch THE BOROUGH EXTENSION. airman said that the Town Clerk suggested that it would be advantageous to present a petition from that Council in favour of a Bill for the extension of the Qgtt°ugh of Warrington. Of course, as they had decidEd h fit terms and entered into agreements to help the 'Corporation, it was simply a matter of form that they -`4oOld help in any way they could.—On the motion of Williamson it was a.,ereed to forward a petition to ointment in favour of the Bill. SANITARY MATTERS. ...The Chairman said that there was one question which :Yas a very SCriOUS one for the locality. At the last meet- bag of the Runcorn District Council he goticed that the I,ever hospital in Warrington w as closed to patients from he rural district which included Sankey Bridges. It 'would be remembered that at the time the smallpox was lc' Preva'ent it was with extreme difficulty that they anaged to get that hospital opened. In consequence thesome dispute between the Rural District Council and 'oe Warrington Corporation, tbe latter had closed the hospital to the Council, and there was now no place to Nivhicila they could send any patients to isolate them. This was a serious matter for a place like Sankey I3ridges. He felt that it was necessary to take some action in the matter and to put such pressure on the Rural District Council as would either open the Warring- hospital again, or have another place provided for any serious outbreak of fever that might occur. It 'Would be in their knowledge that there had been one death. He Vaerefore recommended the Council to send a letter pointing out the circumstances under which theyat .were Placed to the Local Government Board, so they might put pressure upon the Rural Council, Zosae to place the township in a position to fight an out- -1%4 ef smallpox if it occurred. He had taken the th ask Mr. Thompson to attend, as he represented They would be glad the Rural District Council. ()lithe rZbat he had to say. He might be strengthened said th7nneil by any action they took.—Mr. Thompson Desit•ohl' he could only say the District Council felt the acti°n I— very much. It had been brought about by the r Kzi?ti to de_ take as .rredtPeran tion iras justified in the action taken. by the Corporation. He did not think the The case e one of the Rev. J, J. Langham whose W ii 4. was Ordered into the hospital by his medical man, therr bY the medical officer of the district. It was, Li ~la m.edic e ..)r e ---3 v b erY evident that Mr. Laugh= should pay if M alal, a man ordered the patient to go into the hos- bill. rild the Council felt bound to refuse to pay the thee felt a great deal of sympathy for Mr. Langham to cone*irCierPoration had charged excessively,bnt he had n hereaetrnithe:interests of his constituents, and not al- lioyrof hi dti s any injuseßolensPttarotiachntty viith Mr. Langham to lead him into fever hospital' shortly. hdceostuehehye. represented. At the tlaakset taken 0- TOILS ncil the question was . , had in view the opening of a deputati .i. I thought thatfoi xt4ite aron to wait upon the 'owners of a house to try to He had been appointed on the 1 teaA stlangemetits it NvOpoeunldinbge ''cleal Gover to apply ta the the t tpelra c e . —The Ch.air.- opene, , oment Board. He only got the hospital oat the time of the smallpox epidemic by repre- mr.T:l es to the Local Government Board assisted by the 1