        <head>NEWSPAPER STATISTICS.</head>
        <p>NEWSPAPER STATISTICS.</p>
        <p>From " The Newspaper Press !: Directory " for 18951 (Fifty-first Annual Issue) we ascertain that there are now published in the United Kingdom 2,355 newspapers distributed as follows : England : London Provindes 1,357-1,840 Wales ... •.. Scotland 226 Ireland ... 164 Of these there are :- 156 Daily Papers published in England. 7 Ditto Wales 19 Ditto Scotland as Ditto Ireland 2 Ditto British Isles. On reference to the first edition of this useful Directory , for the year 1846 we find the following interesting facts —viz., that in that year there were published in the United. Kingdom 551. Journals. Of these 14 were issued daily—viz., 12 in England and 2 in Ireland ; but in 1896 there are now established and circulated 2,355 papers,of which no less than 202 are issued daily, showing that the Press of the country has more than quadrupled dur- ing the last 50 years. The increase in Daily Papers has still been more remarkable ; the daily issues standing 202 against 14 in 1846.—From b` The Newspaper Press Directory," 1896 (C. Mitchell and Co., Red Lion Court, Fleet-street.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>DR. JAMESON'S TRIAL.</head>
        <p>DR. JAMESON'S TRIAL.</p>
        <p>The mystery as to the whereabouts of Dr. Jameson and his cfficers and the nature of the charge to he pre- ferred against them was dissipated on Tuesday evening. The transport Victoria arrived in the Thames shortly after noun, and in the evening Dr. Jameson and the other defendants were taken to Bow-st. A large crowd of excited admirers gave them an enthusiastic reception both outside and inside the court. The Duke of Aber- corn and other well-known members of the Chartered Company were present. The charge against the de- fendants was that they in December last, in South Africa, within Her Majesty's dominions,and without the license of Her Majesty, "did unlawfully prepare and fit out a military expedition to proceed againsi the domin- ions of a certain friendly State." Only formal evidence of the arrest was given, and the delendants were then remanded, on their own recognisances in £2,000, for a fortnight. Sir J. Bridge, in granting the application fo bail, pointed out that the charge was of the highest posr sible gravity, and he urged the defendants, in view ef the interests of the country, to abstain from causing excitement by appearing in any public place.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>MOVING MOUNTAIN IN THE SOUTH</head>
        <p>THE MOVING MOUNTAIN IN THE SOUTH</p>
        <p>OF FRANCE.</p>
        <p>Further reports are to hand concerning the remarkable phenomenon of a " moving mountain" in the South of France. The Gouffre mountain, in the Gard, continues to move towards the river at a pace of five yards a day. " Its advance has," says a "Daily News" correspondent " destroyed the machinery and pits of the Grand Combs Colliery, and destroyed nearly a mile of the Alais railway. The great thing now is to prepare new channels for the (lard and Gardon rivers, which are sure when the landslip comes to be completely choked up. Six hundred persons have been obliged to leave their homes at the Grand Combe. The lower strata of the Gouffre mountain, which rises sheer from a valley, are of grit and green marl. Both have given way owing to the infiltration of rain. On Sunday five thousand psr- i eons went from Nimes to see the moving mountain from the range fronting it. The noise it keeps on mak- ing is frightful. There are wide cracks in all directions. Nobldy is sufffared to go on the Gouffre or into the valley on which it advances." The " Chronicle," Paris correspondent telegraphs : "Travellers going south- wards 9n. the Paris and Lyons Railway will do well to Nimes. the r nae oaulnartaminin'gromuateunftraoilan . sCllieprmoofnt-tiele Grandrrandto Combo has now affected the line, and passengers have to leave the train at La Pise. The disaster is hourly esrumcabnaenn tel yx tdent that the in .0 o which,m any ahsa s there- direct tgo pto direct route from g solvedrow tvert the l. Paris to Barcelona, is one of great im- portance."</p>
      </div>
