        <head>Erttero to the Mittore</head>
        <p>Erttero to the Mittore</p>
        <p>" COMMUNICANT " makes a strong complaint of the condition, in matters ecclesiastical, of Stockton Heath parish, and he suggests that "it is the duty of the churchwardens to present the true facts of this case to their Bishop."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>LIBERAL SOIREE.</head>
        <p>THE LIBERAL SOIREE.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Your contemporarysthe " Warrington Observer." on Saturday last thought fit to publish certain deprecia- tory statements respecting the promotion and manage- ment of our Soiree. I will briefly answer them. I. We are told that tickets were sold on the day of the Soiree at one shilling each. As a matter of fact,not a single ticket was sold for less than its declared price, as our balance sheet shows. 2. As to free distribution of tickets. This is still less true. With the excep'..on of some half dozen compli- mentary tickets sent to the press, no tickets were given away. If any employer of labour chose to distribute a few tickets amongst his men, no sin can be laid to his I charge : the committee were paid in full. I may state, however, that all but a very few of the tickets disposed of were sold in the ordinary way, at the full price, promiscuously. 3. The committce know nothing whatever of any "tap." N.,t one drop of intoxicating liquor was sup- plied to the company.—Yours truly. F. R. ROBERTS. __ _</p>
        <p>Hon. Sec.</p>
        <p>THE WHARF QUESTION.-ANOTHER LETTER</p>
        <p>FROM MESSRS. BISEIOP AND CO.</p>
        <p>Sir,—On the 6th of August 1895, at a meeting of the Warrington Town Council, it was resolved "That the Rivers Committee be requested to confer with the Pav- ing and Sewerage Committee and with their concurrence to re-open the negotiations with Messrs. Bishop and Co. with a view to their taking a lease of a portion of the river frontage of the Howley stone-yard for the purposes of a wharf." From that day to this, we have heard nothing from the Warrington Corporation on the sub- ject. No negotiations have been re-opened with us and at the present moment we are without a wharf and there is no indication as far as we know, that the Warrington Corporation are moving in the matter. At our sugges- tion, it will be "remenbered that the Warrington Cor- poration bought the foreshore for the purpose of a wharf for ourselves ani then, having bought it, they stuck to it. At the municipal Elections in November.the electors generally took cognisance of this matter and , returned five new members. The reply of the Warrington Corporation to this openly expressed wish of the community, was to put on the Rivers Comm'ttee Mr. Councillor Flepherd, who we understand retires from his office this year. The worthy Councillor seems determined to cramp the 'industries of this town and ours in particular, although it is not generelly known that we handle, here in War- rington, one-twentieth part of the borax of the globe and that our goods come up by the barge load. We are informed that, shortly after the elections, one venerable Alderman, who signed the now celebrated manifesto, in company with others went to view the site of the pro- posed wharf. He said "I tell you whAt we had better do, we had better build a wall from Messrs. Roberts Dale,' crane to the old packet stage and that wilt put a " bend" to the whole matter." Apart from the wharf which would he of such great benefit to the trade of Warrington, nothing has been done in the way of a land- ing stage on the banks of the Manchester Ship Canal at Warrington. Last week 130 vessels passed up and down the Canal or about one an hour, taking the average working week, and yet not one lb. of goods did they take away from Warrington, solely and wholly on account of the incapacity and supineness of a certain number of its members, who have out-lived the day of their usefulness and are unable to grapple with or even comprehend the wants of the present day.—Yours faithfully. W. A. BISHOP k CO. Dial Mills, Warrington, February 20th, 1896.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>I%IR. PEERS-PICTURED AND PILLORIED.</head>
        <p>I%IR. PEERS-PICTURED AND PILLORIED.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Mr. Peers is finding himself in danger of being cornered too closely, and to shield the situation, his only resource is to seek cover under his usual badinage. In the place of argument he flies off to ridicule—and pushes upon us his old hash of long exploded follies. He tries his hand at evasiveness, and makes up for what- ever is wanting by brazen effrontery. He will not escape the pillory for all that. Let it be noticed how 1 conveniently be seeks to elude the force of my reference to the state and condition of the Roman world at large when Christ came in, and applied the purifying salt, so rescuing it from utter corruption and ruin. The hard facts are too much for him to face. He cannot but feel the impotence of fine maxims alone to meet the needs of any people, and the utter fallacy of his puffing about Confucius and the Chinese too plainly confronts him. Note especially the evasiveness with which he deals with " Poor Zaminer's ' witnesses. Plato! and Seneca. "111-selected "are they Mr. Peers? Did you compre- hend or did you purposely smother the point they were selected to elucidate? You pose as a logician, and are not able apparently to follow the line of thought in an argument. No wonder you stumble sl badly in your search alter the truth. It was not on such questions as the Mosaic Cosmo.ony!these ancients were summoned to judge and rebukeyou. It was their testimony to the need of a divine Teacher who could authoritatively re- veal to them the certainty of religious truth and the duties relative thereto. Your fine maxims were not sufficient for them ; and neither their science, nor yours , had they been living now, could have advanced them I one jot to the solution of problems which troubled them. And you may feel assured of one thing Mr. Peers, that the broad mind of Plato with all the possible knowledge of modern science added thereto, would never have al- lowed him to accept such arguntenta divecta as yours 'upon either Moses' writings or his Cosmogony. The men lof the garden would have shown you the gate of exit, with a caution that that was not he place for those who are" wiser in their own conceit than seven men who could render a reason."</p>
        <p>But you assume vastly too much,Mr. Peers,that these ancients would have had,if living at the present period, that knowledge of science such as you are privileged to enjoyoninus that salt of Christianity which for nineteen hundred years has exerted its awakening influence in minds and hearts of men. Had it not been for the reli- gion of Christ,Alaric with his Goths.. and Attila with his northern barbarians would have made short work of the Prospects of such a state of things as you live under. The moral condition of the world then would have affected its whole intellectual history since, and without the ennobling and stimulating influence of the Christian element *mingling in the chaos of the nations you would probably have been little better now than a painted savage. "For seven- teen hundred years pact" says Lessing at the time he wrote, "the New Testament Scriptures have exercised human reason more than all other books, and enlightened it more, were it only through the light which the human reason itself threw into them." "It remains certain," rays Fichte, "that we,with our whole age and with all our philosophical inquiries, are estab- lished on, and have proceeded from, Christianity; that this Christianity has entered into our whole culture in the most varied forms; and that,on the whole, we might have been nothing of all that we are.had not this mighty principal gone before us in Time," "The holiest among the mighty," says Jean Paul Richter," and the mighti- est among the holy, who had lifted up with his pierced hand empires off their binges, has turned the stream of centuries out of its channel, and still governs the ages." And to close with a testimony from Goethe. "I esteem the Gospels to be thoroughly genuine, for there shines forth from them the reflected -splendour of a sublimity, proceeding from the person o Jesus Christ, and of as divine a kind as was ever manifested on earth. No criticism will be able to perplex the confidence which we have entertained of a writing whose contents have stirred up and given life to our vital energy by its awn." (Italics mine). The full force, therefore, of my reference to Plato and Seneca, is evaded and untouched by your light fingering of the subject. But what you could not face in argu- ment you could only pervert to ridicule, and your effrontery lands you in the merest absurdity. You sum- mon to your help the poor half-witted blacking hawker of 20 to 30 years past; and you tell Ns that "he could have reeled off reams of rant such as Zaminer's ' last." Well I have looked over the letter and searched for the "rant "and endeavoured to trace any connection,but I find only historical facts,and deductions therefrom, which any witling little above the level of "Sammy " could not fail to grasp. Now from what I have heard of this good man I am sure that he never dreamt of dealing with the unregenerate by any such methods as I have employed ; he did, I believe, in his simplicity often cast his "pearls before swine "; but this I have not attempted to do. _</p>
        <p>But I fear, Mr. Editor, to trespass further upon your space, and must leave for a future notice Mr. Peers' crowning audacity—Christianity "the religion par- excellence of the poor in pocket and in intellect."—Yours</p>
        <p>ZAMINER.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>truly. 20th February, 1896.</head>
        <p>truly. 20th February, 1896.</p>
        <p>CREDULOUS "LOGIC."</p>
        <p>"Oh 1 that - one would hew me.</p>
        <p>. ..and _ that mine</p>
        <p>adversary had written a book."—Job.</p>
        <p>Sir,—lt is exceedingly funny to see " Zaminer," at the end of each letter, sharpening his whittle—so to speak— and threatening in his next to "do " for Mr. Peers, and immolate him upon the altar of his wrath. But I, like Isaac, have so far escaped; but, as something must be sacrificed, he finds the ram of truth and logical consist- ency entangled in the brambles of his sophistry. Cre- dulity has assuredly fallen upon evil times in these latter days; the b.liever of this nineteenth century tries to graft upon knowledge the scion of illiterate credence, to. retain the "faith "born of nescience and still pose as a reasoning and reasonable being. Like the navvy who sent his boy to the shop for a "Vb. of bacon," with the injunction that it was to be long, wide and thick, for toasting, they are in the "fix "of tryitig to mix incom- patibles. No creed ever had reason for its parent, but rational investigation stands in the place of Atropos for all creeds. The believer who attempts to " reason " is ' lost, or loses his reason. And it is highly amusing to see the truelbeliever who is indiscreet enough to leave the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, FEBRUARY 29, 1396.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, FEBRUARY 29, 1396.</p>
        <p>circumvallation of his faith and make a sortie upon the rationalistic sons of Belial—very funny, I say, to see, when hard pressed, with what alacrity he "downs" both arms and armour and scuttles back to his castle of cre- dulity! "Vivian Grey "—I have no compunction in vivisecting an anonymity—scuttles ludicrously in his tabulation No. 3, where he falls back upon the "strong place" of the faith the " myst2rious ways of God." Has V.G. ever set himself seriously to the task of asking him- self how he knows, not imagines, anything of God's mysterious ways? How can the Infinite—the Bound- less—the All-pervading—have ways which are at all thinkable by our finite and imperfect faculties? And how comes that other scuttler,"Coulson Kernaka.n," to possess such an intimate acquaintance with the infi- nite as to predicate of It seeing, thinking, Sce. I sup- pose the man had not the sense to see that his argument was self-destructive ; but it would pass muster in the literature of" the faith," and with such feeding how can we expect vigorous mental growth ? "Food for babes" is all very well; but a man does not find ade- quate mental nourishment in the " pobs "which con- stitute nine-tenths of the goody goody literature of the day. " Certainty of Salvation," 61. per dnz. ; " All for Jesus," Bd. per doz.; " Are you saved," 61 per doz. are all doubtlessly excellently adapted to keep their constant readers " faithful unto death " by arrested mental de- velopment and the waste of time and attention which might have been devoted to healthier and more invigor- ating reading. Credulity made easy are aids to im- becility ; the mind like the body is strong or weak— other things being equal—in proportion to the exercise it gets. So the mind that is " All fer Jesus" at 81. per doz. gets so far and no further. And now having dealt with the "crumb," I turn to the other, and more "crusty," side of my sandwich. Za.miner would fain oose as a logician. A little examin- ation will show" his unique qualification for the task." My contention is that Zaminer's creed has stolen, from a heathen nation, its best bit of morality and embodied it in its "Divine " teaching without acknowledgement. For myself I fail to find aught of " divinity" in plagiar- ism, but the point I wish to show is that even though the Chicese have made poor use of this most excellent maxim, that is no efficient condonation of the bad taste we display in ramming this stolen maxim down the throats of its original possessors by British bayonets. And the article, again, is returned in an adulterated cendition—mixed up with matter which no sane person or nation could accept—and still be accounted sane. To , " Love your neighbours as yourself" may be a Christian maxim, and good for the Christian who has turned himseif inside out and become "beside himself" but it is a queer way of illustrating it—by gun boats and maxim guns ! ! Well may the Chinese wish us to be more neighbourly, even if we" loved '' them less! And I ob- ject to paying the " shot" for this kind of " Love " being perpetrated either in India or in China. Again we have " Layman" offering himself as an object lesson in the " loeie " of belief ! Hear him ! " He is convinced that a Christian is either a fool or a hyprocrite." Now Ido not say that! A Layman has I evidently, by some inward light, seen the true inward- ness of Christianity; but for myself I leave it to the 'Christians themselves to demonstrate these facts ! This is how Layman does it. He must have his ±!b. of bacon, lon,sewide and thick ; he is to be at liberty to reject the ignorance of antiquity, but retain the superstition founded upon it! In my shallow scepticism I had thought that the removal of the foundation involved the destruction of the super-structure. But there ! that shows the !difference between an ignorant and shallow sceptic and a Layman with 'his mustard-seed of faith which can move mountains of logical difficulties and thaumaturgically, cut the ground from under his creed, leaving it all there, if somewhat cracked. But this is only one way of regarding the matter. The other way is to regard the foundation as not being destroyed— ignorance and credulity being perennial, ever-green,and, like the poor, always with us. I wish to thank Zaminer for the very excellent work he is doing, however blindly, in opening the eyes of his victims as to the missionary fallacy. He goes into detail as to the mode in which this force is applied and then, after weary iteration of the denial, gays, "Be it so, but mast folk will judge that such force was never more rightly employed." Gun boats and bayonets ! But this always was the way of the true believer. He always did kick against the pricks of truth till he hurt his shins, when he would turn round and claim credit for his re- ceptive celerity ! Have I not said that no fsane indivi- dual, or nation Above the status of ignorant barbarians, would receive this creed, in which the heathen wheat so much outweighs the Christian chaff ? Well, here is confirmative evidence from China. In the " Chronicle " of the London Missionary Society for current month.the leader is on " Chinese antagonism to Missionaries." Here we learn, "And yet, somehow,there is the anti-mission- ary feeling." Where does it come from ? How does it arise? from "the Literati and the Yemen men. If these disturbers of the peace would keep their hands off, the missionaries would get along well enough with the vil- lagers." There, the cat's out of the bag ! The missien- ary must have raw—very raw—material to work on. The head must be very emnty or very much cracked if the creed is to be got into it! The Literati —the learned Chinese—are not able to recognise the divinity of an 0110Podrida—a rehash of their own and other Pagan maxitne.—Yours, &amp;a., Penketh, 16th Feb.. 1896.</p>
        <p>JOHN PEERS.</p>
        <p>NEWTON DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>BUSINESS OF THE NEXT MEETING.</p>
        <p>The next meeting of the above-named Council will be held in the Town Hall, Earlestown, on Monday next when the following among other matters will be con- sidered : HIGHWAYS, PUBLIC WORKS AND SANITARY COMMITTER. A letter was read to this committee from Mr. C. T. Whitley, of Wallspitt House. Stretton, near Warrington, in answer to the Clerk's letter of January 28th,1898,sug- gesting the widening of the footpath opposite to his property in Wargrave-road, stating that he was wilting; to give the land for widening the footpath pro- viding the adjoining owners did the same, the Council to pave and flag the new path in the usual manner, and that he be kept free from all charges, whereupon it was resolved that Mr. Whitley's offer be accepted. It was also resolved that the Surveyor prepare a statement to be submitted to this committee showing all those roads within the district where there is no footpath.</p>
        <p>The annual report of Dr. Watkins (Medical Officer of Elealth) presented to the Committee at its meeting on February 17th contains the following information. Dur- ing 1895, there were registered—births, males 257, females 214; deaths, males 109, females 105. The birth- rate was 36.5 and the death-rate 15-3 per 1,000 of the estimated population. The percesitage (55 1) of the deaths of children under 5 years of age is, Dr. Watkins states, very high, and was mainly due he believed to the incidence of non-notifiable infectious diseases. Of the 40 deaths from the seven principal diseases, no less than 36 were of children under 5 years of age, and of these 36, all but one were of infectious diseases not notifiable during life, viz., meales 6, whoopin:: cough 10, diarrlice3, 19. The water supply is good and constant. The ques- tion of the possibility of the increase of supply commen- surately with their growing wants, and the assured permanency of supply, is, remarks Dr. Watkins, at the present time agitating the public mind. In a matter of such vital importance to the public health, undue weight should not be attached to the question of primary cost. An apparently cheap and good source may not in the long run prove to be really economical. The pollution of Newton Lake has been diminished by the measures taken by the Ashton, Golborne, and Newton Sanitary Autho- rities, hut still the affluent stream at Golborne Dale leaves very much to be desired and contrasts greatly with the affluent flowing through Golberne Hol- lows at the west end of the lake. The medical officer believes that 1895 would have been a remarkably healthy year but for the incidence of the three non-notifiable diseases—summer diarrhcei, whooping cough and measles. They knew not how many cases of sickness the 35 deaths of children represented. Their bye-laws might be ideally perfect, their sanitary appliances and operations all up to date, but their control over preventible disease would be crip- pled,if not annulled, till the public was convinced that their co-operation was necessary to the common weal. Dr. Watkins also reports that he had inspected several pigsties in Legh-st. which he condemns as nuisances Lncl injurious to the public health. The committee has there- fore decided that the owners be required to abate the nuisance within seven days, and in default that legal proceedin.;s be taken without further notice. Mr. Gregory, the sanitary inspector, reports that he had found little to complain of in regard to the shops, bakehouses, and slaughter-houses,witu the exception of one slaughter-house which was in a dirty condition. This monthly tolls for street hawking were £3 53. 6J., mar- kets £2O 103. 11d., total £23 163. 5J., compared with £2O 33. Bd.</p>
        <p>A sub-committee having recommended a revised list of charges for the use of the Public Hall, the list has been adopted by the general committee. The hall keeper at the request of the Clerk, reported verbally as to the " disgraceful state of the hall after the volunteer ball on January 24th," and the committee after examin- ing the various rooms in the Town Hall, decided to recommend to the General Committee that the following words be added to Clause 2 of the rules and regulations attached to the scale of charges, " and on no account shall refreshments of any kind be sold after 3 a.m." The Clerk was instructed to write to Mr. George Bailey, hon. sec., Ist Volunteer Battalion South Lancashire Regiment, G Company, Earlestown, com- plaining of the disgraceful state in which they gave up the hall after their ball on January 24th, 1896, and that the committee cannot make any rebate to the volunteers as requested in their letter of December Ist, 1895.</p>
        <p>GAS AND WATER COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of this committee held on February 17th, the Chairman referred to that part of the gas manager's report for January dealing with a proposed expenditure of £1,700 for a purifying and lime shed and connections thereto, which had been referred to him for further consideration at the last meeting of the commit- tee, and stated that after carefully thinking the matter over he had decided to recommend the committee not to incur the expenditure at present, but would recommend instead that the No. 1 gasholder be converterl into a tar reservoir, which he estimated could be done at a cost of about £lOO. This recommendation was adopted. _ -</p>
        <p>_ In the gas manager's report for the month it is stated that 300 automatic gas meters were used at a cost per meter of £2 53. giving a total of £875. Be reports that on the whole the meters worked very satisfactory though sometimes the coin attachment does not work properly with the meter index. He adds that" the index vo bleb shows the amount of gas consumed is the record we re- cognise and in any case of disagreement between that ' record and the amount of money found in the meter box we adjust according to the index—that is,we return the balance of money if the index shows less gas consumed than money deposited, and if the gas consumed is greater than money in box, the consumer should pay the difference. This system has worked pretty well thus far, with the, I think, single exception of a consumer named 0. Pickett, Nelson-st. The meter in this man's house shows amount of gas consumed to the value of 18s. Bd. and the money in box was 17s. 6cl. The Inspec- tor drew the consumer's attention to the fact and le- quested the difference, viz., ls.,—but he refused to pay it. I should be pleased with your instructions for deal- ing with this matter, though I advise that he be made to pay or have the supply of gas cut a at once, as it will be simply impossible to supply gas satisfactorily by the Automatic Meter system if the index reading is not adhered to." In dealing with this report the commit- tee decided that the meter in question should be tested.</p>
        <p>GENERAL PURPOSES COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>This Committee after a discussion as to the advisability or otherwise of Overseers of the Poor for the year ending April Ist, 1897, being chosen from amongst the council- lors, it was resolved—that the Council be recommended to increase the number of Overseers to three, and that two be elected from amongst the members of the Urban District Council in Newton-in-Makerfield, and one from outside.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>EARLESTOWN LIND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN LIND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>MR. B. V. WA.TKINS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Bernard Vlcent Watkins, M. 8., Ch .B (Univ. Viet.) has recently received the diplomas of M.R.C.S. England and L.R.C.P. London, and been appointed a house surgeon in the Manchester Royal Infirmary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>NEWTON-LE-WILLOWS CHORAL SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>NEWTON-LE-WILLOWS CHORAL SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>A meeting was held at the Town Hall, Newton, on Monday evening, when it was decided to wind up the society and to elevote the proceeds realised from the sale of its effects to the St. Peter's Church building fund.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>EARLESTOWN WESLEY GUILD.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN WESLEY GUILD.</p>
        <p>In connection with the above a lecture was given on Thursday evening, by the Rev. C. de P. Gliddon, of Runcorn, on "Joan of Arc " ; her vices and vic- tories." The Rev. J. Bartley presided, and there was a fair attendance of tke public. The lecturer dealt with his subject in an able manner, and the greatest interest was evinced by those present. BREAKDOWN AT SANKEY SUGAR WORKS. _</p>
        <p>On Sunday morni.)g a serious breakdown of machinery took place at the Sankey Sugar Works, which will necessitate a total stoppage of opera- tions for about a fortnight. A number of men are engaged on the repairs, but the mishap will throw about 100 men out of work. DRUNKENNESS.</p>
        <p>At the Town Hall, Newton, on Saturday morning, before Colonel Wood, Thomas Rooke was charged with being drunk, and was tined ss. and costs.—At the Earlestown Police Station on Monday, before Messrs. C. B. F. Borron and J. W. Emmett, Samuel Mains was fined ss. and costs for being drunk and disorderly.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>INQUEST AT NEWTON.</head>
        <p>INQUEST AT NEWTON.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Brighouse, coroner, held an inquiry into the circumstances attending the death ot Mrs. Dlizabeth Jackson, of Gibraltra-row, Newton, who was killed by falling downstairs on Wednesday evening, details of the accident being reported in our last issue. The inquest was held at the Swan Inn on Saturday, and a verdict of "Accidental death "was returned. GOOD TEMPLARY.</p>
        <p>The Earlestown .Ebenezer Lodge held its weekly session on Tuesday evening in the Co-operative Hall. Bro. A. Morris, C.T., presided. One candidate was received into membership. Bro. J.Taylor gave a report of District Lodge held at Liverpool oa Satur- day, and arrangements made for forthcoming con- ve_Ations. "A Visit to Ashton " was the programme for the:evening,and the following took part :—Bros. Leyland, J. Taylor; recitation and reading, Bros. Morris and Taylor. ACCIDENT AT THE VIADUCT WORKS.</p>
        <p>Robert Boughton, of Legh-street, Earlestown, employed as a labourer at the Viaduct Works, was on Saturday morning engaged with other men on a high stack of timber, in the timber yard, unloading planks from a wagon,when from 80010 cause or other he slipped and fell on to a lower stack, rolling from this to the ground, and pulling with him another man named Broomhall. The latter was not injured, but Boughton was very badly shaken, and had to be removed home on a stretcher. Dr. Handyside attended him, and he is now in a f air way to re- covery.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>GOOD TEIVIPLARS.</head>
        <p>GOOD TEIVIPLARS.</p>
        <p>The weekly session of the Hope of Earlestown Lodge of Good Templars was held in the Co-opera- tive Hall on Monday evening. Bro. G. Clarke pre- sided, and the visitors included Bro. W. E. Hall, Criterion Lodge,Liverpool ; and Quartermaster-Ser- geantßoberts,both of whom gave addresses. Thanks were tendered to Bro. Hall for his visit, and he was asked to convey fraternal greetings to his lodge. A report of the district lodge annual meeting was given by Bro. Clarke and adopted. Arrangements were completed for visiting Ashton and Wesley Hall Lodges on Friday. The programme of the evening was "The LO.G.T., what it is and what it does," Bro. G. Clarke being responsible, and it proved an interesting and instructive item. DEATH OF MR. THOMAS KILSHALL.</p>
        <p>On Saturday morning last Mr. Thomas Kilshall, who lived in Cross-lane, Earlestown, died at the advanced age of 70 years. Mr. Kilshall had been in failing health for same time, and although he was assiduously attended by Dr. Mouncey he never re- covered, and died as stated. At one time he resided at the Bradley Hall Farm, but for some years has abstained from agricultural pursuits, and has lived a quiet, retired life in Cross-lane, Earlestown. He has never mixed in public matters, but for some time has been a trustee of the Earlestown Wes- leyan Church. He was interred at Sankey on Thursday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>SONS OF TEMPERANCE.</head>
        <p>SONS OF TEMPERANCE.</p>
        <p>The Samuel Morley Section of Cadets of Temper- ance met on Wednesday in the Co-operative Hall Earlestown, Sister Louise Barrett, W.A., in the chair. After the opening ceremony,one member was admitted. Recitations were given by Sisters Reece, Eleanor Barrett,Ethel Pearson, Bros. Anderton, (St. Helens), Pearson and W. Whatley.Each spoke a few encouraging words to the members. Several of the members of the eection visited St. Helens on Mon- day, and took part in an entertainment in the Con- gregational Mii•sion Room, Gerard's Bridge, which was very successful. The adult division met in the same room at 8 o'clock, Bro. Yorke, W.P., in the chair. After the routine business was disposed of, impromptu speeches were given on various sub- jects and discussions took place on several,in which various members took part. A very interest- ing talk was enjoyed by the members present. Next meeting a series of papers will be commenced by Bro. W. Pearson, from Dr. Edmund's papers which appear in the Son'of Temperance monthly. The meet- ing closed at 9.45.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>MARRIAGE OF MISS STURGESS OF</head>
        <p>MARRIAGE OF MISS STURGESS OF</p>
        <p>E RLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday afternoon at the Primitive Methodist Chapel, Earlestown,Miss Caroline Sturgess,second daugh- ter of Mr. Joseph Sturgess. of Wargrave-road, Earles- town, and foreman at the Viaduct works, was united in wedlock to Mr. Frederick Dickens,son of Mr. Dickens, , head tailor at Mr. E. T. Evans, Earlestown. The officiat- ing minister was the Rev. E. R. Davies. The bride looked well in a cream alpaca dress, with satin and pearl trimmings with hat to match. The bridesmaids were Miss Lillie Sturgess and Miss Ada Dickens, who were dresses of grey crepen, with hats to match. The groomsm in was Mr. T. Dickens, and the bride was given away by her father. A reception was held at the home of the bride's father, and there was a large number of guests. Later on in the evening the newly married couple left for their new home. The following is a list of the presents ;--Mr. and Mrs. Dickens electro-plated cruet ; the Misses Dickens. cutlery ; Mr. T. Dickens,tea service ; Miss Sturgess and Mr. J.Gavan, bedroom ware ; Miss L. Sturgess, cheese dish: Mr. and Mrs. G. Sturgess, dinner service ; Miss Griffiths (War- rington), trinket set ; Mr. Chadwick and Miss Foley (Manchester), trinket set ; Mr. and Mrs. E. T. Evans, marble time piece; the Misses Evans, wicker chair; Miss White and Mr. W.Partingt, (Wa,rrington).Vienna rent- lator ; Mrs. Willis.hamhno table ; Mr.and Mrs. T.Ca.wley (Haydock), kitchen table ; Mrs. Wrig.ht, copner kettle ; Mrs. Lindsay ( \tanc.hester), vases ; Mr. J. Lindsay, sardineldish, Miss L. Lindsay..d'oyleys ; Mrs. Hindley (Newton-le-Willows), water jug ; Mrs. Burke, celery glass ; Mrs. Stringer, fancy jug : Miss P. Stringer, plaque ; Mr. E. Mullins, breakfast cruet ; Mr. and Mrs. E. Brammer, antimacassars and mats ; Mr. C. Dickens and Mr. H. Matthews, silver fish knife, fork and spoon in case ; Mr. Baron (Golborne), case of salt cellars ; Mr. and Mrs. Ormand, timepiece ; Mr. Sharroc.k (Hindley). bookcase ; Mrs. Sharrock, cheese dish and ornaments ; Miss N. Wright, biscuit barrel ; Miss M. Phail, butter cooler; Mr. D. Parry (Warrington), crumb tray and brush; Mr. and Mrs. Crawford, decan- ters ; employes of Mr. T. Evans. curb, dogs, irons and flower stand ; Mr., Mrs. and Misses Wilkinson, hand- Painted opals and frames ; Mr. and Mrs. H. Woodward (Wellington). white table cover ; Miss Watson, tidy ; employ6s of Mr. Sharrock copper kettle and stand ; Miss Perry (Flindley), set of jugs ; Miss M. Higgins, mats ; Mr. and Mn. Moorhotise (Stalpybridge), water jug and tumblers ; Mr. and Mrs. Banton, ink stand ; Mrs. Wavwell (Warc.iraye), flower vases ; Mr. Clarke, photo stand.</p>
        <p>MEMORIAL WINDOW_ TO THE LATE REV. T. r.</p>
        <p>KIRKMAN.</p>
        <p>On Sunday two sermons were preached in Christ Church, Croft. by the Rey. Richard Tebbs, vicar of St. Thomas's. Wigan, and collections made on behalf of the memorial window fund for the late Rev. T. P. Kirkman, for upwards of 50 years rector of the parish. The collections amounted to .C 4 183. 4d. The window will be in the centre of three handsome stained glass windows, to be erected in the chancel to the memory of the late Rector and Mrs. Kirkham, in place of the present plain diamond glass ones. The other two windows will he erected by the family of the late Rector and Mrs. Kirkman. The cost of the windows will be £lO7.</p>
        <p>OILS.—GARLAND, Importer, 51, Bridge-street, Warrinzton. STOVES.—Iron Merchant, GARLAND, Bridg -street llousEneto LtvgNs.hernmed and marked Ire ge —J. and W. Durrox,dankoy-et.. Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>BRUNNER, M.OND, AND CO., LIMITED.</head>
        <p>BRUNNER, M.OND, AND CO., LIMITED.</p>
        <p>The 30th half-yearly ordinary general meeting of Messrs. Brunner. Mond, and Co.. Limited, Winnington. Northwich, was held on Friday last week, in the Law Associatioo Rooms, Cook-st., Liverpool. Sir J. T. Brun- ner, M.P., presiding over a large attendance. The Chairman, in moving the adoption of the report and statements of accounts (already published) said they were of what he might call the usual satisfactory character. There was a large new item in the balance- sheet under the head of property and assets. These were the works at Middlewich. of which they took possession on the Ist day of August. and they had now the result of five days' working in the balance-sheet. He was very glad to say that there was every prospect that these Middlewich works would be a thoroughly satisfactory item of their property, and that they would be a propor- tionate aid in the making of a dividend. The items "stocks on hand" and " debts owing to and by the company" were exactly in proportion to the increased business done. They all increased practically half-year by half-year, and remained in proportion to the total as they had done in the past. The premium received on the 6000 preference shares allotted appeared, as usual, to the credit of the reserve fund. They were aware that during the last half-year the company had entered into an agreement with the United Alkali Company and Messrs. Bowman, Thompson and Co. As they would readily understand, they did not initiate such a movement, but the directors were!not bound to resist—in fact. that they were bound to accept —any proposals that might be made in that direction. The prices of alkali were, it was also notorious.below the cost of production in most cases, and that, in the direc- tors' opinion, was a thoroughly unwholesome state of affairs. They were glad for their own sakes to put an end to this unreasonable competition. But looking ahead, as the directors always felt themselves bound to do, they were very decidedly of opinion that they ought not to put themselves in such a position as would encourage fresh competition in the future, and therefore he thought they were all agreed that they should not raise the market price so as to encourage undue competi- tion in fatnre. In moving the adoption of the report and accounts, he further proposed that the following dividends be declared :—To the holders of preference shares 7 Per cent, per annum, and to ordinary share- holders 30 per cent. per annum. The motion was seconded by Mr. H. Coghill. The Chairman, in reply to a wstion by Mr. Reddrop, said the 6000 preference shares had been issued for cash, and the remainder had been issued.for the purch.se or part-purchase of the Middlewich works. He further stated, in answer to Mr. n arsie, that the arrangement with the United Alkali Company had not limited them as to their make in any way. They could make and sell as much as they could without any limit. On the motion of Mr. Jones. seconded by Mr. Harting, the usual remuneration was voted to the directors. The retiring directors, Messrs. Galloway, H. Brunner, Forgan and J. F. L. Brunner were re-elected, on the motion of Mr. C. M. Holland. On the motion of Mr. Gallaway, a resolution was passed fixing the mode and amount of remuneration I payable to Mr. J. F. L. Brunner as general manager of the London branch of the business. Messrs. T. W. Read and Co. were reappointed auditors on the proposition of Mr. Armitage, and the meeting concluded with thanks to the chairman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>LICHFIELD-ELECTION.</head>
        <p>LICHFIELD-ELECTION.</p>
        <p>ANOTHER LIBERAL VICTORY.</p>
        <p>Po'line took place in the Lichfield Division on Wednes- day to fill the vacancy resulting from the unseating on petition of Mr. Fu'ford (L). who was elected last July by a majority of forty-four. The candidates were Major Darwin (U), the unsuccessful candidate at the general election, and Mr. Courtenay Warner (L). The result of the voting was announced on Thursday afternoon as follows : Warner (L.) Darwin (U.)</p>
        <p>Majority There are 9,384- electors on the register. Tne figures at the two previous elections were :- 1892. _</p>
        <p>Major Dirwin (UnicTo-is-t) Sir J. Swinburne (Liberal) 3,572</p>
        <p>Unionist majority 1895.</p>
        <p>Mr. Fa'ford tLiherali- 3.902 Major Darwin (Unioni6o</p>
        <p>Liberal majority In 1892, on a recount of the votes, the UGionist majority was declared to be eleven.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>FINANCE COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>FINANCE COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>At a meeting on February 17th, the Chairman referred to the letter of Mr. Haslehurst which had been before the committee on January 23rd, 1893, having reference to the audit of the accounts of the Connell, and which was referred to the Law Clerk for consideration. Mr. Worsley being present, the Chairman requested him to state the position of the Council in regard to the matter. After the Law Clerk's statement thereon, it was unan- imously resolved that the Chairman of the committee (Mr. Ormand), Mr. Worsley and Mr. Brierlev, be a deputation to the Local Government Board, for the pur- pose ef arriving, if possible, at a solution of the ques- tion, as to whether the accounts of this Council come under the Act of 1894, and ard subject to audit by the District Auditor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>SCFIOOL ATTENDANCE COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>SCFIOOL ATTENDANCE COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>- At a meeting of this Committee the Clerk stated that he had received no reply from the Rev. Father Lennon in answer to his letter asking him to be good enough to send in his usual quarterly return of attendances for the quarter ended December 31st, 1895. It was decided to allow the Rev. Father Lennon a further extension of time in which to send in the return asked for.</p>
        <p>TECHNICXL INSTRUCTION COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of this Committee it was resolved that the application of the Joint Committee of the Viathict Technical Institute, asking for a grant of £B5 towards the current expenses, be acceded to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>REV. C. F. AKED AT EA RLESTO WN.</head>
        <p>THE REV. C. F. AKED AT EA RLESTO WN.</p>
        <p>On Monday evening at the Town Hall, EarlesLown, the Rev. C. F. Aked of Liverpool lectured on " The strongest man 015 earth," the proceeds being in aid of the fund for bull ling a new Baptist Sunday School. Mr. Councillor E. T. Evans presided ,and he was accompanied on the platform by the Rev. C. F. Aked, Rev. F. E. Miller, Messrs. G. F. Clarke, J. G. Mullins, J. Phillips and Rev. E. R. Davies. The Rev. F. E. Miller said he had an explanation to make which had been forced upon him. He bad to say that they had been brought there upon false pretences. It had been announced that the lecture would be" The man of Nazareth ; and the social revolution," and he had tried to persuade Mr. Aked to give the lecture he had intended, or at any rate let the title remain. The real and genuine title of the lecture was not " The man of Nazareth," but, " The Strongest Man on Earth." Since the subject was announced, Mr. Aked had come to the conclusion that they would be more interested in the lecture on " the strongest man on earth." The lecture had been received with much en- thusiasm in many places, especially across the Atlantic, which he thought was a good recommendation, and he was authorised to say that if any of them should feel they had been defrauded at the title of the lecture, that they could have their money back, and Mr. Aked would pay it. (Laughter). Mr. Aked, prefaced his lecture by saying there was very little wander that old Greeks killed Socrates. The man must have made himself intolerable to the people. A little flat nosed man who went about asking the people imprudent questions at inconvenient times, and it was very little wonder they killed him. Passing on he referred to the works of Ibsen. His work was to ask us questions that we certainly believed concerned our denaocracy,—to put questions to men about things that men felt themselves most certain of, and like Socrates himself to let us know how much we do not know. There were some plays of Ibsen to which a man might turn with profit. There was one play, a strange pedantic tragedy called</p>
        <p>"Ghosts."Up to the time of the publication of "Ghosts,' 1 Ibsen's life had been fairly tranquil and easy, literary- distinctions and honour had begun to reach him, but with the publishing of that document he became a storm centre ; his critics tried to shout him down, and he re- plied with" An enemy of the people." An enemy of the people was Ibsen himself. The " strongest man on earth," was the man who stood most alone. He then passed in review over many notable characters, Martin Luther, Oliver Cromwell, and John Wesley,who saved this coun- try from hell, and he was a man who was never afraid to stand alen9, and time after time he had to learn that the strongest man on earth was the man who stands most alone. Garibaldi bad forced the truth upon us also. The world waited for another incarnation of the same faith,the same enthusiasm, the same religion ; for a reformed theology and a social gospel, a gospel that should be as good for the polling booth as it was for the prayer meeting ; for the Houle of Commons as it was for the House of prayer, a religion as good for the coal pit as for the pulpit, or else good for nothing, a re- ligion that should build up our national life, in the likeness and image and according to the incarna- tion of the Son of God. Get God's will done in the Council, in the Legislature,in the Land Question, and in the broad fields of international life, as it was done in heaven. The democracy—common ignorant, undeveloped man. In a democracy the majority ruled. Fools were in a majority anywhere, therefore in a democracy fools ruled. Were we to give up our belief in a democracy ? We must admit part of Ibsen's ghost. We were in danger of unduly glorifying the common man. He did not mean the man who was poor in this world's goods. Ibsen had something to say for himself, and that we were in danger of glorifying the ignorant undeveloped common man. Majorities did riot rule, and never did ; minorities ruled ; ideas governed ; God was not on the side of the big battalions. Fools rush in where angels fear to tread. It was always the fools and fanatics that made history. In the 14th century it was the fool, the mad Wat Tyler. It the 16th century it was the fool called the Protestant keeping cool amidst the flames of Smithfield fire, and in the 17th century it was the fool called the Separatist, and in the 18th century it was the foal called the Methodist and in the 19th century, it was the Chartist, the teetotaler who makes the forces with which statesmen have to deal. Failing the democracy, on what;were they goingto fall back? The money bag of the aristocracy. We were waiting for the arrival of -our strong man. There was absolutely not one moral move- ment of our time which was not in some temporary de- pression. England had lost the strongest ruler she had had, since Cromwell went hame to God. Germany had no strong man. Belgium had taught a lesson that Europe might well learn. We needed a man who felt that all religion from crown of head to sole of stocking was secure and settled. On the motion of the Rev. J.E. Miller, seconded by the Rev. E. R. Davies, a hearty vote of thanks was accorded Mr. Aked, and a similar compli- ment was paid the chairman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>SENSATIONAL MURDER OF A WARRINGTON LADY.</head>
        <p>THE SENSATIONAL MURDER OF A WARRINGTON LADY.</p>
        <p>A TRAMP ARRESTED IN MANCHESTER.</p>
        <p>On the night of the 14th inst. a daring burglary, which was accompanied by the murder of an old lady named Mrs. Kaye, who formerly lived in Bewsey-street, Warrington, was committed at Arnold, near Nottingham. A man, supposed to be a tramp, called at the house of Mrs. Powley,market gardener, and told Mrs. Powley that he had been sent to inform her of a serious accident which had befallen her husband at a place about a mile distant. The suggestion was that the husband nad been run over by a cyclist. Mrs. Powley pro- ceeeded to the place indicated, only to find the story untrue, Mr. Powley being evidently found at the house of a friend. When she reached home again a ghastly sight was presented to her. Mrs. Kaye, an elderly guest, was lying on the floor with terrible wounds on her head, her skull having been fractured in three places. Further search led to the discovery that £7 and some jewellery had been stolen. Mrs. Kaye, who was sixty-eight years of age, was taken to the Nottingham Hospital, where she died three days later. A description of the man who told the false tale to Mrs. Powley was issued by the Notts police. On Monday last a man who gave the name of Thomas Wilkinson was brought before the Manchester City magistrates on the charge of breaking into the pavilion of the Anson Golf Club in Birch Lane, Rusholme, and was committed for trial. Detective Inspector Goodwin, who was in court, suspected, from his resemblance to a photograph,that the prisoner might be the per- son wanted for the Notts murder. By direction of the Chief Constable the Notts police were communi- cated with, and on Thursday morning, Superinten- dent Harrop came to Manchester,with Mrs. Powley, who was taken to Strangeways Prison in order that Wilkinson might be confronted with her. She at once identified him as the man who bad given her false information respecting her husband on the day of the murder. The prisoner will, therefore, it is expected, be called upon to answer tha more serious charge.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT (Continued from page 3.) THURSDAY.</head>
        <p>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT (Continued from page 3.) THURSDAY.</p>
        <p>The Holm SHCRETARY, asked by Mr. J. Wilson in the House of Commons on Thursday if his attention had been drawn to thesteady decline in the number of con- victions for drunkenness in Liverpool since 1889, said it was quite true that since that year the number of prose- cutions for drunkenness had fallen from 16,042 to 5,657. The Chief Constable of the city believed that a change in the system of police inspection of licensed houses which was introduced in 1889 had conduced to the diminution of drunkenness, but he (Sir M. W. RIDLEY) regretted to say that the proportion of drunkenness in Liverpool still stood high as compared with the returns from other towns. Mr. LONG told Mr. Parker Smith that he did no think a muzzling order for dogs applicable to the whole country was either necessary or feasible under existing circumstances. _ _ _</p>
        <p>In reply to Sir J. Long, Lord U. HAMILTON said the Indian Factory Act permitted the examination and re- pair of machinery on Sundays,the weekly holiday being given on some other day. Mr. CURZON, in reply to Mr. J. A. Pease, said active work on the Uganda railway would soon be begun, and the Railway Committee hoped to have it completed in less than fouryears. Mr. PARKER Smrrn, asked what salary the Poet Laureate received, an whether any Minister was vs- sponsibie for his actions. Mr. MeCARTAN asked Mr. A. J. Balfour to consider the desirableness of submitting to either the Lord Chamberlain or some other Minister not only the political utterances of the Poet Laureate but his poetry generally. Mr. BALFOUR replied that the Poet Laureate's salary was £7O a year, charged on the Civil List. He did not think any department of the Govern- ment was specially qualified to exercise poetical censor- ship. Mr. Cuezos, replying to Sir E. A. Bartlett, said no proposals had recently been made to the Government, either by Turkey or any Great Power, which would in- volve the early evacuation of Egypt by the British troops. The twelve o'clock rule having been suspended, the House resumed the debate on Mr. Labouchere's amend- ment to Mr. Balfour's resolution devoting Fridays to Committee of Supply and limiting the number of days for Supply to twenty. The amendment which proposed to omit the clauses establishing a time limit was defeated by 258 votes to 124. In the course of the debate on sub- sequent amendments Mr. BALFOUR admitted that his I proposid was somewhat inelastic; and later be submitted an amendment, which was agreed to, giving a Minister of the Crown power, at the end of the twenty days, to move that not more than an additional three days should be given to the discussion of Supply.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>SHIP CANAL</head>
        <p>SHIP CANAL</p>
        <p>RUNCORN URBAN COUNCIL AND THE BILL.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Parliamentary and General Purposes Committee of the Council was held on Wednesday even- ing, when the question of opposing the Ship Canal Bill was under consideration. After carefully considering the matter,it was decided that inasmuch as the investig- ations as to the probable effect of the closing of the tidal openings were incomplete, and that the time was so limited, not to oppose the Bill in the House ef Commons. The investigations are, however, to be continued, and should it be discovered that the result of the closing of the tidal openings will endanger the health or trade of the district, a petition against the Bill will be forwarded when it comes before the House of COMMODS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>HALTON.</head>
        <p>HALTON.</p>
        <p>WESLEY GIIILD.—The members of this guild had their monthly" Literary Night" on Monday. The subject under discussion was whether war was justifiable. Mr. Walton Moores took the affirmative, and Mrs. Boys the negative. A number of members also joined in the dis- cussion which proved most interesting. The chair was occupied by the Rev. C. D. Putron Gliddon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>. 0n...........4111..•1101•1••• FINANCIAL AND MINING NEWS.</head>
        <p>a . 0n...........4111..•1101•1••• FINANCIAL AND MINING NEWS.</p>
        <p>The "Money Market Review" says.: "Sheba Queen shares have been largely bought during the week on cable orders from South Africa, and the inference is that the mine is looking better than people on this side areas yet aware of. It is confidently predicted that a mueb higher price for the Shares (which close to-day at 4s. 34 to 4s. 9d.) will shortly be attained." In a circular to the Sheba Queen shareholders it states that four assays of the quartz yielded 1 oz. 2 dwts., 2 oz. 2 dwts., 3 oz. 8 dwts., ani 4 oz. 6 dwts. respectively. The reef is already 6 ft. wide, the total width not yet being ascer- tained.</p>
