      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <p>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN AT</p>
        <p>RUNCORN.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-29_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>BY OUR RUNCORN CORRESPONDENT.</head>
        <p>BY OUR RUNCORN CORRESPONDENT.</p>
        <p>It cannot yet be stated that Runcorn has etae:l6-. from that, state of trade depresion which it hi° suffered from so long. While other towns are re" porting increased trade aztivity, Runcorn still Jagt behind, and business is very little better tban. twelve months ago. Various reasons are given W account for this. Some lay the blame at the door of the United Alkali Company, and others are equally persistent in contending that the Mall', chester Ship Canal Company are very largely tO blame. With regard to the Alkali Company it IS very certain that the chemical ring, as it has bef!li called, has worked havoc with that industry 10 Runcorn. An industry which once engaged, per haps more workpeople than any other in the toW. fir has languished to almost the extinguishing point, and the only reminder of its existence is the der late and woe-begone appearance of the once pr 4 perous works.</p>
        <p>Then again the construction of the Ship Cane cannot be stated to have improved the trade ei Runcorn, in fact it must be admitted that at the present time the very reverse is the case. The' Canal Company have received great privileges from Runcorn,but they have not yet acknowledged theL in the same generous fashion. They were allowedl without opposition, to cut off from the port el Runcorn, not only their title to that maritime diE tinction,but also to stop for ever the lapping on rhe Runcorn shore of the river Mersey, by the constry.C° tion of a huge wail. The result has been the der, truction of the fishing industry and the removal ot, the ship repairing trade to other places. Further, if the Bill passes for the closing of the tidal opelr ings, fresh river water will not reach the canal at Runcorn, and many or the men engaged dredging, the waterway in the district, will be thrown out employment.</p>
        <p>It is quite true that the Company have providecli employment for a number of them, but it is verii questionable whether this compensates for the 1001 sustained. It is, however, premature, to take e,! pessimistic view of the situation. The provision of extended lay-by accommodation may yet prove Of some advantage to the town, but the latest rumou current is that the Company intend to materially;l increase their charges for the use of the extended' lay-by which may nullify the advantages expected.; It is to be hoped that the rumour is incorrect. Given a fair chance, Runcorn with her natural advantages, may prove the best distributing centre. on the canal. Apart from the exceedingly goon connection with the Bridgewater and other canalgt there is the greater advantage of large ships being. able to reach Runcorn without lowering or cutting the masts in order to get under the bridges, and WI Ship Canal Company may yet find it more profitable to induce ships to traverse the canal as far as Riiir corn than not to receive thein at all.</p>
        <p>A movement, which if carried out, promises to be of great advantage to Runcorn and district, is theD initiated by Mr. Branch. For years there has beee, a basin at Westoti Point, which, Mr. Branch eon' tends, could very easily and without great expeneef be converted into a graving dock for the repair of vessels. To some extent this would be a recompeneei to the district for the loss of the greater portion o that trade in consequence of the construction of the Ship Canal. Many vessels plying the watercvai often require repairs, and it would be a great sal: ing to the owners if a repairing yard were provide at Weston Point, and thus prevent the necessity 01' sending their vessels .0 Birkenhead and other more or less distant places. The matter is receiving 030 attention of the Weaver Trustees who are the owners of the dock in question, and it is to be that they will see their way to entertain the p) posal.</p>
        <p>uineet 1110 t ;bat+ Leal' 7clo g1:0</p>
        <p>THE 800211 IN THE CYCLE TRAIA</p>
        <p>An unprecedented state of things is recorded in con"- tion witu the Birminbam tinplate trade. Comicial Stevens, the secretary of the Trade Society, states tl not a single member of the association is now unt ployed. The demand for men cannot be met. Cy' gear-case making has given the trade a great fillip; deed, not for thirty years has it been in so prosperauts state.</p>
