        <p>MAKE LIFE ENJOYABLE. Loss of health means loss of all which makes life en- joyable ; for, in addition to the bodily pain awl suffering which it causes, it entails a vast amount of mental pain and anxiety, especially in the homes of the working classes. It brings in its train a host of discomforts which retard the recovery of the sufferer, and hinder, instead of assisting, Nature to perform her functions. When a working man suffers from any ailment which prevents his following his daily avocation, he is natur- ally filled with anxiety -regarding the maintenance of those depending on him for their daily sustenance. His very love for his family retards and hinders his speedy recovery, and his spirits become deprested at the antici- pation of the gloomy prospects before them in the event of a long continuance of his illness. It is well known that depression of spirits often proves a serious bar to a speedy recovery. These results arise in addition to the various incon- veniences and discomforts which the sufferer and his family experience as direct results of his illness, such as additional expenses for luxuries for the sick, with in- creased difficulty to obtain those luxuries, or even the necessities of life. Most of the various forms of disease which trouble the labouring classes of the country, and which are often attributable, more or less, to some unhealthly condition of their daily work, may be classed under a tew leading heads. To avoid till discomforts arising from various forms of disease,every working man should seek the aid of GYFILYM EVAN'S QUININE BITTERS. THE VEGETABLE TONIC, Which has gained a world-wide renown as the most wonderful medicinal discovery of the age ; and as un- failing remedy it is prescribed by many leading physi- cians in various ailments. For Bronchitis, Asthma, Coughs, and Colds. Shortness of Breath, Neuralgia, General Weakness, Low Spirits, Nervousness, Indigestion, lufluenzi. • GWILYM EVANS' QUININE BITTERS Sold In Bottles.at 2.3. 9d. and 4s. 6d. each. See the name Gwilym Evans on Label, Stamp and Bottle. Sole Proprietors: Quinine Bitters' Manufacturing Co., Ltd., Ltanelly, South Wales.</p>
        <p>PENKETH. GREAT SANKEY SCHOOL BOARD MEETINO.—The monthly meeting of the Great Sankey School Board was held on Tuesday evening in the Board Schools, Mr. W. P. Skel- ton (chairman) preetiding. There were also present the Rev. Sandys Jackson, Mr. H. Bate, Mr. J. Wright, Mr. W. D. Jeans, jun. (clerk), and Mr. George Clarke (head master).—Mr. Clarke's monthly report stated that the sum received from the scholars durinic the past month amounted to £2 191.8 d. There were 22 children absent from sickness. The attendance had been—February Ist, 289; February 811, 300; February 15!,h, 301 ; and February 22nd, 301.—The Clerk reported a balance in hand of £B3 Bs. 53.—The Clerk also reported that the audit took place on the 14th February, and the books were found to be correct and were passed by the audi- tor. The cost to the rates was 411., and not 6d. Re- ceipts were only drawn to £l6O, and that was the amount the auditor allowed the cAlculation on ; so that it brought the rate down to 411. for the year.—The Clerk read the applications for the post of ex-pupil teacher (article 50) which had been rendered vacant by the resignation of Miss Seed. The applicants from which testimonials were read were :-1, Miss R..Farrington,sl, Padgate-lane, Warrington ; 1.. Miss Louise Booth, Red- lane Bridge, Hill Ciiffe ; 3, Miss Marion E. Foster, Pen keth ; 4, Miss Amy Boardman, Albion-villa, Penketh. The choice of the Board ultimately fell upon Miss Far- rington. Frederick Burden was remanded at Sputhampton on Monday on a chary of murdering a married woman named Faithful, with whom he lived.</p>
