        <p>LIVING PHOTOGRAPHY. WIDOW WELCH'S PILLS FOR FEMALE COMPLAINTS AIRS. SKITHERS, Grand-daughter to the Widow Photography, and, indeed, all pictorial art, has hither- A-V-1 Welch, continues to prepare this celebrated Medicine to fallen short of reproducing a moving living picture. trom the only original and genuine Family .Recipe. We have fixed portraits and scenes, but life and move- f just celebrity for all Female Complaints has haps Purchasers me,nt are entirely absent from the pictures. What would sohrouitlda therefore most ex te antstievned atroi d the increasing foi iendg CAUTION.—To 11.3 es be thought of a reproduction of a busy street scene with careful Vile what they purchse is prepared by Mrs. Smithers, cabs and omnibuses,and crowds of people, all in motion, who is the only person in possession of the ORIGINAL or say of a great cricket match at the Oval, with Dr. RECIPE. The genuine are wrapped iu Blue Paper, and signed Grace shown actually making one of his famous leg on the Outside label by Mrs. Smithers, and the names of the bits? The thing seems well nigh impossible, but if we Sole Wholesale Agents, "w.EDIcAR),Scse.: SON, 157, Queen In boxes at are 9dn-. are to credit an exhibition of the" Cinematographe," in- vented by Messrs. A. and L. Lumiere, given recently at Victoria each,Street,"formerlyof 67. by most Chemists. the Marlborough Ball, Regent-st., London, photographs can be reproduced so as to form a moving animated life- size picture. Such photographs were shewn on a large white screen, by means of the electric light. There was a railway station with a train dashing in and passengers getting into the carriages and alighting when it had stopped, and the outside of a big factory with departing operatives issuing forth till the last worker was gone. A reside photograph showed bathers jumping headlong into the sea from a spring board, and another depicted a gardener with a hose watering the flower beds while someone played tricks upon him. A domestic picture was shown of father and mother, with their infant pro- digy, at a tea table, and the parents' delight at the child feeding was a true bit of nature. Yet another photo- graph exhibited three gentlemen playing at cards outside an mn,. and a waiter approaches with a bottle of wine, which is poured out and drunk. The whole action of card-playing, with the laughing of the group, and even the smoke ascending from their cigars, was vividly dis- played. Now, all these were photographed scenes from real life, as well as one showing a crowded thoroughfare. H. ow, then, were they reproduced so that the spectators in front of the screen saw thereon the scenes enacted again before their eyes ? The explanation is simply that the photographs were taken on a continuous band at the rata of 900 a minute, being instantaneous photography so rapidly continued—fifteen to the second—that every aspect of a scene is caught. These photographs being projected on the screen by t'ze electric light at exactly the same rate of movement a changing picture is pro- duced faithful to the original. By this means people in a room may have reproduced in lifelike st 3 le many scenes, even, say, the finish to the Derby at Epsom.</p>
